3 STAE VA
3 CTRY United States
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Thomas Gibson was born abt 1667 in Virginia.. He is seen as the son of https://www.geni.com/people/Nicholas-Gibson/6000000001784733095?through=2363156 (1649-1734) and Deanna Martin (abt 1651- aft 1691) without supp
2 CONC orting evidence.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Gibson's wife may have been Mary.
2 CONT
2 CONT Some of his children were:
2 CONT
2 CONT # Frances Gibson (b. abt 1714, married Humphrey);
2 CONT # Mary Gibson (b abt 1716, married a Brook);
2 CONT # Thomas Gibson (b. abt 1718);
2 CONT # John Gibson (b. abt 1720); ;
2 CONT # Valentine Gibson (b. 1723):
2 CONT # Eunice Gibson (b. abt 1729, married Edward Nicks or Nix)
2 CONT
2 CONT  ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas married Mary Allen (Disputed) (1642-1734) in 1685 in the St. Martins Parish in Hanover County, VA. Mary Allen was the daughter of Richard (Allin) Allen (1611-1690) and Mary Hobbs Allen (1615-1664) (is this proven?). Doing m
2 CONC ore research about St. Martins Parish, we found out that the name is now "The Fork Church", and it is located at Doswell, Virginia in Hanover County, VA. It used to be a part of New Kent County, VA but later this county divided an
2 CONC d became Hanover County. "Celebrating God for more than 275 YEARS Welcome to historic Fork Church, an Episcopal Church in the Diocese of Virginia". - from Facebook. "Fork Church is situated in the midst of old trees at the fork of
2 CONC  two rivers in western Hanover County.
2 CONT
2 CONT Since 1735 this landmark Colonial building has sheltered a living Episcopal church. It is both a symbol of the faith which guided its founders and a witness to the ongoing power of the Gospel of Jesus Christ." Still quoting from F
2 CONC acebook, "November 2010 marks the 275th anniversary of The Fork Church". 275 Historical Fun Facts: Situated in Hanover county near the confluence of the North & South Anna Rivers, Fork Episcopal Church was the Parish Church of St.
2 CONC  Martin's parish. Patrick Henry worshipped here frequently when he lived at nearby Scotchtown." "Did you know that Katherine Hepburn's grandfather served as rector for The Fork Episcopal Church? Hepburn's father, Thomas, was born 
2 CONC in Hanover County in 1879 and spent his boyhood here, graduating from Randolph-Macon College in 1901". Mary Allen's first husband was Robert Long (1642-1677).
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas and Mary Allen Gibson had the following 6 known children: Frances, Eunice, Mary, John, Thomas and Valentine.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas died on 7 November 1734 in St Martins Parish, Hanover County, Virginia, United States. He was about 67 years of age. The brick church was completed in 1735.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ancestry.com names 6 Famous People related to Thomas Gibson (BETA). Here are four of these: Elvis Presley(1935-1977), American Entertainer, 2nd Cousin 8 times removed; Bill Clinton(1946-), 42nd President of the United States of Am
2 CONC erica, 7th Cousin; Dwight D Eisenhower(1890-1969), 34th President of the United States, 8th Cousin 3 times removed; 
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Disputed Origins 
2 CONT
2 CONT From http://joannepezzullo.blogspot.com/search?q=valentine
2 CONT
2 CONT >Take Thomas Gibson who died in 1734 in Hanover County, Virginia for instance. Researchers were positive he was the ancestor of Thomas Gibson who is found in Orange County, North Carolina and is ancestor of at least some of the Me
2 CONC lungeon Gibsons. DNA of four of Valentine Gibson's descendants, the son of Thomas died 1734 Hanover DOES NOT MATCH the Melungeon Gibsons proving they are not related. Further testing of autosomal also shows no 'cousin finder' matc
2 CONC hes between these two families. Still, there are those that insisted despite the Y DNA not matching they had to be related, perhaps an NPE [Non Parental Event] but one would think if they didn't have the same father but had the sa
2 CONC me mother, surely the 'cousin finder' would show all the descendants of Valentine matching the descendants of Thomas Gibson the Melungeon. But it does not, there are no matches. That doesn't seem to change anything, those who have
2 CONC  sworn by the DNA matching for years, now discard it because it is proving something that they do not want to be proved, for whatever reasons. And now this is spilling over into the DNA projects. After 27 years of researching the 
2 CONC family of Valentine and his father Thomas I have not found one record that shows who the mother of Valentine was. Yet thousands of trees report it was Mary Allen. '''Not one piece of evidence can be found as to the parents of Thom
2 CONC as Gibson who died in 1734 was, yet thousands of trees lists them as Nicholas and Deanna Martin Gibson. Where this comes from I have no clue.''' Most of these trees go on to claim Nicholas' parents as Thomas Gibson died 1652 in Yo
2 CONC rk with a wife Elizabeth BOUTH or SOUTH. Thomas who died in 1652 did indeed have a son Nicholas and a wife Elizabeth but I have written to half of the online trees asking for a source. Most don't answer and the ones that do simply
2 CONC  reply they have no idea, they just copied it from someone else. Now Elizabeth BOUTH, daughter of Robeth Bouth received gifts from Thomas Gibson in York County "for reasons known only to him" and this is the basis for Elizabeth BO
2 CONC UTH being the wife of Thomas Gibson. However the daughter Elizabeth of Robert Bouth married to Patrick Napier, of that there is no doubt and after this was pointed how number times in the past years Elizabeth BOUTH then became Eli
2 CONC zabeth SOUTH, again with not one bit of evidence.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Will==  
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.choicemn.com/trees/gibson-tullis/1062.html 
2 CONT
2 CONT 7 November 1734
2 CONT
2 CONT *I Thomas Gibson Senior of St. Martins Par., Hanover Co. Sick and weak but of sound mind and memory do make this my Last will and Testament. I recommend my soul to the hand of Almighty God and my body to the earth. 
2 CONT
2 CONT *After all my debts and funeral charges are paid I give to my loving son Thomas Gibson 50 a. of land; 
2 CONT *to my well beloved son John Gibson 50 a. of land; 
2 CONT *to be well beloved daughter Nice? Nicks wife of Edward Nicks, Frances Humdrey and Mary Brook one Shilling each;
2 CONT * to my well beloved son Edward Nicks awhom I do hereby constitute sole Exor. of this my will and Testament my house likewise my stock of hogs and cattle together with all my Estate goods and Chattels moveable and unmovable to ena
2 CONC ble him to bring up my Children 
2 CONT *John Gibson, Valentine Gibson also William Robertson. 
2 CONT *I do hereby utterly revoke and make void all former wills made by me. 
2 CONT *Thomas (+) Gibson 
2 CONT *Wit: Isaac (x) Johnson, James (x) Phillips, G. Gillingham, School Mstr. 
2 CONT *7 Nov. 1734 proved by oaths of the witnesses. 
2 CONT *REF: Hanover Co., VA Court Records, 1733-1735 
2 CONT
2 CONT [See 170 Lunenburg Tax List]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Comments 
2 CONT
2 CONT (Notes by Dorothy Tuttle who did research in New Kent, VA & York Co., VA.)
2 CONT
2 CONT >Thomas Gibson made his will of 7 Nov 1734 and he died in late Nov or early Dec because the inventory of his estate was made 5 Dec 1734. He did not have daughter named Mary & Mary C.  He had a daughter Mary who md Richard Brooks a
2 CONC nd they are named in his will. '''There are so many mistakes on this line because of lack of proof.'''  I did a lot of research in Hanover Co,  New Kent Co and York Co. & did not find a record where Thomas md Mary Frances Allen or
2 CONC  that Thomas was a son of Nicholas Gibson.  I did not find a birth date for Thomas Gibson & I do not believe he was born in Isle of Wight.  Isle of Wight was created in 1634 .  New Kent was created in 1654 from York Co. Va and par
2 CONC t of James City. York Co. was created in 1634 and was formerly Charles River Co.    I have not done any research in Isle of Wight but I have in York Co. where there was a Thomas Gibson there about the same time as Thomas Gibson wh
2 CONC o died in 1734.  I believe Thamas was born in York Co., VA and not Isle of Wight
2 CONT
2 CONT  ----
2 CONT
2 CONT About St. Martin's Parish:
2 CONT
2 CONT THE FORK CHURCH, HANOVER COUNTY. VlRGlNlA. BY BOSEWELL PAGE.
2 CONT
2 CONT THERE Is a record in the county of Louisa, Virginia, according to a letter in my possession, a copy of a petition sent by certain taxpayers of that newly-formed county to the house of Burgesses in 1740, praying to have refunded to
2 CONC  them a certain tobacco tax that had been levied on them to build a large, new and convenient church in St. Martin's Parish, Hanover county.
2 CONT
2 CONT That this church was The Fork church, or "The Old Fork Church," as it is generally known, is asserted by two eminent Virginians who formerly lived in the respective counties of Louisa and Hanover.
2 CONT
2 CONT This petition bears date two years before the cutting off of Louisa from Hanover, and of Fredericksville Parish from St. Martin's Parish, which appear from Henning's Statutes (Vol. V., pp. 21 and 208) to have been so separated in 
2 CONC the year 1742.
2 CONT
2 CONT It may add to the value of this paper to state that the boundary between the two parishes was a line drawn from the mouth of Gladys creek, on the south side of the North Anna river, a course south 20 degrees west, till it intersec
2 CONC ts the Goochland line. And when Fredericksville Parish was divided, that part which adjoined St. Martin's was called Trinity (Hen. Sts., Vol. VII., p. 428).
2 CONT
2 CONT St. Paul's Parish in Hanover was divided in 1726, six years after the county of Hanover was cut oft from New Kent, and to the parish was given the name St. Martin's, after St. Martin-in-the-Fields, London. For it was in that very 
2 CONC year that that London church was built by Glbbs (See Brit. Enc., Title "London").
2 CONT
2 CONT The church was no doubt called St. Martin's, but was soon known as "The Fork Church," from its position with reference to the two forks of the Pamunkey, as the North Anna and South Anna were called in many of the legal documents o
2 CONC f that time. In the last twenty-five years the name has been applied to the neat little church at Doswell, five miles away from the mother church. Two other churches in the western end of the parish, Allen's Creek and Hollowing Cr
2 CONC eek, in the memory of the oldest inhabitant of the parish, have passed away, and no trace of their existence survives. In the place of these, two other churches have been built in the parish, but The Old Fork church survives as a 
2 CONC noble monument to the Colonial Churchmen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Built of the glazed end brick, so familiar in Colonial buildings, its birthday is fixed at 1735, two years after that of the courthouse some twelve miles to the east, which sheltered the throng assembled there in 1763 to hear Patr
2 CONC ick Henry in the "Parsons' Cause." It is a matter of great satisfaction to all lovers of the history of the community to know that both of these noble buildings are in an excellent state of preservation. Tradition says that these 
2 CONC buildings were built of brick brought from England, as it says of many other of our Colonial buildings; but, thankless as the task is to destroy such a tradition, I am compelled to state that neither these, nor the brick for any o
2 CONC ther of our churches, were brought from England. 1 am confirmed in this statement by the answer of the learned Dr. Philip Slaughter, to whom some years ago I propounded the question.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Fork church Is a solid structure, whose length, 75 feet, is about three times its breadth, with a door at the southern end, and another on the side, near the northeastern corner. Over each door there is a portico on brick colu
2 CONC mns, whose proportion and entasis are the admiration of lovers of architecture.
2 CONT
2 CONT Although the records of Hanover county and of St. Martin's Parish have been lost or destroyed, the history of the old church is safe in the tradition and life of the people.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1886 the Rev. Dr. Philip Slaughter published in the Southern Churchman an account of his recovery of what he called "The Rectory Book" of St. Paul's Parish, without which, he says, even Bishop Meade had been unable to give a fu
2 CONC ll history of that parish. Among the names he mentioned as figuring in the vestries in St. Paul's Parish, which as we have seen embraced St. Martin's, Trinity and Fredericksville Parishes until 1726, were the Crawfords, Merewether
2 CONC s, Winstons, Henrys, Grymeses, Bickertons, Jones, Andersons, Rylands, Garlands, Merediths, Pages, Pendletons, Timberlakes, Lipscombs, Goodalls, Abbotts, Macons, Skeltons, Pierces, Taylors, Darracotts, Chapmans, Streets, Crosses an
2 CONC d Pollards.
2 CONT
2 CONT An entry of some interest is the following: "September, 1708, Mr. Thomas Sharpe having offered to be our minister, it is agreed that he preach in both churches till the last day of December come twelve months, and if at the end of
2 CONC  that time he likes us and we like him, to continue. Otherwise each party to provide for themselves." It is a satisfaction to know that preacher and people liked each other, for he continued to "be hired" from year to year until 1
2 CONC 720, when St. Martin's was cut off as we have seen.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Fork church la rich in historic associations. Hither came Patrick Henry in his early infancy, and in later life while living at "Scotch Town," the interesting old hipped roofed structure some five mills away, through whose wid
2 CONC e hall, in spite of the stone steps, Tarleton and his raiders rode. For Patrick Henry, with all of his zeal and enthusiasm for the liberty of his country, and with all of his feeling in behalf of the people which burst forth in th
2 CONC eir defense against the Parsons when they demanded more than was thought their due, always revered the Episcopal Church in which he was baptized and in which his father, John Henry, had been vestryman, and his uncle, the Rev. Patr
2 CONC ick Henry, for whom he was named, was a parish minister for forty years. (Records of St. Paul's Parish ante.) To the Fork church from "Scotch Town" came Henry's cousin, Dorothea, better known as Dolly, little dreaming, perhaps, as
2 CONC  she sat in the high-backed pew over which she could hardly see when standing on tiptoe on a cushion, that she was one day to be the wife of James Madison, President of the United States.
2 CONT
2 CONT There preached in this parish, and at a church called The Fork, near "Ground Squirrel Bridge," Samuel Davies, the great Presbyterian preacher and president of Princeton College, as well as founder of the Hanover Presbytery, that v
2 CONC irile body, whose staunch stand against the Establishment has been well described by Cooke, the Virginia historian.
2 CONT
2 CONT St. Martin's Parish still owns the beautiful communion service, the paten and chalice inscribed with the following legend: "For the use of the churches in St. Martin's Parish, in Hanover and Louisa counties, Virginia, 175SK"
2 CONT
2 CONT The history of this service is lost. There are two traditions about it. One that it was presented by St. Martin's church, London, and the other that it was presented by William Nelson, president of the Council, and brought over by
2 CONC  his son Thomas (afterwards Governor Nelson) upon his return from England that year, upon the completion of his education.
2 CONT
2 CONT The following incidents are also related of this old service, in each of which Mrs. Berkeley, of "Airwell," is the heroine: 1st, that she defied General Tarleton and his raiders when they demanded the service; and 2d, that she def
2 CONC ied the overseers of the poor who demanded
2 CONT
2 CONT it after the glebe lands were taken from the church. Bishop Meade is authority for the last statement. (Vol. II., Old Churches, p. 26.) It is of interest to note that this same service is now kept at the same place by the descenda
2 CONC nts of that redoubtable Churchwoman.
2 CONT
2 CONT Near the Fork church were grants of land made by the crown to Thomas Nelson, grandfather of General Nelson, upon a part of which his descendants now reside. The Marquis de Chastellux, who served in America as Major-General under R
2 CONC ochambeau, describes his visit to the "Offley," the home of General Nelson, a few miles above the Fork church. (Howe's Miscellanies, p. 295.) It was at Mont Air, the home of his son Francis, who so long represented the parish in t
2 CONC he councils of the Church, that General Nelson died; and it was within a few miles of the old church at "Springfield" that his widow lived, having survived him nearly forty years. Beneath the shadow of the old church her remains l
2 CONC ie buried along with those of a great number of her descendants. It may be safely asserted that from this sainted lady the Church has had as many adherents both clerical and lay, as have ever sprung from one stock in the same leng
2 CONC th of time. With the aid of one of her granddaughters, I have counted up twenty-four clergymen of the Episcopal Church among her descendants. When during the war the vestrymen were unable to raise the minister's salary, a daughter
2 CONC  of hers sent them word that she would guarantee it personally.
2 CONT
2 CONT Among those ministers furnished by this parish, Bishop Meade mentions the Rev. W. N. Pendleton, Washington Nelson, Robert Nelson and Farley Berkeley. To these may be added the names of the Rev. G. W. Nelson, late rector at Warrent
2 CONC on, and the Rev. Frank Page, of Brooklyn.
2 CONT
2 CONT It was to this parish, and to the home of Dr. Carter Berkeley that Bishop Meade came to choose his second wife, Thomasia Nelson, stepdaughter of Dr. Berkeley. She, too, is buried at the Old Fork church.
2 CONT
2 CONT To the neighborhood of this old church came Lewis Minor Coleman, with his Hanover Academy and his influence for good hardly second to that of Dr. Arnold, of Rugby, which school and influence were well maintained by his successor, 
2 CONC Colonel Hilary P. Jones, who had, however, to yield to the inevitable, and this great school is now but a memory.
2 CONT
2 CONT The picture of this old church will recall many recollections to the former students at Hanover Academy, many of whose names m%T now be found on the backs or seats of the solid heart-pine pews.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the early part of the last century that demon of architecture, which Mr. Jefferson said had spread its maledictions over the land, broke loose, and the high-backed pews were taken out, and the pulpit which had been at the side 
2 CONC of the church, was put at the end.
2 CONT
2 CONT An old Bible in the parish, that of the Fontaines, shows that in 1787 the Rev. Robert Barrett was in charge. It was he of whom Bishop Meacle (Vol. II., p. 43) says he received 320 pounds of tobacco for each sermon preached in Loui
2 CONC sa county, where he preached twenty-four times a year during days of labor.
2 CONT
2 CONT The list of clergy who have ministered in the parish since Mr. Barrett includes the Rev. Messrs. Peter Nelson, who became a Baptist; Boggs, Phillips, Wydown, Cooke, Bowers, Stringfellow, Isaac Gibson, Wm. A. Alrich (whose first wi
2 CONC fe, a lovely woman, the sister of James M. Love, Esq., of Fairfax county, lies buried at the Fork church), R. Douglas Roller, Edward S. Gregory, R. Roane Claiborne, Curtis Grubb, Anselem Buchanan, S. S. Hepburn and Alexander Gait.
2 CONC  To all these godly men the parish and this church are greatly indebted. Perhaps to Mrs. Hepburn more than any other person is due the present excellent condition of the Fork church, and the grounds surrounding it.
2 CONT
2 CONT The present wardens of the church are Nathaniel Burwell Cooke and Joseph F. Grubb.
2 CONT
2 CONT Within the last few years two funds of $3,000 and $200, respectively, have been established for the benefit of the church, the larger fund subject only to the maintenance of the Nelson-Page burying ground.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bishop Meade gives the list of the true friends of religion and of the Episcopal Church in the parish as Fontaines, Nelsons, Morrises, Wickhams, Taylors, Winstons, Pollards, Robinsons, Pages, Prices, Shepherds, having already ment
2 CONC ioned the Berkeley family, and made note of Dr. Carter Berkeley, "whose name may be so often seen on the Convention journals of the last and present century."
2 CONT
2 CONT Among the names on the vestry since Bishop Meade's time, in addition to those mentioned by him, many of whom are related to those so mentioned, are Minor, Noland, Fleming, Hunter, Jones, Cooke, Doswell, Terrell, Thompson, Grubb an
2 CONC d Duke. There are many other families about the church whose love and affection for it are exhibited in the fact that though members of other churches, their attendance Is regular, their aid efficient and their pride in the old ch
2 CONC urch as marked as if they were members of the Episcopal Church. Thither they bring their dead to be burled, and often their young people to enter this old church of their forefathers.
2 CONT
2 CONT The only monument inside the church is a beautiful tablet to three of its faithful sons:
2 CONT
2 CONT "The Rev. Robert Nelson, Missionary to China during thirty years— of whom it is alleged, 'He followed the Holy doctrine which he taught, comforting many.' "
2 CONT
2 CONT "William Nelson, late Colonel of Artillery C. S. A., who in this parish served God and helped his fellowmen for over sixty years."
2 CONT
2 CONT "John Page, late Major C. S. A. who in this parish through a long and honorable life did his duty to God and his neighbor."
2 CONT
2 CONT On the outside of the church lie buried many of those already mentioned and not mentioned. Among the latter may be named Captain and Mrs. Charles William Dabney, whose names are honorably associated with the history of the county 
2 CONC and parish, and over whose remains a handsome monument has been erected by their children.
2 CONT
2 CONT A strong iron fence surrounds the church grounds, and this noble old church, with its massive walls and slate roof, bids fair to stand for generations as a lasting monument to the zeal and good taste of its builders. That its hist
2 CONC ory should be lost is a great misfortune. It is, indeed, one of the pathetic things about our Church's past, no less than about many of the cherished possessions of our State, that any adequate history thereof is entirely lacking.
2 CONC  Nineveh and Karnac are hardly less known.
2 CONT
