1 NAME Peter /Venables/ MP, of Kinderton
2 GIVN Peter
2 SURN Venables
2 NSFX MP, of Kinderton
2 _MARNM Venables
2 NICK Peter Venables
2 NICK MP
2 NICK of Kinderton
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 22 APR 1604
2 ADDR
3 CITY Kinderton
3 STAE England
3 CTRY United Kingdom
1 DEAT
2 DATE 13 FEB 1669
2 ADDR
3 CITY Kinderton
3 STAE England
3 CTRY United Kingdom
1 BURI
2 DATE ABT 13 NOV 1669
2 ADDR
3 CITY Kinderton
3 STAE England
3 CTRY United Kingdom
1 OCCU Baron Of Kenderton
1 TITL Baron
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Family and Education b. 22 Apr. 1604, 3rd but o. surv. s. of Thomas Venables of Kinderton, being 1st s. by 2nd w. Anne, da. of Sir cotton Cotton Gargrave of Nostell, Yorks. educ. L. Inn 1620. m. (1) c.1622, Mary, d
2 CONC a. of Sir Richard Wilbraham, 1st Bt., of Woodhey, Cheshire, 1s. d.v.p.; (2) settlement 11 Sept. 1628, Frances, da. of Sir Hugh Cholmondeley of Cholmondeley, Cheshire, 4s. (2 d.v.p.) 2da. suc. fa. 1605.1
2 CONT
2 CONT Offices Held
2 CONT
2 CONT Sheriff, Cheshire 1633-4, j.p. by 1634-46, July 1660-d.; commr. for disarming recusants 1641, array 1642, assessment Aug. 1660-1, 1663-d., corporations 1662-3, oyer and terminer, Chester 1663.2
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography Venables rejoiced in the style ‘baron of Kinderton’, an honour said to have been created by the 1st Earl of Chester soon after the Norman Conquest. The family’s parliamentary record was less impressive, though an ancesto
2 CONC r sat for the county in two Tudor Parliaments. Venables inherited eight manors in Cheshire, with salt works in Nantwich and Middlewich, and property in Staffordshire, yielding some £3,450 p.a. In the Long Parliament he voted again
2 CONC st the attainder of Strafford. His neighbour Sir William Brereton described him as ‘very virulent in his courses against the Parliament ... an enemy to reformation and a tyrant among his tenants’. He joined the King at Oxford duri
2 CONC ng the Civil War, though, unlike his son, he was not in arms. They jointly compounded for their delinquency in 1647 for £6,150. He was included by Roger Whitley among the Cheshire Royalists in 1658, and again returned for the coun
2 CONC ty in 1661. He was not an active Member of the Cavalier Parliament, being named to only 11 committees, of which the two most important were those for the uniformity and corporations bills. He died on 13 Feb. 1669, the last of his 
2 CONC family to sit in Parliament.3
2 CONT
2 CONT Ref Volumes: 1660-1690 Author: Gillian Hampson Notes 1. Ormerod, Cheshire, iii. 199-200. 2. Chester mss. assembly bk. 2, 137v. 3. Keeler, Long Parl. 372; SP23/196/477-91.
2 CONT
2 CONT married firstly Mary Wilbraham, secondly Frances Cholmondeley
2 CONT
2 CONT High Sheriff of Cheshire
2 CONT
2 CONT Date of death has also been (erroneously?) reported to be 1679.
2 CONT
