1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richard_N._Venable
2 CONT Richard Nathaniel Venable (1756–1838) was a nineteenth-century American politician from Virginia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early life
2 CONT Venable was born at Slate Hill Plantation in Prince Edward County.
2 CONT
2 CONT In his twenties, he served in the Revolutionary War in the United States Army, and rose to the rank of Lieutenant.
2 CONT
2 CONT Career
2 CONT In 1782, Venable earned a Master of Arts degree at Princeton College, and a Bachelor of Laws from the College of William and Mary in 1785.
2 CONT
2 CONT As an adult, Venable continued to live in Prince Edward County, Virginia. He was elected as a member of the Virginia state Senate as a Whig.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the originator and first president of the Upper Appomattox Canal Company. It was incorporated in 1795 completed the Upper Appomattox Canal by 1816 for polled bateaux freighters. It extended from the head of the falls of the
2 CONC  Appomattox River at Farmville, a distance of 100 miles to a basin in Petersburg, Virginia. The canal was rebuilt in the 1830s and continued in use until the 1890s.
2 CONT
2 CONT Venable was elected as a delegate to the Virginia Constitutional Convention of 1829-1830. There he was elected by the Convention to serve on the Committee on the Executive Department. He was one of four delegates elected from the 
2 CONC senatorial district made up his home district of Charlotte, Halifax and Prince Edward Counties, serving alongside [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Randolph_of_Roanoke John Randolph of Roanoke.] [https://www.geni.com/people/John
2 CONC -Randolph-of-Roanoke-U-S-Senator/6000000003232933040 (Geni profile)]
2 CONT Venable died in 1838 at Prince Edward County, Virginia.
2 CONT ----
2 CONT per https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Venable-31
2 CONT Richard Nathaniel Venable
2 CONT Born 16 Jan 1763 in Slate Hill, Prince Edward County, Virginia
2 CONT Son of Nathaniel E Venable I and Elizabeth Michaux (Woodson) Venable
2 CONT Brother of Samuel Woodson Venable Sr., Abraham Bedford Venable, Elizabeth Ann (Venable) Watkins, Martha Ann Venable Venable, Ann Nancy (Venable) Daniel, Mary Venable, Nathaniel Venable II, William Lewis Venable, Thomas Venable and
2 CONC  Elizabeth Woodson (Venable) Wilson
2 CONT Husband of Mary (Morton) Venable — married 5 May 1797 in Charlotte County, VA
2 CONT
2 CONT Father of Frances Anderson (Venable) Leach
2 CONT Died 17 Jan 1838 at age 75 in Prince Edward County, Virginia
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT Richard Nathaniel Venable and his wife, Mary Morton, had several children. Their son Richard (1806-1844) married Magdelena McCampbell and the two named their son Richard Morton Venable (1839-1910). Richard Morton Venable graduated
2 CONC  from Hampden-Sydney College in 1857. He became a Major in the Confederate Army and a trustee of the college, and the dormitory called Venable Hall is named in his honor.
2 CONT
2 CONT His education was received from Princeton and William & Mary Law School. He was interested in agricultural improvements, canals, railroads and education. His marriage to Mary Morton on 3/5/1797 led to the bearing of numerous child
2 CONC ren to include; Betsey, Martha Watkins, Richard, William Henry, Frances Anderson, Elizabeth Woodson & Mary. During his life, he served as a Lieutenant in the Revolutionary War, and as a State Senator and member of the Convention o
2 CONC f 1829. Supposedly his death in 1838 was caused by heart failure as he was found face down in only 2 inches of water near "Slate Hill".
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT # William Henry Venable1798 VA Margaret Reid
2 CONT # Frances Anderson Venable 2 JUN 1799 6 JAN 1861 VA James Henry Cosden Leach
2 CONT # Mary Morton Venable abt 1812 VA James D Ligon
2 CONT # Martha Watkins Venable 1813 VA, Prince Edward Co Richard Jennings Gaines
2 CONT # Richard Venable
2 CONT # Elizabeth Woodson Venable
2 CONT # Betsy Venable
2 CONT
