2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT m Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henry_Doss
2 CONT Henry Doss was born about 1790 in Henry County, Virginia. According to his 1858 marriage license, his parents were John and Sallie Doss. Henry is the progenitor of the main line of Doss surnames that originated from the Washington
2 CONC  County, Virginia area. Those descendants would end up spreading into West Virginia, Tennessee, and North Carolina as well.
2 CONT The earliest record of Henry Doss is the 1850 Census for Washington County, and the second is his tax payment in 1852. There are no records for Henry's parents John and Sallie at the Henry County Courthouse where his marriage and 
2 CONC pension records state he was born. It is likely that these particular Dosses were related to the group that settled in Pittsylvania County in the late 1700's, though this is purely speculation. The largest repository of Henry Doss
2 CONC  information comes from his War of 1812 service record located at the National Archives. It can be presumed from historical data, that Henry never had to actually serve in combat, because combat veterans were awarded Bounty Land W
2 CONC arrants (BLW). The Henry County militia is not on the eligibility list for BLW. Henry came to Washington County at some point after he served a period of five months in the Henry County militia during the War of 1812. Henry marrie
2 CONC d Nancy Thomas daughter of Lewis and Mary Thomas on September 2, 1858 when Henry was 68 years old, and after all of their children had been born. Court records in Washington County show that several indictments were handed down to
2 CONC  both Henry and Nancy claiming lewd behavior and fornication. This suggests that someone had a problem with their unmarried status.
2 CONT According to Census data, Henry Doss was a miller. It is reasonable that he must have worked under the wealthy Andrew Patterson, because Andrew Patterson was his neighbor in 1850 and he owned a Mill. Andrew Patterson was the capta
2 CONC in of the Washington County 70th regiment during the War of 1812, and apparently was a minister at some point (according to War of 1812 Pension affidavits), and performed the marriage for Henry Doss and Nancy Thomas. The marriage 
2 CONC of Henry and Nancy came late in their lives, after all of their children had been born. There are two indictments in April of 1858; one of lewd and lascivious behavior for Henry Doss and Nancy Thomas and another for fornication on
2 CONC  Nancy Thomas. We can deduce from these court orders, that someone decided to make an issue out of their living arrangements, which ultimately led to the September 2,1858 marriage of Henry and Nancy. This worked out historically f
2 CONC or Nancy, as she was able to inherit Henry's pension at his death. Two of Henry's sons (Sampson and Elias) served in the Civil War, and would suggest they were brought up in a pro-Southern rights family. Existing records show that
2 CONC  Henry never owned any slaves.
2 CONT Henry died in Washington County on May 18th 1872.
