1 NOTE {geni:about_me} A Patriot of the American Revolution for VIRGINIA with the rank of Private.  [http://services.dar.org/Public/DAR_Research/search_adb/?action=full&p_id=A004628  DAR Ancestor # A004628] Married 12/26/1772 in Amherst 
2 CONC county, VA . ELISHA4 BAILEY (JOHN3, SAMUEL2, WILLIAM1)4 was born November 20, 1763 in Albermarle County, Virginia, and died November 30, 1841 in Adair County, Kentucky. He married HANNAH GAY December 19, 1782 in Albermarle County,
2 CONC  Virginia, daughter of THOMAS GAY and MARGARET. She was born March 11, 1764 in Virginia, and died about 1845 in Adair County, Kentucky. Elisha was listed on the 1787 Albemarle County, Virginia Tax List, as being above 21 years old
2 CONC , then he moved to Lincoln County, Kentucky about 1788, where he was listed on the Tax List from 1789 to 1800. When Adair County was formed in 1802, Elisha was listed as paying taxes on property on Butler’s creek. The 1806 and 181
2 CONC 0 Tax List also has him on Butler’s Creek. Elisha acquired a Land Grant in 1801 in what is now Adair county, Kentucky. It was for 100 Acres and was described as being near Russell Creek, the county line, Pettits Fork, A. Scaggs pr
2 CONC operty, Big Creek and Butlers Fording. He also bought two lots in town, then sold them in 1809 (Deed Book B, page 210). He bought 280 acres near the clefts of Russell Creek in Adair County, Kentucky in 1822, then sold 100 acres of
2 CONC  this land in 1837. He served eight months in the Virginia Army during the Revolutionary War, serving under Layfayette and General Washington at the battle of York where General Cornwallis surrendered. He applied for a pension in 
2 CONC 1833, detailing his war record and turning in a Bible page to prove his pension claim. According to the Daughter’s of the American Revolution Patriot Grave Book, Elisha is buried in the Clear Springs Methodist Church Cemetery, but
2 CONC  there is no gravestone now visible with his name on it.  Elisha was listed on the 1832 Adair County Presidential Voter List for Andrew Jackson and in the 1810 to 1840 census records of Adair.
2 CONT
2 CONT WILL of ELISHA BAILEY
2 CONT
2 CONT Will Book E - page 330 Adair County, Kentucky
2 CONT
2 CONT In the name of God Amen, I Elisha Bailey of Adair County Kentucky do certify this my last will and testament revoking all others. First I do give and bequeath to my daughter Betsy and son Benjamin, jointly my farm or tract of land
2 CONC  I now live on containing one hundred and eighty acres with all appurtenances in consideration of them taking care and supporting me and my wife while we may live. My two slaves, Majors & Judea, I do manumit and give them their fr
2 CONC eedom at the death of my wife. It is my wish that the said two slaves may live on the place or make it their home while my daughter Betsy & son Benjamin and them agree and they behave prudently and if they cannot agree they, Major
2 CONC s & Judea, must shift for themselfs. Further, I desire at the death of my wife, all my property not herein named, shall be sold and the proceeds thereof to be equally divided among all my children. I wish Cornelius Gay to have a y
2 CONC oung horse "beast" reserved for him out of some of my young colts before any sale takes place and for the purposes heretofore stated, I constitute my daughter Betsy Blaine and son Benjamin Bailey my Executrix & Executor to this my
2 CONC  last will and testament. In testimony I set my hand this 26th of October 1841
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisha Bailey (seal) Attest Jackson Atkins G. C. Elliot proved 7th December 1841
