1 NAME Nicketti /Hughes/ Person of legend
2 GIVN Nicketti
2 NSFX Person of legend
2 _MARNM Hughes
2 NICK Ni-ka-tai
2 NICK Nicati
2 NICK She Who Sweeps the Dew from the Flowers
2 NICK Sarah Hooe
2 NICK Sarah Goodwin
1 SEX F
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 1625
2 PLAC Virginia
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE AFT 1720
2 PLAC Virginia
2 ADDR
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} According to a story first published in 1895 Trader Hughes was the first permanent White settler in Amherst County, Virginia. He and his Indian wife '''Nicketti''' established a trading post on the north side of th
2 CONC e James River, west of the Tobacco Row Mountains. His wife is said to have been a daughter or other relative of [https://www.geni.com/people/Opechancanough-paramount-chief-of-the-Powhatan/6000000005937340015 Opechancanough] and ni
2 CONC ece to [https://www.geni.com/people/Pocahontas/6000000005385138952 Pocahontas]. (Nancy KIser, Dec. 24, 2004). This story has been elaborated by subsequent generations of genealogists.
2 CONT
2 CONT A clue to Nicketti that predates the 1895 publication of the story is the name of [https://www.geni.com/people/Nicketti-Johnston/6000000010155352901 Nicketti Floyd] (1819-1908). Her father [https://www.geni.com/people/John-Floyd-2
2 CONC 5th-U-S-Governor-of-Virginia/6000000006788391332 John Floyd] was Governor of Virginia (1830-1834) and a supposed descendant of this family. It is possible, however, the name of the governor's daughter was the inspiration for the n
2 CONC ame of their Indian ancestor rather than vice-versa.
2 CONT
2 CONT There is also a ''Nicati Floyd'', age 71, enumerated on the 1850 census at Burke's Garden near Tazewell, Virginia. She was living in the household of the Governor's daughter, Lavalett (Floyd) Holmes. She is thought by many to have
2 CONC  been the Governor's aunt, daughter of William Floyd and Abadiah (Davis) Floyd.
2 CONT
2 CONT Some researchers believe she might have been the Indian girl purchased by [https://www.geni.com/people/Rice-Hooe-II/340568821330013984 Rice Hoe], of Charles City County, from [https://www.geni.com/people/Rice-Hughes/60000000631606
2 CONC 46921 Rice Hughes], of New Kent County as agent for Manwaring Hammond. She was the subject of a 1662 lawsuit in Charles City County.
2 CONT
2 CONT == Her Name ==
2 CONT
2 CONT ""Nicketti" is not an identifiable Indian name, and is probably a corruption of some other name. It could be derived from "Necotowance," the former name of a creek in King William County, taken in turn from the personal name of Op
2 CONC echancanough's male successor. Nothing is known about that man except that he signed the Treaty of 1646 on behalf of many of the Powhatan tribes. He disappeared from the English records after 1649." (jeaniesgenealogy.com, Nov. 16,
2 CONC  2011). Some sources say the original form of her name was ''Ni-ka-tai'' but no original sources are cited. 
2 CONT
2 CONT >"Miss Ann Mason Lee of Richmond, a granddaughter of Nicketti (Floyd) Johnston, says that her grandmother told her that she was the first Nicketti. The story she told was that just after she was born, and before she was christened
2 CONC , some Indians stopped at the home of her father, Dr. John Floyd, a grandson of William and Abadiah. He noticed that as they looked at the baby, they kept saying, “Nicketti.” The word fascinated him; and when he asked what it mean
2 CONC t, the Indians told him it was their word for “pretty baby.” Dr. Floyd thereupon decided to name the baby Nicketti. This story, however, is refused by the child’s mother, who, in her letter to her son Rush, states “The father of R
2 CONC obert Davis had married a half-breed girl, Nicketti.” (Smith, 2011: 33, ''citing'' Anna M. Cartlidge, "The Children and Grandchildren of William and Abadiah (Davis) Floyd” (1966)).
2 CONT
2 CONT == Chronological Issues ==
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Catherine Seaman, "The genealogy of Opechancanough's descendants is biologically possible although Opechancanough was said to be nearly 100 years old when he was murdered In 1644. 'The daughter of his old age' Nickett
2 CONC i. may have been born when he was in his 70s, (by 1614), and she may have married in the early 1630s. Nicketti's daughter, who was married about 1680, must have been born in the late 1650s when Nicketti was in her late thirties." 
2 CONC (Seaman, 1992: 158).
2 CONT
2 CONT Chronological difficulties arise because "Trader Hughes is supposed to have established a Trading Post in Amherst County, Virginia. Traders began moving into this area of Virginia between 1710 and 1720.  If Trader Hughes was born 
2 CONC in as late as 1635 he would have been 85 years old when he set up shop. Nicketti would also be around 80-85." (jeaniesgenealogy.com, Nov. 16, 2011). 
2 CONT
2 CONT == Floyd Version (1843) ==
2 CONT
2 CONT "William Floyd left the eastern shore (and) went up the country as far as Amherst County, which was then a very wild region; he met with a family by the name of Davis whose ancestors had come from Wales. They had traded with the C
2 CONC atawba Indians and got much property in that way. The father of Robert Davis had married a half-breed Indian girl, Nickette. This Robert Davis was the father of Miss Abidiah Davis whom Mr. William Floyd married. Davis owned many o
2 CONC f the rich lands of Amherst. His other daughters married a Mr. Venable and Gen'l Even Shelby of Maryland. His oldest son, Robert Davis, emigrated even at that early day to Natchez where many of his descendants lived." (Letitia Pre
2 CONC ston Floyd, 1843 in her "Memoirs" letter written in Cairo, Illinois).
2 CONT
2 CONT == Cabell Version (1895) ==
2 CONT
2 CONT "[W]e will consider another very interesting tradition, which has it that Mrs. Elizabeth Cabell was descended from an Indian princess of the Powhatan tribe (some accounts have it 'of the Catawba tribe,' but this is not tenable), a
2 CONC nd that it was the knowledge among the neighboring Indians of this descent which protected her husband while locating these lands, and herself when she was managing them in his absence. It was more probably owing to her relationsh
2 CONC ip to members of the Society of Friends, with whom the Indians were on friendly terms. However, the story is interesting, and 'the evidences of its truth' are said to 'have been carefully collected' in several branches of the Brec
2 CONC kinridge, Floyd, and other families. I cannot vouch for it, but I will give it as I find it in the Floyd tradition.
2 CONT
2 CONT "'Opechancanough, the celebrated chief of the Powhatans, who was brutally murdered, while a prisoner, in 1644, left a lovely young daughter, the child of his old age, the Princess Nicketti — 'she sweeps the dew from the flowers.' 
2 CONC Some years after this graceful Indian maiden had reached the years of mature womanhood, a member [the name is not given] of one of the old Cavalier families of Virginia ' fell in love with her and she with him,' and the result was
2 CONC  a clandestine marriage, and a half-breed Indian girl who married about the year 1680 a Welshman (others say a native of Devonshire, England,) named Nathaniel Davis, an Indian trader, and, according to some accounts, a Quaker ; an
2 CONC d from this alliance many notable people in the East and in the West have descended. Their daughter, Mary Davis (born about 1685), married Samuel Burks of Hanover (the ancestors of the Burks family of Virginia), and their daughter
2 CONC , Elizabeth Burks, married Capt. William Cabell, the ancestor of the Cabells ; Martha Davis, another daughter, married Abraham Venable, the ancestor of the Venables. Robert Davis, Sr., a son (the ancestor of ' the black Davises ' 
2 CONC of Kentucky, and from whom Jefferson Davis descended), had a daughter, Abadiah (or Abigail) Davis, who married William Floyd, the ancestor of the Floyds of Virginia and of the West. A daughter, or granddaughter, of the Quaker, mar
2 CONC ried Gen'l Evan Shelby of Maryland, the ancestor of the Shelbys of the West. Samuel and Philip Davis of the Blue Mountains were sons, and there may have been other sons and daughters.
2 CONT
2 CONT "'William Floyd left the eastern shore of Virginia, went up the country as far as the present Amherst County, which was then a very wild region, where he met with this family of Davis, who had traded with the Indians and had gotte
2 CONC n much property in that way. [The Quakers were much given to friendly trading with the Indians.]
2 CONT
2 CONT "'William Floyd and his wife's brother, Robert Davis, Jr., with their families, emigrated to Kentucky with the first settlers, and finally located in the Bear-grass region, near Louisville, where the kinsmen (Floyds and Davises) h
2 CONC ad a fort, called 'Floyd's Station.'
2 CONT
2 CONT "But it is not necessary to follow the Floyd narrative farther. It seems well to say, however, that I have seen a Davis pedigree which asserts that 'the Indian blood first entered the family through the marriage of Abby Davis with
2 CONC  William Floyd, a half breed Indian.' Other Davis pedigrees and traditions do not deny the Indian blood, while every Floyd with whom I have corresponded has asserted positively that 'it was through Abby Davis the Indian blood came
2 CONC .'
2 CONT
2 CONT "The Princess Nicketti's name (it may be because the marriage was clandestine) has not been popular among her traditional descendants. The first Governor, John Floyd of Virginia, named one of his daughters for her. I know of no ot
2 CONC her namesake ; but if the tradition is true, no more lovely women than some among her descendants ever 'swept the dew from the flowers.'" (Brown, 1895: 42-44).
2 CONT
2 CONT == Floyd Version (1912) ==
2 CONT
2 CONT "In a region which was little more that a primeval forest, now known as Amherst County, he [Col. William Floyd] patented a body of land on which he made a home for himself. A dozen miles distant was the commodious Bungalow of Nath
2 CONC aniel Davis, a Welshman by descent, and one of the very early settlers in that region. He had made quite a large fortune by trading with the Catawba and other Indians, and by locating choice river-bottom lands from the present sit
2 CONC e of Lynchburg up to the Balcony Falls. Mr. Davis had among other children a beautiful daughter named Abadiah, whom the young man [Col. William Floyd] fell in love with and won her for his bride. She was of excellent Welsh ancestr
2 CONC y on her father’s side, and one-fourth of her blood on her mother’s side, was derived from the most distinguished Indian ancestry. Her mother’s mother, Nicketti—Indian equivalent for “Beautiful Flower”—was a granddaughter of the n
2 CONC oted Powhatan (the daughter of his youngest daughter) while the father of Nicketti was a chief of the small but warlike Cayuga tribe. Nicketti, whom the white people dubbed “Princess Nicketti,” married a noted Scotch hunter and fu
2 CONC r trader by the name of Hughes who made his chief headquarter near the beautiful Balcony Falls of James River, where Nathaniel Davis met and married a daughter of his who was the mother of Abadiah." (Floyd, 1912: 11).
2 CONT
2 CONT == Seaman Version (1992) ==
2 CONT
2 CONT "Living far from the courthouse of old Henrico, the first settlers often failed to patent the land they settled, leaving more oral traditions than courthouse documents to tell us their story. Traditions have it that as early as 17
2 CONC 10-1720, a man known only as Trader Hughes lived with his Indian wife in his trading post built along the banks of the upper James River near Otter Creek. He was among the first white settlers, if not the first, to settle that par
2 CONC t of the county, then the wild frontier of old Henrico County.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Who was Trader Hughes? Several men by the name of Hughes had arrived in the Colony in the 1630s, but which Hughes had made his way to the banks of Otter Creek near the borders of old Amherst is not yet known. Neither does anyone 
2 CONC know where Hughes met his wife, both may have come from the Tidewater, or Hughes may have been a trader in the western area before he met her. In any case, Hughes was safe enough among the remaining Indians to locate his trading p
2 CONC ost close to the Indian path that followed the James River through the Blue Ridge to the Warrior's Path. He had the skills to construct a trading post and a stone chimney that lasted for years. Dr. William Cabell, qualifying as as
2 CONC sistant surveyor of Albemarle in 1746, used the chimney as a landmark to locate his earliest surveys, and the old chimney continued to appear as a boundary marker in the deeds for many years. As late as 1977, the McLeRoys report t
2 CONC he ruins of a massive, two-story log building in the woods behind Otter Lake that they took to be the remains of Hughes's trading post.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Hughes's wife, according to traditions in the Floyd family, was a descendant of Powhatan's brother, Opechancanough (Brown, 1895: 46-47; 57-58; Woods, 1901: 49). Murdered after the Indian uprising of 1644, Opechancanough left a yo
2 CONC ung daughter, ...the child of his old age,... named Princess Nicketti - 'she sweeps the dew from the flowers,' who clandestinely married an unnamed member of an old 'Cavalier' family. Brown writes that 'he fell in love with her an
2 CONC d she with him' (1895: 46). Nicketti and the Englishman had a daughter who, about 1680, married an Indian trader, Nathaniel Davis, a Welshman or native of Devonshire, and in addition, a Quaker. The couple had at least three childr
2 CONC en, Mary Davis, born about 1685, Martha, and Robert Davis." (Seaman, 1992 : 156-57).
2 CONT
2 CONT == Other Versions of the Story ==
2 CONT
2 CONT Information from Arnold gedcom, JD Watson: Opechancanough, the celebrated chief of the Powhatans, was brutally murdered while a prisoner in 1644. He left a lovely young daughter, the child of his old age, the Princess Nicketti. So
2 CONC me years after this graceful Indian maiden had reached the years of mature womanhood, a member (the name is not given)} of one of the old Cavalier families of Virginia "fell in love with her and she with him". This resulted in a c
2 CONC landestine marriage and the birth of of a half-breed Indian girl, (Mary) Elizabeth Hughes, who married about 1680 a Welshman (others say a native of Devonshire, England), named Nathaniel Davis. Nathaniel was an Indian trader and a
2 CONC ccording to some accounts, a Quaker. From these alliances of indian and white man many notable families in Virginia are descended.
2 CONT
2 CONT == DNA Research ==
2 CONT
2 CONT Powhatan at the end of the names are identifiers for tribal affiliation only, no records exist of any individual actually having the last name Powhatan. The current Pamunkey (Powhatan) tribe only states that her history was so lon
2 CONC g ago, physical documentation is non existent, not she wasn't an actual historical figure. All we have currently is oral history passed down through the generations. A Dr. Glanville didn't state she wasn't "real" just that without
2 CONC  proper proof, it can't be documented as fact either way. A great deal of research has been done on Nicketti to include discussion with a current Pamunkey tribal chief which states the same, "can't prove either way". The Pamunkey 
2 CONC Historian, also has no written record of her, so her "supposed"  tribe couldn't say either way. Perhaps one day solid proof will surface. Please do NOT bother her tribe with questions of enrollment, or records. They have no opinio
2 CONC n on her, nor opinions on what others say of  her. I have personally searched several VA state museums and discussed with historians of which some agreed this is actual VA history, her life that is. I personally believe the many o
2 CONC ral family stories I have heard over the years giving the same facts and circumstances but coming from different families that have never met one another. There is a new document added showing the Floyd's were targeted by Walter P
2 CONC lecker, it also shows that the Monacan Tribe (another tribe of the Powhatan) seem to show a Robert Davis. The Floyds had a  Robert Davis also as their  Grandfather of all the Floyds that were claiming this on the census documents 
2 CONC changed.  I still haven't verified this to be the same Robert Davis the Floyd family lore arose from. But I know there was a Robert Davis living in the same time periods  around the same time.  I added what information the Monacan
2 CONC  posted on their Robert Davis to our Robert Davis' profile,  it's showing though he as a Saponi tribe member. Really should be researched in more depth,  I was unable to download this section here but will try later, this informat
2 CONC ion is on our Robert Davis profile though.  
2 CONT
2 CONT In media section, just my latest on the search, nothing has been verified yet nor am I thinking this is our Robert but would be great to see where their Robert got his Davis surname from . 
2 CONT
