1 NAME Mary /Adams/
2 GIVN Mary
2 SURN Adams
2 _MARNM Moorman
2 NICK Mary Ann (Addams) Moreman
1 SEX F
1 BIRT
2 DATE OCT 1720
2 ADDR
3 CITY Goochland
3 STAE Virginia
1 DEAT
2 DATE JAN 1809
2 PLAC Louisa, Virginia, United States
2 ADDR
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Death January 1809?
2 CONT
2 CONT Possibly due to failing health, Robert Addams wrote his Will in Feb 1738. His estate was left to his wife Mourning, sons James and Robert and daughters Judith Clark, Mary Moreman [Moorman], Eliza Moreman [Moorman], Agnes Farguson 
2 CONC [Ferguson], Susanna, Lucy, Anna the younger and Sally.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT * https://www.familysearch.org/en/tree/person/M82T-F9X
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary Adams, born Abt. 1715 in Virginia; died 1757. She was the daughter of Captain Robert Adams and Mourning Lewis.
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles Moorman414,415, born October 1715 in Hanover, now Louisa Co., Virginia; died October 12, 1778 in Louisa County, Virginia. He was the son of Charles Moorman and Elizabeth Reynolds. He married Mary Adams Abt. 1733 in Camp Cr
2 CONC eek, Louisa Co., Virginia.  The son of Elizabeth (nee Reynolds) and Charles Moorman, Charles was born in Virginia. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Mary Adams and they had at least eight children: 
2 CONT * 1. Elizabeth (b. 1737/1738); 
2 CONT * 2. Lucy (b.1741); 
2 CONT * 3. Agge b.1743) who married John Venable (1740-1811); 
2 CONT * 4. Charles (b. 1746); 
2 CONT * 5. Jude 1748-1774) who married Christoper Anthony; 
2 CONT * 6. Molley (b. 1751); 
2 CONT * 7. Robert (b. 1753); 
2 CONT * 8. Thomas (b. 1756). The children's birth dates are from Hinshaw 's Encyclopedia of American Genealogy.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles inherited 300 acres on Hudson's Creek in what is today Louisa County, Virginia. He was a Quaker and a very successful businessman. He owned and operated at good profit a grist mill on Husdon's Creek. He owned many acres of
2 CONC  land and numerous slaves in two counties. When Charles died in 1778, his estate was valued at 3100 pounds in Albemarle County and more in Louisa County. (Polly Ryan, Journey in Virginia.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles lived during an interesting period of development for the Quakers in Virginia. Up until this time the Quakers were prosperous planters who, like the rest of society that they lived in, used Negro slaves to operate their pl
2 CONC antations. Quaker conscience was now beginning to see things differently however. On May 28, 1778, by deeds of manumission, Charles Moorman provided for the freedom of his thirty-three slaves - - those of age immediately and those
2 CONC  under age on becoming of age. These deeds were confirmed in his will dated September 2, 1778, in which his executors were particularly instructed to make application to the General Assembly for an act legalizing the "freedom here
2 CONC by intended."
2 CONT
2 CONT The following is an example of these deeds, all of which, with the exception of the name of the slave, were alike: "I, Charles Moorman, from mature consideration and the conviction of my own mind, being fully persuaded that freeed
2 CONC om is the natural right of all mankind, and that no law, moral or divine, has given me a right to or property in the persons of any of my fellow creatures, and being desirous to fulfill the injunction of our Lord and Saviour, Jesu
2 CONC s Christ, by doing to others as I would be done by, do therefore declare that having under my care a negro man named Jack aged fifty years, I do for myself, my heirs, executors, and administrators hereby release unto him, the said
2 CONC  Jack, all my right, interest, claim, or pretension of claim whatsoever to his person or any estate he may acquire, without any interruption from me or any person claiming for, by, from, or under me. In witness I have hereunto set
2 CONC  my hand and seal this 28 day of 5th mo. 1778." (Office Clerk of Court, Campbell County, Virginia, Deed Book, No. 2, pp 418-419.) On December 12, 1787, the General Assembly passed an act (12 Henning 613-16) carrying into effect Ch
2 CONC arles' benevolent intentions. Since some of his slaves had been sold by his children and were in the possession of new owners in South Carolina and Georgia, Christopher Johnson, one of the executors, was put to great expense, trav
2 CONC elling upwards of ten thousand miles in executing the will. With a view to obtaining the freedom of one such slave, Milley, the executors brought suit in the Superior Court of South Carolina, losing the suit (1 Bay 232-35; 2 Call 
2 CONC 297.) The possession of thirty-three slaves indicates that Charles was relatively a large slaveholder, since an estimate shows that more than 96 per cent of Virginia slaveholders at this time owned fewer than thirty-three slaves. 
2 CONC This information was from an article "The Moorman Family of Virginia", by Charles Paullin in The William and Mary College Quarterly Historical Magazine, 2nd Series, Volume XII (1932) p. 177-180.
2 CONT --------------------
