1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Richard was Clerk of the Superior Court of Atlanta from 1862 until he died on Sept. 5, 1873. James D. Collins, son of James A., filled the vacancy on Oct. 6, 1873. Venable's first job in Atlanta was as a bookkeeper
2 CONC  for cotton merchant, A.B. Forsyth. In 1856 he was elected to Clerk of the Superior Court. He married Cornelia Hoyt, sister of Judge Samuel Bogle Hoyt. In January 1862 William was elected to succeed Benjamin P. Bomar as Clerk of t
2 CONC he Superior Court and office that he held previously. He lived in Jackson Co., VA before moving to Atlanta in 1848. In 1864 as General Sherman and the Union Army approached Atlanta, William Richard Venable hid the Superior Court R
2 CONC ecords in a false bottom wagon taking them to his old home in Jackson County for safe keeping. Call for a public meeting on June 24, 1865 was made by Venable as Clerk of the Superior Court, Mayor Calhoun and four other citizens to
2 CONC  consider return to the Union. One of these citizens James L. Dunning was made U.S. Marshall. William died at his home on Forsyth Street and the funeral was held at the First Presbyterian Church. William and Cornelia had nine chil
2 CONC dren, seven of whom survived him and Elizabeth was born a month and a half later. The funeral, reported to be one of the largest seen in years, filled the church with friends and family, many people had to remain outside for lack 
2 CONC of room. The procession was so long, that when the hearse was passing City Hall the last carriage had not left Broad Street.* Reference: [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/31351699/william-richard-venable Find A Grave Memorial] 
2 CONC - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Apr 4 2022, 16:34:43 UTC''
