1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Migrated from Talbot or Dorchester Co., MD. to Orange Co., N.C. in 1753 with a stop-over in Culpepper Co., VA.
2 CONT
2 CONT Relgion: Member of the Buffalo Presbyterian Church. Listed among the 'Nottingham Colony'. The founders of Buffalo Presbyterian Church in Orange, later Guilford Co., N.C. John Nicks secured grants for two sections just east of the 
2 CONC church.
2 CONT
2 CONT Listed among the 'Nottingham Colony' ~ founders of Buffalo Presbyterian Church in Orange, (later Gulford Co.) North Carolina.
2 CONT
2 CONT John Nicks migrated from Talbot or Dorchester County, Maryland to Orange County, North Carolina in 1753, with a stopover in Culpepper County, Virginia. He is listed among the "Nottingham Colony," the founders of Buffalo Presbyteri
2 CONC an Church in Orange, later Guilford County, North Carolina.
2 CONT
2 CONT His wife was Margaret, and their children were Sarah, George (1756-1838), John, Elizabeth, Nancy, Quinton, and two other daughters, one of whom married Bazell Brasher, and the other married Isaac Brasher, sons of Robert, and both 
2 CONC moved to Tennessee after the War. Sarah married William Spruce and lived on the south side of North Buffalo; John moved to Tennessee after the War; Elizabeth married George Purcell of Rockingham County; Nancy married Zacheriah Rob
2 CONC erts and moved to Tennessee after the War; Quinton died unmarried; George married Elizabeth, and their children were John, Sarah, Margaret, George, Elizabeth, Yarburough and Anne. George, Sr., lived at the J. Al Rankin place. John
2 CONC  Nicks, Sr., died in 1781. [end of page 26]
2 CONT
2 CONT Parents: George Blake Nicks (1689 in London, Kent, Eng.–?) and Phoebe Price (?–?). 
2 CONT *SIBLINGS: unknown. 
2 CONT *Spoude: Margaret Quinton Edwards (1717–?) in 1736 in Maryland.
2 CONT
2 CONT CHILDREN:
2 CONT *SONS: Quinton (1740–1813), unmarried; George (1756–1838); and John (1758–1825), who m. Margaret Doaks.
2 CONT *DAUGHTERS: Margaret (abt. 1734–?), who m. Moses Short; Phoebe (1738-1811), who married Robert Samuel Brashears; Elizabeth (1741–?), who m. George Purcell; Rebecca (1742–?), who m. George Ford; Sarah (1742–1819), who m. William Sp
2 CONC ruce.
2 CONT
2 CONT 'PRIVATE CAREER.- EDUCATION: level of literacy unclear; his mark appears on his Granville land grants instead of a signature.
2 CONT
2 CONT *OCCUPATION: farmer.
2 CONT
2 CONT PUBLIC CAREER.'- COUNTY OFFICES: served as constable for the lower settlement on Saxopahaw (Haw River) in Rowan (Guilford) Co.NC.
2 CONT *MINOR OFFICES: served on petit jury in March 1754. 
2 CONT
2 CONT WEALTH DURING LIFETIME - LAND: obtained a Granville land grant containing 650 acres in Rowan (Guilford) Co. for three shillings proclamation money (and twenty-six shillings yearly rent) on December 4, 1753, and sold to James Denny
2 CONC  for £200 in June 1762; obtained a Granville land grant containing 640 acres in Orange Co. for ten shillings sterling (and twenty-five shillings and seven and one-half pence yearly rent) on July 29, 1760; sold 230 acres in Guilfor
2 CONC d Co. to James Denny for £100 (currency of Va.) in March 1772. 
2 CONT
2 CONT WEALTH AT DEATH -  PERSONAL PROPERTY unknown. No record of a will or estate inventory has been located.
2 CONT
2 CONT .Sources: 
2 CONT *https://barmeach.wordpress.com/2015/07/09/18/
2 CONT *http://hdl.handle.net/1805/2028
2 CONT *https://scholarworks.iupui.edu/bitstream/handle/1805/2028/The%20Nottingham%20Settlement--Introduction.pdf?sequence=2&isAllowed=y pg. 3
2 CONT *https://scholarworks.iupui.edu/bitstream/handle/1805/2028/Chapter%203%20--%20Analysis.pdf?sequence=5&isAllowed=y pg. 69, 71, 72, 75, 77, 79, 81
