1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Moses Winters is a DAR Patriot Ancestor, A128955. There is warning on DAR File. 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.tnssar.org/patriot_grave_reports/Moses_Winters.pdf
2 CONT
2 CONT Fold3.com, Revolutionary War Service Records, Winters, Moses, https://www.fold3.com/image/21357702?terms=winters,north,moses,carolina : accessed 20 January 2022
2 CONT
2 CONT Source information: Fold3, Moses Winters (https://www.fold3.com/memorial/630303323/moses-winters : accessed 21 Jan 2022), database and images, https://www.fold3.com/memorial/630303323/moses-winters
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Winters-859
2 CONT
2 CONT The Moses Winters memorial stone & his gravestone were erected by the descendants of Moses and Elizabeth in the Head Cemetery, however, 
2 CONT
2 CONT Moses and Elizabeth are NOT buried in this Head Cem. They are buried at 
2 CONT
2 CONT Wessyngton on part of their property Moses' son inherited and sold to Joseph Washington at Cedar Hill...this is where Moses and Elizabeth are buried.
2 CONT
2 CONT Much of the Winters genealogy has been kindly shared by Ken Winters. His email is: kgwinters@hotmail.com 
2 CONT
2 CONT Will of Moses Winters
2 CONT
2 CONT Recorded - Wills, Inventories, Bonds Robertson Co., Tennessee
2 CONT
2 CONT Vol. 1 1796-1812 pp 30-31
2 CONT
2 CONT I, Moses winters, of the County of Robertson, State of Tennessee, being very sick but of sound and perfect mind and memory but knowing the uncertainty of this transitory life, do think proper ot make this, my last will and testeme
2 CONC nt in manner and form followint: (to wit)
2 CONT
2 CONT First, I lend to my loving wife, Elizabeth Winters, all of my stock of cattle, hogs, and horses. Also all of my household and kitchen furniture and plantation utensils. Also the plantation and lands where I now live (to be at her 
2 CONC disposal_ for and during her natural life or widowhood (and no longer.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Also I give and bequeath to the heirs of my daughter, Elizabeth Nicks, the sum of one dollar (to be paid out of my estate by my executyrix, hereafter named) to her and her heirs in lieu and satisfaction of any dower they may herea
2 CONC fter claim in my estate.
2 CONT
2 CONT Also I give and bequeath to my daughter, Sarah Nicks, the sum of one dollar to be paid to her out of my estate by my executrix hereafter named, to her and her heirs forever in lieu and satisfaction of any dower they may hereafter 
2 CONC claim on my estate.
2 CONT
2 CONT Also I give and bequeath to my daughters Pheobe McNeily, Mary Smoathers, Nancy Cocks, Catherine Cocks, Amy Winters and Elizabeth Wood, all of my stock of cattle (said stock being lent to my wife) to be equally divided among my sai
2 CONC d daughters after the death of my wife (or in case she should marry) to them and their heirs forever.
2 CONT
2 CONT Also I give and bequeath to my son, Caleb Winters, the sum of ten dollars to be paid to him or his heirs out of my estate by my executrix hereafter named, to him and his heirs forever.
2 CONT
2 CONT Also I give and bequeath to my son, Moses Winters, (on his attaining the age of twenty-one years) a tract of land beginning forty poles above a cave spring on the west side of Caleb's Creek )the spring being between my fields and 
2 CONC the mouth of said creek) running on east course until it comes to Benjamin Manee's line then with his line to Sulphur Fork Creek, thence down said creek to the mouth of Caleb's Creek, then with the line to the beginning to him and
2 CONC  his heirs forever.
2 CONT
2 CONT And after the death of my wife (or in case she should marry) I give my son, Moses Winters, half the stock of hogs and horses, household and kitchen furniture and plantation tuinsils (which I have herein lent my wife) to him and hi
2 CONC s heirs forever. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Also I give and bequeath to my son Aaron Winters, after his attaining the age of twenty-one years, all the remaining part of my land and after the death of my wife, Elizabeth (or in case she should marry) I also give and bequeath 
2 CONC to my son Aaron Winters, the remaining half of the household and kitchen furniture and plantation utinsils (Which I have herein lent my wife) to him and his heirs forever.
2 CONT
2 CONT WILL AND INVENTORY MOSES WINTERS
2 CONT
2 CONT Also what monies I may have by me or due to me by bonds and credit, it is my will that my executrix, hereafter, named, collect as soon as may be convenient and threrof pay all my debts. And the remainder (if any) I give to my lovi
2 CONC ng wife, Elizabeth Winters, to use as need requires.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lastly, I hereby constitute my loving wife, Elizabeth Winters, My whole and sole executrix of this, my last will and testement, hereby revoking and annuling all wills by me heretofore made. Ratifying, confirming and establishing t
2 CONC his and no other to be my last will and testement. In witness, whereof, I have hereunto set my hand and affix my seal this ninth day of July One Thousand Seven Hundred and Ninety-eight. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Signed Moses Winters (Seal)
2 CONT
2 CONT SIGNED, SEALED, PUBLISHED, PRONOUNCED, AND DECLARED BY SAID MOSES WINTERS TO BE HIS LAST WILL AND TESTEMENT IN THE PRESENCE OF US.
2 CONT
2 CONT MILES KIRBY
2 CONT
2 CONT WILLIAM FLEWELLEN
2 CONT
2 CONT Robertson County Court October term 1798, the above will was proven in open court agreeable to law by the oath of William Flewellen, a subscribing witness thereto. TEST THOMAS JOHNSON CLERK.
2 CONT
2 CONT ____________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT INVENTORY OF THE ESTATE OF MOSES WINTERS, DECEASEDD. DELIVERED UNTO COURT AT JANUARY TERM 1799 BY THE EXECUTRIX.
2 CONT
2 CONT 7 horses 2 kittles 1 doz. plates 
2 CONT
2 CONT 30 hogs 1 pot 3 dishes
2 CONT
2 CONT 16 cattle 3 small lise pots 3 basons
2 CONT
2 CONT 2 narrow axes 1 dutch oven 2 feather beds
2 CONT
2 CONT 1 broad ax 1 fry pan and furniture
2 CONT
2 CONT 1 hand ax 1 skillet 2 set plow irons
2 CONT
2 CONT 3 weding hoes 1 mattock 1 saw
2 CONT
2 CONT 2 chisels 1 draw knife 3 augers
2 CONT
2 CONT 1 guage 2 iron wedges 1 fro 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1 iron square 1 sett makers tools 440 acres land
2 CONT
2 CONT Moses Winters and his wife Elizabeth were among the first settlers who came to Robertson County Tennessee in 1779.
2 CONT
2 CONT Moses Winters came from SC to the Holston Settlement in East TN where he joined with James Robertson to travel overland to Middle TN, they arrived there Dec 15, 1779. His wife, Elizabeth (Head) Winters and their seven daughters fo
2 CONC llowed in the John Donelson's Flotilla down the Tennessee River, and arrived in April of 1780. 
2 CONT
2 CONT They were innkeepers and were granted Tavern License in 1797.
2 CONT
2 CONT Submitted by Margaret Nolen Nichol, Jacksonville, Florida:
2 CONT
2 CONT Moses Winters and his son, Caleb, and stepson, Lewis Crain received 640 acres for helping defend the early settlements against the Indians.
2 CONT
2 CONT They helped build and defend Heaton's Station. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Moses Winters and John Crain, the eldest son of Elizabeth, served in Christian's Campaign in the upper East Tennessee valley. This campaign was organized to put down the uprisings of the Indian tribes who were allied with the Brit
2 CONC ish. Two expeditions were made before the majority of the Indian Chiefs sued for peace.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jury Duty, 17 Oct 1796, Robertson County, Tennessee. John Crain was on Grand Jury: The court order that John Crain, Caleb Winters, Isaac Menses, Joseph Barns, Jonathan Dadin, Matthew Johnston, Moses Winters, Benjamin Minses, Wm. B
2 CONC rown, Sr., Wm. Fort, Hugh Lewis, Archer Cheatham, Jr., Archer Cheatham, Ephraim Pratt, David Hamilton, and Elijah Hamilton lay off a road from Brown's fork of Karr's Creek to the road leading from Winters to John Donelsons, about 
2 CONC a half mile above said Winters to Brown's ford on Red River, thence to the Kentucky line, crossing Elk Fork at the mouth of Hopper's Hollow. They are to alter the public road by Winter's plantation as much as they think fit doing 
2 CONC justice to said Winters farm and also to the public road. Above order granted October term, 1796, on which Jury returned "nothing done for want of the Sheriff". (In order to serve on the Grand Jury, a person had to own, at least, 
2 CONC three hundred and fifty acres.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Jan 1798, Robertson County, Tennessee: John Crain, Ezekiel Clampet, Archer Cheatham, Sr., Samuel Crocket, Jacob McCarty, Moses Winters, Sr., Benjamin Menee, and James Long to alter the road from Springfield to Clarksville at the F
2 CONC ord of Hoods Branch crossing said branch as near old road as the ground permits for a wagon road, falling into the road from Brown Fork to Red River about a mile from where it falls into old county road, thence to Moses Winters.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jury Duty, 16 Apr 1798
2 CONT
2 CONT DAR Posting for Moses:
2 CONT
2 CONT WINTERS, Moses
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth: Circa 1740
2 CONT
2 CONT Service: NC
2 CONT
2 CONT Rank: PS
2 CONT
2 CONT Death: TN p 7-9-1798
2 CONT
2 CONT Patriot Pensioned: No Widow Pensioned: No
2 CONT
2 CONT Children Pensioned: No Heirs Pensioned: No
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse: Elizabeth Head 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Wessyngton Plantation was built in 1819 by the Washington family slaves. Joseph Washington was a cousin of George Washington.
2 CONT
2 CONT DEED:
2 CONT
2 CONT Feb 9, 1802
2 CONT
2 CONT Moses WINTERS to Joseph WASHINGTON
2 CONT
2 CONT pg 115) This indenture made this 9th day of February in the year of our Lord one thousand eight hundred & two between Moses WINTERS of the County of Robertson of the one part and Joseph WASHINGTON of the County & State aforesaid o
2 CONC f the other part, witnesseth that the said Moses WINTERS for and in consideration of the sum of eight hundred dollars in hand paid by the said WASHINGTON to him the said WINTERS the receipt whereof is hereby acknowledged, he the s
2 CONC aid Moses WINTERS hat bargained & sold & by these presents do grant bargain, sell, alien, convey and confirm unto the said Joseph WASHINGTON, his heirs and assigns a certin tract or parcel of land situated lying and being in the C
2 CONC ounty & State aforesaid and lying on the South side of Sulpher Fork of Red River, being part of Moses WINTERS deed, preemption containing 204 acres more or less and bounded as follow, to wit, Beginning at a black gum runs West 73 
2 CONC poles to a white oak, thence North 45 degrees West 120 poles to a white oak on the Sulpher Fork Creek, then down the meanders of the Creek as follows, North 10 degrees East 41 poles North 45 degrees East 13 poles, thence North 50 
2 CONC degrees East 67 poles, thence North 40 degrees West 26 poles, thence North 70 degrees West 12 poles, thence West 38 poles, thence North 70 degrees West 32 poles, thence South 70 degrees West 18 poles, thence South 30 degrees West 
2 CONC 40 poles, thence South 18 degrees West 40 poles, thence South 5 degrees East 18 poles, thence South 18 degrees East 23 poles, thence South 70 degrees East 6 poles, thence South 20 degrees East 53 poles, thence South 75 poless, the
