1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Served CO A 1st ALA CAV under Col. George E. Spencer
2 CONT ~~~
2 CONT book, "A Glimpse of The Past" by Carolyn McGough Rowe, 2000, p. 28:
2 CONT Joseph McGough was the fourth of 13 sons born to Welthy Ann NIX and William B. McGOUGH. He may have been the first of William's children to be born in Walker County since William sold his Coosa County land Oct 2, 1839. On Jan 25, 
2 CONC 1841 an Administrator was named as there was no family left in Coosa County to claim the estate of William's father John McGough settled on that date.
2 CONT According to the Civil War pension records, just two months after the start of the War Between The States, on July 20, 1861, in Thorn Hill, Winston County, Alabama, Joseph McGough and Martha Jane GIST (GUESS) (born 1843 in Alabama
2 CONC ) were married by Missionary Baptist Church minister named J. P. Johnson. Martha was the daughter of Mary McNUTT and Christopher GIST. Martha Jane could not write, so her name is spelled different on various documents. Nine months
2 CONC  later, son William Buell was born on 29 April 1862 in Thorn Hill, Winston County, Alabama.
2 CONT According to a deposition given by William McGough, on Sep 8, 1862 his son Joseph along with his brother Richard left home where they joined the Union's 1st Alabama Calvary, Co "A", 1st Regiment, US Army as a Private, in Iuka, Mis
2 CONC sissippi (just across the state line from Florence, Alabama) serving under Col. George E. Spencer. The 1st Alabama Calvary USA was an escort for General Sherman as he made his march through Georgia and South Carolina during the Wa
2 CONC r. Joseph had two brothers who served in the Union Army while four brothers fought for the Confederacy. According to their father William, three of his sons were forcefully drafted with only one who was a true Southern sympathizer
2 CONC .
2 CONT On enlisting Joseph is described as being 5 ft 9 inches with light complexion, gray eyes and brown hair, and being a farmer.
2 CONT In researching the town of Iuka, Tishomingo County, MS and learning of the Battle of Iuka on 19 Sep 1862 which fit with the information for Joseph McGough; that information lead to the Second Battle of Corinth which only substanti
2 CONC ated the information for Joseph.
2 CONT Joseph was in the Iuka-Corinth Mississippi area for two major battles:
2 CONT · The Battle of Iuka, MS on Sep 19, 1862 (263 CSA KIA, 692 Wounded, and 561 captured/ missing)
2 CONT · The 2nd Battle of Corinth, MS on Oct 03-04, 1862 (473 CSA KIA, 1997 Wounded, and 1763 captured/missing)
2 CONT On Oct 03-04, 1862 Joseph was captured and held in Corinth which had sent a Union Garrison to guard the rail crossroads.
2 CONT Joseph died four and one half months later on January 25, 1863 in Corinth, Mississippi, just twenty-five miles from home. Military records indicate Joseph died from a relapse of measles. He was 22. Records indicate Joseph never re
2 CONC ceived any pay. Joseph's burial site is unknown.
2 CONT Son William Buell was just four months old when his father Joseph left home. After Joseph joined the military service, wife Martha Jane left Alabama and on June 3, 1865 from Paducah, McCracken County, Kentucky filed for Joseph's p
2 CONC ension. (Did she move North to avoid persecution from the Rebels?) By March 30, 1870 Martha had returned to Alabama where she drew $8.00 per month for herself and $2.00 per month for son William Buell until she re-married William 
2 CONC John CONLEY (born 1843 Illinois) on Feb 15, 1872 in Winston County, Alabama. Six months later William R. Long from Allen's Factory in Marion County, Alabama was made guardian of William Buell, now age 9, to protect William's finan
2 CONC ces while he drew his father's military pension. William continued to draw $8.00 per month until he was age sixteen on 29 April 1878. William R. Long had served with Joseph McGough in the 1st Alabama Calvary during the war.
2 CONT The 1850 Walker County, Alabama census lists Joseph as age 9 living with his parents.
2 CONT The 1860 Kansas, Walker County, Alabama census lists Joseph age 18 living with his parents.
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph's parents and most of his siblings are buried at Flatwoods Cemetery, Nauvoo, Walker County, Alabama.
