1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * JACKSON, SAMUEL [http://services.dar.org/Public/DAR_Research/search_adb/?action=full&p_id=A061253 DAR Ancestor #: A061253]
2 CONT * Service NORTH CAROLINA  Rank(s): PRIVATE
2 CONT * Birth 1758 PHILADELPHIA-NEAR PENNSYLVANIA
2 CONT * Death 6-18-1834 STOKES CO NORTH CAROLINA
2 CONT * Pension Number S*W5004
2 CONT * Service Source S*W5004
2 CONT * Service Description 1) CAPTS CLOUD, GARIUS, DAVIS & LOPP 2) COLS SHEPPARD & SMITH, NC LINE
2 CONT *SPOUSE  1)  HANNAH GIBSON
2 CONT
2 CONT RESIDENCE
2 CONT *  1) County: ROWAN CO - State: NORTH CAROLINA
2 CONT *  2) County: SURRY CO - State: NORTH CAROLINA
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel Jackson, Jr Birth: 23 Jan 1758 Chester County, Pennsylvania, USA Death: 6 Jun 1834 (aged 76) Stokes County, North Carolina, USA Burial: Unknown
2 CONT
2 CONT Memorial #: 100775358 Bio: "Early Families of the North Carolina Counties of Rockingham and Stokes with Revolutionary Service", Vol. 2Born 1758 near Philadelphia PA" "on the Delaware River", died 6 June 1834, Stokes Co., NC, marri
2 CONC ed by Micajah Clark, JP in Surry Co., NC 23 Jan. 1782, Hannah Gibson, born 9 Oct. 1764, died 1841 (will probated Dec. 1841).  Samuel moved when young with his parents to Rowen Co., NC, that part that became Davidson Co., NC.  Whil
2 CONC e a resident of Surry Co., NC, on the Dan River, he enlisted and served at various times in 1780 and early 1781 - in all, three months - as a private in Capt. Joseph Cloud's Company in the regiment of Col. William Sheppard.While l
2 CONC iving in Rowan Co., he enlisted and served at various times for four months as a private under Capt. William David in Col. Smith's Regiment.  He was captured by Tories during this time but soon released and later assisted in scatt
2 CONC ering the Tories at Raft Swamp.  He was discharged after the surrender of Cornwallis.  Samuel Jackson was granted a pension of $20.00 per annum on his application which was executed in Stokes Co. 15 March 1833.  Hannah Jackson was
2 CONC  granted a widow's pension after filing application on 9 March 1839 in Stokes  Co. Samuel Jackson had been a member of the Quaker Westfield Friends Church and was disowned  for marrying "out of unity".
2 CONT
2 CONT Other children of Samuel and Hannah:
2 CONT
2 CONT *Jackson, Isaac, b. 1787 Surry Co., NC, d. bef July 1854, Delaware Co., IN - married Nancy Sizemore and Jemima
2 CONT * Jackson, Sarah, b. 1793 Stokes Co., NC, d. 1851 Hamilton Co., IN - married Isaac Myers 18 April 1820, Stokes Co., NC
2 CONT *Jackson, John, b. 1793 Stokes Co., NC, d. 19 Aug 1854 Laclede Co., MO - married Nancy King 2 July 1819, Stokes Co., NC
2 CONT *Jackson, William, b. 1795 Stokes Co., NC
2 CONT *Jackson, Amer, born 1797, Stokes Co., NC, d. after 1870, maybe Mercer Co., IL - married Elizabeth Watson and Nancy Hooker.
2 CONT *Jackson, Hiram W., b. 28 Oct 1801 Stokes Co., NC, d. 9 Nov. 1875 Henry Co., IA - Married Nancy Malcolm 5 Oct 1826, Putnam Co., IN
2 CONT *Jackson, Joseph Henry, b. 23 Aug 1804, Stokes Co., NC, d. 25 March 1858, Louisa Co., IA - married Phoebe Cox 6 Sep 1827, Randolph Co. IN
2 CONT
2 CONT —-
2 CONT
2 CONT From https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/100775358/samuel-jackson
2 CONT
2 CONT Family Members
2 CONT *	Spouse		Hannah Gibson Jackson                	1764-1841
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT * 		Jehu Jackson                	1783-1850
2 CONT * 		James Hiram Jackson                	1785-1853
2 CONT * 		Elizabeth Jackson Ross                	1792-1847
2 CONT * 		Samuel Jackson                	1792-1849
2 CONT * 		Joseph Jackson                	1804-1858
2 CONT
2 CONT Created by: Diane Miller (47219028) Added: 16 Nov 2012 URL: https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/100775358/samuel-jackson Citation: Find A Grave, database and images (https://www.findagrave.com : accessed 27 February 2019), memoria
2 CONC l page for Samuel Jackson, Jr (23 Jan 1758–6 Jun 1834), Find A Grave Memorial no. 100775358, ; Maintained by Diane Miller (contributor 47219028) Unknown.
2 CONT
2 CONT ------
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Notes by Clarence Leon Goins #RH1690982, Volunteer Old Cheraw at Large
2 CONT
2 CONT From https://wc.rootsweb.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=boisejo1&id=I29198
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel Jackson, Jr. was disowned from the Westfield Friends Church due to  marrying "out of unity." He enlisted and served at various times in 1780 and 1781 as a private under Captain William David in Col.Smith's Regiment. He was 
2 CONC captured by the Tories during this time, but soon released and discharged after the surrender of Cornwallis. He was granted a pension of $20.00 per year on his application which was executed in Stokes, North Carolina on 15 Mar 183
2 CONC 3. The early census records use the names Samuel Jackson, Sr. and SamuelJackson, Jr, and Samuel Jr., gave almost the same information in his request for pension that Jacob Jackson gave on his "Testimony." He stated he was born in 
2 CONC Chester Co., PA and that his parents moved to Rowan Co.,North Carolina when he was around 12 years old. When his parents, Samuel and Catherine Jackson moved to Tom's Creek, their land was in Surry Co., but when Stokes County was f
2 CONC ormed in 1789, the line was drawn at Westfield and Samuel Jackson, Sr. found his land had been divided by the county line. Samuel Jackson, Jr.'s land was in Stokes Co. North Carolina . On the 15th day of March 1832, personally app
2 CONC eared in open court before the Justice of the Court of Pleas and Quarter Sessions,for the County of Stokes: Samuel Jackson, a resident of North Carolina and the county of Stokes,aged about 74 years, who being first duly sworn in a
2 CONC ccording to law, doth on his oath make the following declaration in order to obtain the benefit of an act of Congress passed 7 Jun 1832. That he entered the service of the US under the following named officers, and served at known
2 CONC  stations. He states first that he was born in the year 1758 in the state of PA a few miles from the City of Philadelphia, on the Delaware River. That he has no record of his age, that when quite young he moved with his parents to
2 CONC  that part of County Rowan in the state of North Carolina that is now called Davidson Co. He states that when he was first called into service he lived in the county of Surry and that aforesaid was on Dan River now in Stokes Co. A
2 CONC t other times in County of Rowan, that since the Revolutionary War he has lived in the county of Stokes, and lives there now. That he first served in a Company commanded by Captain Joseph Cloud of the county of Stokes, then Surry,
2 CONC  in the year of 1780 for a term of four months.Then he volunteered and was put in service and appointed to guard Tory prisoners, at Old Town in Bethabara. That afterwards in the year of 1780, and the second place he served under C
2 CONC aptain Gaines, father of General Gains, and he said that he was employed in guarding British soldiers taken prisoners at the Battle of Kings Mountain, and served for one month. Afterwards in the same and succeeding years he was en
2 CONC gaged and sent on several scouting parties. In all for a term of one month under different officers, whose names he cannot now remember. That when engaged in the service mentioned the assignment to which his company was attached w
2 CONC as commanded part of the time by Col. William Sheppard and Major Joseph Winston. He recollects while on duty at Old Town a private soldier shot a Sgt. Smith for which offense he was merely whipped. Afterwards, I think, in the year
2 CONC  1782,in the county of Rowan, about the time the British marched south toward the Battleground of Guilford, he volunteered his services in a company of militia commanded by Captain William Davis. In which company was chiefly calle
2 CONC d out to hunt Tories and obtain them. He was taken prisoner by the Tories and after being confined a while was released, and services and complacement lasted about one month.Shortly after this, in the same year of 1782, he entered
2 CONC  service again in a company commanded by Captain Lapp and Lieutenant Bodenhamer and marched toward Wilmington, North Carolina and while on the march, they helped to scatter Tories at "Rafts Swamp" and from there he marched to the 
2 CONC mouth of the Neuse River where they made an attack, which was occupied by a British garrison, and then marched toward Georgetown, SC and back. They lay in the backwoods until the news assured them that Cornwallis had been taken. H
2 CONC e was then marched home and discharged. He recollects that at "Rafts Swamp" there were 200 men of Guilford that were shot. He states he has no documentary evidence of his service and knows no person who can testify to his service 
2 CONC except the testimony of Jacob Idol. He also states that in his neighborhood he is known to Charles Banner and Jacob Carson who can testify of his transactions for his service as a soldier of the Revolution. Sworn and submitted thi
2 CONC s day and year aforesaid - Samuel Jackson - (hismark)
2 CONT ——
