2 DATE 17 SEP 1687
2 ADDR
3 STAE Virginia
3 CTRY Colonial America
1 OCCU Cooper
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 1) John Doss probably came to America between 1660 and 1680 from the British Isles.  He was a Cooper (e.g. made barrels). Passage to America was paid by Major Robert Beverley with a 7 year indenture serving as a Co
2 CONC oper in Middlesex County, Va..  Virginia in the mid to late 1600s offered quick wealth to only a few. Ninety percent held small holdings of 50 to a few hundred acres. The settlers in Virginia worked hard clearing forests for their
2 CONC  tobacco crops. Tobacco was legal tender in Virginia. The great staple was Indian corn and was used for batter or spoon bread or cone pone and was served for breakfast with butter or molasses. Irish and sweet potatoes and peas, es
2 CONC pecially black-eyed peas were grown by everyone. Hogs ran wild as did chickens. Most of the settlers had cattle and sheep and small orchards of apples, peaches, plums and figs. There was an abundance of wild game.   
2 CONT
2 CONT Settlers killed foxes, wolves, minks, polecats, bears and an occasional panther, partially for the safety of the homesteaders and to protect their livestock.   
2 CONT
2 CONT Indians avoided forts but fell upon isolated farms in a new clearing of settlement. The settlers felt the government was indifferent to their safety and well-being.   
2 CONT
2 CONT Their were families which could boast of armigerous families who had holdings of several hundred acres which brought in indentured workers, but these were inthe minority.   
2 CONT
2 CONT Of the indentures, no more than 15% were unskilled. Little more than 1% came from the gentry and most of those were brought as tutors and clerks or indentured to kinsmen already in the colony. Before 1676, a small number of crimin
2 CONC als were brought to Virginia but few survived. Indentures may have been as high a 75% of those who came to Virginia, but former indentures comprised no more than one third of the landholders, partly due to the appalling mortality 
2 CONC rate. Casualties were as high as 100,000 in the first 30 years, or 5 of every 6 adventurers. Some indentures ran away.   
2 CONT
2 CONT Freeholding families could rise rapidly in social position.   
2 CONT
2 CONT Many of our ancestors were in Virginia prior to 1700 but after 1624.
2 CONT
2 CONT * Ref: [http://www.myheritage.com/research/collection-1/myheritage-family-trees?itemId=196575801-5-674&action=showRecord MH Family Tree record]
2 CONT
2 CONT 2) Author: LibbieGriffin3, former genealogist for the Doss Family Association. http://boards.rootsweb.com/surnames.doss/1172.2/mb.ashx
2 CONT
2 CONT No one has ever proved the parents of John Doss -- we don't even have a good guess. "Ambrose Doss" is someone's creation. Beginning with the earliest known American ancestors, John Doss and his wife Ann Taylor were married and liv
2 CONC ed in Middlesex Co. Va. where early records still exist:
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Christ Church Parish Register, Middlesex Co., Va.''' (referred to here as "CCPR") lists marriage: "John Doss and Ann Taylor was Married the 26th of January 1679."
2 CONT
2 CONT CCPR: page 1, "John Dawd, Cooper & Servant to Major Robert Beverley Departed this Life 17th of Septemb[er] 1686." [Lots of Doss researchers (including me) think this is our John. I looked at the original and it clearly says DawD. 
2 CONC This death is recorded again on p. 31 but under the name of John DAVIS. When Davis is written longhand, it looks just like Daws, except for the dot on the i.]
2 CONT
2 CONT CCPR lists the marriage of Ann Doss and Thomas Winger on 15 July 1688, and the death of Ann Wingo (sic) on June 15, 1726. [The remarriage of Ann Doss less than two years after the death of "John Dawd" shown above serves to add wei
2 CONC ght to our belief that John Dawd was in fact our John Doss. The same researchers believe this is our Ann remarrying, and that this is a record of her death. The parish records tend to strengthen this argument, as Ann Wingo and Isa
2 CONC bel Herrin seemed to be neighbors, or even living in the same house; they may have both been living with John Doss the younger when they died (see his record).]
2 CONT
2 CONT As for who married Sarah Herring, the CCPR is very clear: "Jno. Doss and Sarah Herring married June 17th (Banns) 1735." ["Jno." is an old abbreviation of John] The birth of their daughter Mary was recorded in CCPR on 3 Jan. 1739/4
2 CONC 0.
