2 ADDR
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Sarah Petty Cathcart was the daughter of James & Martha Petty. Sarah's  estimated birthdate is 1749 to 1751, she  married Samuel Cathcart abt. 1766 a early settler of Ninety-Six District SC. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sarah's daughter Jane Cathcart McCravy was born the 17th of August 1767 as inscribed on her tombstone on find a grave.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sarah the wife of Samuel Cathcart died between 1810 & 1820. She appears on the 1790, 1800, 1810 Spartanburg SC census but is not mentioned in her husbands 1820 Will.
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel Cathcart mentions all of their children in his 1820 Will; sons James & John Cathcart and daughters Jane McCravy, Martha Clark, Sally Hindman, Nancy Foster, Ann Grice.
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel Cathcart and wife Sarah Petty were members of the Tyger River Baptist Church known today as the Friendship Baptist Church in present day Pauline, Spartanburg SC. The Church was organized in 1765 and was first known as "Jaco
2 CONC b Roberts Church" and traces its heritage to Fairforest Church and the Separate Baptist Missionaries of Shubal Stearns.
2 CONT
2 CONT James Petty the father of Sarah Petty Cathcart is mentioned as being a Carpenter and a large land owner, History of South Carolina Churches mentioned that in 1790 he was living in a house hold with eleven other people and had no s
2 CONC laves.
2 CONT
2 CONT James and Martha Petty the parents of Sarah Petty Cathcart came with a group of Virginians in the early 1650's and settled in the Piedmont region of the Waxhaws in North Carolina and built their homestead on Robinsons creek near H
2 CONC aw River tributary they were members of Haw Baptist Church founded by Shubal Stearns.
2 CONT
2 CONT About 1759 or 1760 Rev Mulkey led  a group of thirteen from Deep River in North Carolina to Broad River in South Carolina. They incorporated into a church in this place and their membership increased to one hundred. However the or
2 CONC iginal body remained only until Dec 1762 when they moved to Fairforest, a tract lying in the fork between Fairforest Creek and Tyger River...Three hundred families between Fairforest Creek were connected with the congregation by 1
2 CONC 772 p.p. 125-126
2 CONT
2 CONT James & Martha Petty were founding members of Pacolet Baptist Church in ninety six district now called Skull Shoals it was the sister church of Sandy River Baptist Church in Camden District The church was formed by Rev. Fowler and
2 CONC  Rev. Isaac Edward. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Shoal Skull Baptist Church in Gaffney is on the Pacolet River located in present day Cherokee and Union Counties was just a few miles from Grindal Shoals or ford where people and wagons could cross, During the early colonial era i
2 CONC t was an important area were the first colonists settled.. The Petty Cemetery is located in Gaffney
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel Cathcart birthdate estimate 1740-48 arrived in Old ninety six  district of SC in 1654 Source "The Scotch Irish and their first settlements on the Tyger River" 
2 CONT
2 CONT  In the Waxhaws the earliest settlements were established by the Scotch-Irish by 1653 in Camden district later known as Chester District where the early settlements of Rocky Creek and Fishing Creek were establish. In the 1760's a 
2 CONC group of Presbyterian Covenanters arrived by ship in Charleston from Ireland and in 1661 they established the Union Church of Rocky Creek that they called "Catholic".. Among these settlers were the Cathcart's .. The history of the
2 CONC  the early Union church established in Old ninety six in 1653 has been confused with the establishment of the Union church of the Covenanters in 1661 ... this historical detail is unknown to researchers and has caused great confus
2 CONC ion among them. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel Cathcart did not arrive in the Camden district via Charleston but arrived with his family to old Ninety Six in 1654 with  a group of other Scotch Irish emigrants from Pennsylvania  who had lived under the ministry of Rev Ca
2 CONC thcart, Source Early Scotch-Irish settlements on the Tyger River ..They built  their Church on Browns Creek near Tyger River in the wilds.  The town of Union was built around Old Presbyterian Church, it was a Presbyterian Church b
2 CONC ut it also used for Methodist, Episcopal, Baptist and Presbyterian Congregations in the area..
2 CONT
2 CONT Who was Rev Cathcart? He may have been Robert Cathcart a Licentiate from Londonderry North Ireland received by the Pennsylvania Synod in 1730. It is mentioned in his history that he  had a problem with the church government during
2 CONC  the Great Awakening that created the Schism from 1644 to 1658 in the governing body of the Synod. He may have decided to leave. His nephew Robert Cathcart from Ireland was called by the Pennsylvania Synod to minister to the Irish
2 CONC  Immigrants that had settled in York Pennsylvania.
2 CONT
2 CONT James & Sarah Cathcart's oldest daughter Jane married a widower Archibald McCravy who was quite a bit older than her.
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles Lavender the son of Simeon Lavender & Mary the daughter of Archibald McCravy and Frances Marie Hembree wrote his autobiography that included information about both of his grandfathers. He wrote this about his grandfather A
2 CONC rchibald McCravy, "He was on of the Virginia Blues under the command of George Washington. He was one of the few survivors at the defeat of general Braddock, on the 9th of June in 1756, then seventeen years of age "He was of Irish
2 CONC  descent and lived past the age of 94. According to one account , he came to America in the 1740's to York Pennsylvania. Another account  says he changed his name for McCreary to McCravy due to an error when he was in the service.
2 CONC  He had 11 children with each of his two wives.
2 CONT
2 CONT Archibald McCravy served in the Virginia Blues during the French Indian War, the site of George Washington's regiment headquarters was at Fort Loudon in present day Winchester Virginia in the Shenandoah Valley.
2 CONT
2 CONT Joshua Pettit was in the same area of Virginia as Archibald McCravy before moving to the Back Country. Joshua Pettit a mill owner lived on the Pacolet River in old ninety six and had some business with Samuel Cathcart perhaps bart
2 CONC ering
2 CONT
2 CONT  Joshua Pettit left New Jersey after the French Indian war had ended and after the road opened along the Ohio River making it easier to travel, he settled on South Branch in the Shenandoah Valley. He owned a lumber Mill and sold h
2 CONC is lumber for the construction of the new town Romney. He is found on a 1769 list of things that were sold by the widow of Dr. Hindman in Romney. McCravy who was living in the area may have served in the military with Richard Pear
2 CONC is who had a Trading Post at the Fort. Pearis came up with a scheme to purchase Cherokee land in the back country that was illegal and had to by pass government authority. He received legal assistance from Joshua Pettit who had wo
2 CONC rked in the courthouse in Elizabeth town New Jersey before moving to Deep Spring. Richard Pearis and and a group of men  rode out to the back country abt. 1768 to negotiate with the Cherokee, some of them returned Joshua Pettit is
2 CONC  recorded back in Virginia in 1769, and it is believed he was living in Old ninety six by 1770.
2 CONT
2 CONT Archibald McCravy qualified for the Savage land grant for compensation for military service during the French Indian War. This was British land along the Ohio River and lower Guyandotte and big Sandy Rivers.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Sandy River is a Tributary of the Dan River and Sandy Creek empty's into the Dan River.. Sandy Creek is were Shubal Stearns established his first Separatist Baptist Church; The Petty and Hembree families from Lunenburg Virgini
2 CONC a settled along these creeks of the Dan River  in North Carolina.  The Hembree's were member of  the Grassy Creek Baptist church and the Petty families were member of Haw Baptist Church both were associated Sandy Creek association
2 CONC  established by  Shubal Stearn's and both families eventually moved into the Back Country in South Carolina and were early members early churches established by Shubal Stearns missionary's.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is possible that Archibald McCravy received his compensation for his war service and went to North Carolina where he  built a homestead along one of the creeks that flowed into the Sandy River.. and most certainly he was a memb
2 CONC er of a church established by the missionaries of Sandy River association established by Shubal Stearns before moving to old ninety six where he owned a Mill.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cacapon Baptist Church in Romney, Hampshire, Virginia was established by missionary Rev Daniel Marshall the brother in law of Shubal Stearns. After the Braddocks defeat in 1755, of which young Archibald McCravy was one of the few 
2 CONC survivors, a group of families who were members of the church left Virginia and settled on along the the creeks in the neighbourhood of Sandy River in North Carolina and became members of the separate Baptist churches organized by
2 CONC  the missionaries of Shubal Stearns.  Shubal Stearns ordained several pastors and established the Sandy River association that was responsible for the establishment of several separatist churches in the Carolinas during the Great 
2 CONC Awakening.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1655 James Hembree signed a petition for the inhabitants of Grassy Creek a tributary of the Dan River. Grassy Creek Baptists Church was a member of Sandy River Association.  James Hembree a planter purchased land in the Back Co
2 CONC untry on Cains Creek North of Tyger River and was a member of Fairforest Church established by missionary Philip Mulkey 
2 CONT
2 CONT Joshua Pettit's daughter Hannah married Joel the son of James & Sarah  Hembree. Archibald McCravy married Frances Marie the daughter of James & Sarah Hembree . Benjamin  the son of Joshua Pettit married Elizabeth Hindman. John Hin
2 CONC dman married Sally the daughter of Samuel & Sarah Petty Cathcart. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Jane Cathcart the daughter Samuel & Sarah Petty Cathcart was the second wife of Archie McCravy a widower with 11 children.
2 CONT
2 CONT Why is the Pettit family bible attached to Archibald McCravy's pension papers???
2 CONT
2 CONT The Pettit family bible of Benjamin Pettit and Elizabeth Hindman is attached to Archibald McCravy Pension Papers (note) The complete NARA Revolutionary War record contains the answer why a portion of the Pettit Bible is attached t
2 CONC o McCravy's pension papers.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jane Cathcart married Archie McCravy and bore 11 children. Archie McCravy was a widower with 11 children who was twenty-five years older than his second wife.. Archie McCravy died in 1838.  Jane McCravy had to prove her connection
2 CONC  to Archie McCravy in order to receive a  widows pension evidently her marriage record /family bible had been lost or destroyed. 
2 CONT
2 CONT She remembered a baby of Ben Pettit and wife Elizabeth Hindman the sister of her brother in law was delivered on the exact same night she had delivered her baby and by the same midwife. The midwife testified in court the date when
2 CONC  she delivered the babies on the same night.  In order to substantiate the claim, Jane McCravy asked the Ben & Elizabeth Pettit to submit their Bible record that recorded the date of birth of the child mentioned by the midwife and
2 CONC  were assured they would be given the Bible record back.
2 CONT * Reference: [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/236561164/sarah-cathcart Find A Grave Memorial] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Jul 22 2023, 18:09:51 UTC''
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/236561164/sarah-cathcart
2 CONT ----
