1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Obituary from the Tennessean:
2 CONT
2 CONT "AUNT NANCY" BOZE DIES AT AGE OF 101 AT OLD HOMESTEAD
2 CONT
2 CONT CARTHAGE, Tenn. June 25 -- (Special)- Mrs. Nancy Boze, familiarly known as "Aunt Nancy," died yesterday at her home three miles from this place on the Gordonville road. She had the distinction of being the oldest person in the cou
2 CONC nty or any where in this section of the state, being 101 years of age next October if she had lived. She was the wife of Elijah Boze of this county, who died some thirty years ago.
2 CONT
2 CONT There are many interesting things in connection with the remarkable history of this old lady. Her father fought under General Jackson at the Battle of New Orleans, and she, herself, was born on the thirteenth day of October of tha
2 CONC t year. She was buried in the same place where she was born and had lived all of her life. She was much of a home body, never having been beyond the confines of her own county, nor farther from home than Rome, some seven miles fro
2 CONC m this place. The house in which she died was the third in which she had ever lived.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Aunt Nancy reached her one hundredth milestone last October a great family reunion was held at her home, where there gathered hundreds of relatives and friends, and a great dinner was spread in her honor. She was the mother o
2 CONC f nine children, and there are now living of her progeny, three children, forty-seven grandchildren, eighty-five great-grandchildren, and nine great-great-grandchildren. Her oldest son, Capt. W.C. Boze, now living near Elmwood, in
2 CONC  this county, is 74 years old, and was a gallant Confederate soldier throughout the late war, being wounded in the fighting at Gettysburg, and no man is more highly esteemed than he. Her grandson, Dr. Floyd Boze, is at present cou
2 CONC nty health officer for this county.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Boze was remarkable for her energy, good health and strong mind up to a comparatively short time before her death. She died of no particular disease, but seemed to have just "worn out." Her services were conducted by Rev. J.L
2 CONC . Smotherman, of this city, in the presence of a large gathering of friends, relatives, and neighbors.
