1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Nathaniel and Margaret were early settlers of Jennings Co., IN. Nathaniel was in Franklin Co., IN. in 1814 when he signed a petition to General Thomas Posey, Governor over the Indiana Territory, to extend mercy tow
2 CONC ard Elliott Herdon, and by 1815 he was in Jefferson Co., IN. where he owed taxes to William Vawter. He filed for land in Clark Co., IN. in 1816. When census was taken in 1820 in Jennings Co., IN., that was were he was and where he
2 CONC  remained until about 1837 when he sold his land to his oldest son, William, and He and Margaret left for Illinois. He and Margaret are said to have died in Illinois, I think his son Daniel moved to Illinois around this same time,
2 CONC  but Daniel and his family came back to Jennings Co., IN. According to Addison Davis { he was son of James Davis, who may be Nathaniel's brother} obit, The Davis's came to Jennings County in 1820 , at which early date they were co
2 CONC mpelled to cut the road all the way from Vernon to their home on Brush Creek, and it took them nearly a week to travel that distance. James, Nathaniel and Phanuel Davis and Charles all lived on Brush Creek and were members of Brus
2 CONC h Creek Baptist Church. { Don't know if Phanuel was a member} The Brush Creek Baptist Church was constitued July 1836 at a log school house. The church met once a month at said school house until about 1841, when they began to hol
2 CONC d meetings in a new log house erected as a church. The first church stood where the cemetery now is. Jess Grindstone told a story of the old log church. Stoves were not avaible and the men folks fell logs, burned them nearby, and 
2 CONC the coals were gathered up in iron receptacles and carried into the church and this crude method sufficed for heating purposes. In 1849 the church was burned by vandels. They held their meetings at houses. Man of the church and Co
2 CONC mmunity volunteered to build a new bulding. It was first occupied in May 1860, but was not completed until two or three years later. Although the church was in a hilly , rough section of woodland, it was noted center of spiritual 
2 CONC life for several decades. During the Civil War the congregations were especially large. A few years after 1900 the church was a disbanded organization. The one big cause was this- in the relocation of the main highways, The Brush 
2 CONC Creek Church was left off to one side, isolated,the main road which had passed it's door was abandoned above the church.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1820 census, Jennings Co., IN.:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1830 Jennings Co., IN. :
2 CONT
2 CONT 1840 census:
2 CONT
2 CONT Land Survey 26 Mar 1806, Livingston Co., KY -- “Nathan Davis” -- 200 acres, Book 10 (The Kentucky Land Grants, Vol I, Part 1, p 296, Willard Rouse Jillson, posted on Kentucky Land Grants, Ancestry.com database)
2 CONT
2 CONT 1820 US Census, Jennings Co., IN: “Nathaniel Davis”
2 CONT
2 CONT “1825 Jennings County Petition: To the Honorable Board of Justices of Jennings when met to do county business at that January Session 1825. We undersigned Petitioners Citizens of said county Humbly shewith to your honor that it wo
2 CONC uld promote the convenience of your Petitioners if you would set off a new Township containing the following Bounds to Wit. beginning at the corner of Sections 14-15-22 & 23 in Town 7 of Range 8 thence east one mile thence on a st
2 CONC raight line to the South west qr of Section 33 in Town 7 Rang 9 thence East with Township line to the Ripley County line thence along said county line to the Columbia Township line and along the same to the Geneva Township line an
2 CONC d along the same to the place of Beginning and as in Duty Bound will ever pray. William Davis, Nathaniel Davis, Joshua Davis Jr., Joshua Davis Sr., Daniel Davis, Phanuel Davis, James Davis, John Davis, Walter Davis. Indiana Source
2 CONC  Book - Material from The Hoosier Genealogist, 1985-1988, Vol 6. Compiled by Ruth Dorrel.” (Cathy Cadd, Davis Genforum database, #22184, 29 Aug 2001)
2 CONT
2 CONT 1830 US Census, Campbell, Jennings Co., IN: “Nathaniel Davis”
2 CONT
2 CONT Nathaniel Davis, name next to Phanuel, three lines from Charles -- 1830 Indiana Census, Jennings Co., Campbell Township, Roll 21, p 144 (photocopy found in Ancestry.com database)
2 CONT
2 CONT 1840 US Census, Winchester, Scott Co., IL: “Nathaniel Davis”
2 CONT
2 CONT "This excerpt from a family Bible owned by Janeen Proctor: 'The history of the Davis family has been passed down from father to son for several generations. In order to insure that this history does not get lost over time, I put t
2 CONC o paper, as a gift to my children and grandchildren, a small piece of this history.' - Easter E. Davis May 10, 1890. (Easter was married to Charles and Sally's grandson). . .Nathaniel and Margaret were early settlers of Jennings C
2 CONC o., IN. Nathaniel was in Franklin Co., IN in 1814 when he signed a petition to General Thomas Posey, Governor over the Indiana Territory, to extend mercy toward Elliott Herdon, and by 1815 he was in Jefferson Co., IN, where he owe
2 CONC d taxes to William Vawter. He filed for land in Clark Co., IN, in 1816. When census was taken in 1820 in Jennings Co., IN, that was where he was and where he remained until about 1837 when he sold his land to his oldest son, Willi
2 CONC am, and he and Margaret left for Illinois. He and Margaret are said to have died in Illinois. . .According to Addison Davis [he was son of James Davis, who may be Nathaniel's brother] obit, the Davises came to Jennings County in 1
2 CONC 820, at which early date they were compelled to cut the road all the way from Vernon to their home on Brush Creek, and it took them nearly a week to travel that distance. James, Nathaniel and Phanuel Davis and Charles all lived on
2 CONC  Brush Creek and were members of Brush Creek Baptist Church. [Don't know if Phanuel was a member.]. . .Nathaniel sold his land in Jennings in 1837 and researchers believe he and Margaret went to Illinois and that Nathaniel died th
2 CONC ere in 1843 and Margaret in 1845. . .As for Nathaniel's wife Margaret, I also have her listed as Peggy in 'Early Landowners of Jennings Co., IN' by Maurice Holmes. The birthdates correspond but I have her birth place listed as Lex
2 CONC ington, Fayette Co., KY. I have their wedding listed as about 1800 in KY, and I have 13 children listed." (Shannon Miller, Davis Genforum database, #28202, 10 Sept 2002)
2 CONT
2 CONT "Jennings County land records showed Nathaniel (or at least 'N. Davis') owning a plot in Campbell Township, east of North Vernon and north of the branch of the Muscatatuck River which runs through there. That property adjoined Pha
2 CONC nuel's to the south. Nathaniel disappeared from the census record after 1830." (Michael W. R. Davis, Davis Genforum database, #20506, 13 June 2001)
2 CONT
2 CONT NOTE: I have no proof of the following parents being true. Phanuel is a Scottish name and I believe that they are of the line of Nathaniel and Elizabeth Davis. Nathaniel named his son Nathaniel, who went by “Nathan” most of the ti
2 CONC me.” Charles William is named after two of his brothers. We may find Phanuel’s name in his wife’s family if we can ever do a proper identification of her.
