2 TITL Rescue of Daniel Boone's daughter and Calloway girls. inspiration for fictional "Last of the Mohicans
2 NOTE Boonesborough July 21, 1776 Col. Floyds letter reads. The same day hostel native took out of a canoe within sight of this place miss Betsy Callaway her sister Frances, and Jemima Boone, a daughter of Daniel Boone. The two last are
3 CONC  about 13 0r 14 years old and the other one grown. The affair happened late in the afternoon. They left the canoe on the opposite side of the river from us, which prevented our getting over for some time to pursue them. We could n
3 CONC ot that night follow more than five miles before dark. Next morning by daylight we were on the tracks but found they had totally prevented our following them by walking some distance apart through the thickest cane they could find
3 CONC . We observed their course and on which side we had left their signs, and traveled upward of thirty miles. We then imagined they would be less cautious in traveling, and made a turn in order to cross their trace, and had gone but 
3 CONC a few miles till we found their tracks in a buffalos path. We pursued and overtook them in going two miles, just as they were kindling a fire to cook . Our study had been more to get the prisoners without giving them time to murde
3 CONC r them after they discovered us than to kill them. we discovered each other nearly at the same time. Four of us fired and all rushed on them, which prevented their carrying anything away except one shotgun without ammo. Mr. Boone 
3 CONC and myself had each a pretty fair shot just as they began to move off. I am well convinced i shot one through and the one he shot dropped his gun, mine had none. The place was very thick with cane, and being so much elated on reco
3 CONC vering the three poor little heartbroken girls, prevented our making any further search. We sent them off almost naked, some without their moccasins, and not one of them so much as a knife or tomahawk. After the girls came to them
3 CONC selves enough to speak, they told us there were only 5 native four was Shawnees, and one Cherokee, and could All speak good English. They said they should take them to the Shawnee, towns and the war club we got was like those i ha
3 CONC ve seen from that nation.
2 NOTE From the year 1755 to the battle of New Orleans, your Paternal ancestors
3 CONT unsheathed the Sword and poured out their blood for this Country. The "Last
3 CONT of the Mohicans" you will remember in your grand uncle Nathaniel Floyd,
3 CONT whose old age did not deter him from service at New Orleans. You recollect
3 CONT the utter destitution of that old age.
3 CONT
3 CONT found in letter from 4th great grandma wife of Gov. John Floyd...
3 CONT written Feb 22' 1 843
3 CONT
3 CONT Perhaps Rush Floyd grand uncle Mrs. Floyd refers to was Nathaniel Floyd, 1767-1842. He
3 CONT would have been 47 years old if he had fought at the battle of New Orleans in 1814. What this
3 CONT man has to do with the "Last of the Mohicans" ? is obscure, other than perhaps to Mrs. Floyd (Grandma) he
3 CONT embodied the last of a breed of her husbands fathers line
