2 CONC o see the full information.&amp;lt;br&amp;gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;Additional information: LifeSketch:Member of the Virginia House of Burgess 1769, voting to resist the &quot;Stamp Act.&quot; as Signer of the Articles of the Mercant
2 CONC ile Association 9/12/1769. In approval of his resistance to the policy of the Crown he was unanimously reelected. to the House of Burgesses. He was a member of the first Revolutionary Convention held in Virginia that elected deleg
2 CONC ates to the Continental Congress. He was chairman of the first Amherst County Committee, preparing for Virginia&#039;s participation in the Revolution, 1775 he was elected to the Committee of Safety convening near Williamsburg. Ag
2 CONC ain elected to Burgess 11-1775 and voted to send a delegate to the General Congress to declare the United States Colonies free and independent states. He was a member of Propositions and Grievances. He was appointed a trustee for 
2 CONC the erection offurnaces and functions of war. He was elected a State Senator to the first Virginia Assembly to which state Patrick Henry had been elected Governor in the first organized state of America. Colonel William Cabell was
2 CONC  a most active man in Virginia in raising funds, recruiting and equipping the army. 1776 he was a member of the Virginia Assembly which ratified the Treaty made between the United Colonies and France. He was engaged in the busines
2 CONC s of war as Virginia&#039;s representative throughout the Revolution. After conspicuous service as a civilian, statesman and patriot his last public trustto which he was unanimously elected, was that of presidential elector. He jo
2 CONC yed in casting his vote for General George Washington for President of the United States having seen his county rise superior to the storm having seen Washington in command of &quot;The Ship of State&quot; he could retire to the w
2 CONC alk of a private citizen Up until the time of his death he devoted himself to the civic betterment of his county, state, and nation. Colonel William Cabell&#039;s title of Colonel was given by the colony of Virginia. As Colonel of
2 CONC  the Militia. His son also a Colonel of militia entered the service of Virginia in the Revolution as will be seen by reference to Heitman&#039;s Officers of Historical Register, Officer of the Continental Army 1776-1783 2nd editio
2 CONC n page 139. Major Virginia Militia at Yorktown in 1781 died 1822.&amp; Their Kin  Record 40001:1057726976: 
