1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Captain Obadiah Woodson'''
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/202806319/obadiah-woodson Find A Grave Memorial ID # 202806319]
2 CONT
2 CONT Obadiah Woodson born about 1712, died 21 April 1767
2 CONT Father: Richard Woodson (born about 1662, died 1730)
2 CONT Mother: Elizabeth Ann Smith (born about 1670, died 14 April 1746)
2 CONT
2 CONT Family: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Constance Watkins, wife, (born UNKNOWN, died 1773)
2 CONT
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT
2 CONT * David Woodson (born about 1735)
2 CONT * Judith Woodson (born about 1743)
2 CONT * Charles Woodson (born about 1745)
2 CONT * Jacob Woodson+ (born 11 May 1748, died 5 December 1839)
2 CONT
2 CONT '''NOTE:''' He died before the beginning of the American Revolution
2 CONT
2 CONT Facts and Notes:
2 CONT
2 CONT Title: Captain1,4
2 CONT Birth: About 1712; Henrico County, Virginia
2 CONT Marriage: 1734; Constance Watkins; Prince Edward County, Virginia,
2 CONT Residence: About 1750; Appomattox County, Virginia; The Woodson-Biglow Home "Happy Valley" was built by Captain Obadiah Woodson in about 1750.4
2 CONT
2 CONT Anecdote: 1755; Virginia; See person note1
2 CONT Military: Between 1757 and 1758; Virginia; He was a captain of a Prince Edward County volunteer militia company during the French and Indian War, participating in several expeditions against the Shawnee Indians from 1757-1758. A l
2 CONC etter addessed to Captain Woodson by Governor Dinwiddie is in the Note section 4,1
2 CONT Will: 9 September 1766; Will signed on 9 September 1766; Will proved on 21 November 17671
2 CONT Death: 21 April 1767; Prince Edward County, Virginia; Age: 553,4
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: Henry Morton Woodson, in his book the Historical Genealogy of the Woodsons and Their Connections, included the transcription of a letter written to Obadiah Woodson from Governor Dinwiddie'' (the letter part of the Dinwiddie 
2 CONC Papers in the Virginia Historical Collection):''
2 CONT
2 CONT "Governor Dinwiddie to Captain Obadiah Woodson.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nov'r 20th, 1755.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir,
2 CONT
2 CONT I rec'd y'rs of the 13th y's Day, I am sorry for the Disappiont't in not having men sufficient to undertake w't you propos'd ag'st the Shawnees, and of the Disappointm't of the Cherokees. I am afraid I was imposed on by Pearis, th
2 CONC eir interpreter, and I hear the Shawnees have gone to the So'w'd to join the Creeks, and have left y'r Town, but y's wants confirmat'n. Under these disappoitm'ts, I am of the opinion you had better ret'n, and if any thing can be d
2 CONC one in Feb'ry or March we may then endeav'r to prosecute w't you formerly propos'd, and if you are not near Capt. Hogg, I suppose the Inhabit'ts will take care of the Fort built by Capt'n Smith. Sam'l Overton proposes to go at the
2 CONC  above time on the same project if hereafter it sh'd be tho't eligible. I am glad no mischief has been lately done to the Ind's, and I hope the Inhabit'ts will return to y'r plantat's, as they have Forts to retire to on occasion o
2 CONC r in any Distress.
2 CONT
2 CONT I doubt not of y'r frugal care of the money you had, and I hope I will suffice till you retu'n.
2 CONT
2 CONT I am, with respect,
2 CONT
2 CONT S'r Y'r most h'ble Serv't,"
2 CONT
2 CONT Obadiah (the son of Richard) married Constance Watkins, daughter of John Watkins, the traymaker.
2 CONT https://archive.org/details/jstor-1915487/page/n5/mode/1up?view=theater&q=thomas+anderson page 55
2 CONT
2 CONT Obadiah Woodson is an ancestor of Hillary Rodham Clinton also.
