1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Nephew of the celebrated Hugenot preacher, Saurin. 
2 CONT James Saurin was a minister of the French Church of the Savoy in the Strand, London, in the year 1703. 
2 CONT A prayer book, with this note in French inscribed on a blank page: M. Saurin, minister, has sent this Book of Common Prayer to his nephew Abraham Michaux in Virginia, was presented in 1857, by H. F. Cabell, Esq., through Bishop Me
2 CONC ade, to the Theological Seminary at Alexandria, Virginia ‎(Cabells and their Kin, p. 272)‎.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''ABRAHAM MICHAUX''' (1672-1717), a native of Sedan, France, was a Huguenot refugee who fled to Amsterdam where he married July 13, 1692, Susanne Rochet, daughter of Moise Rochet, or de la Roche. Moise Rochet, also a Huguenot, wa
2 CONC s the father of four daughters of whom Susanne was the youngest. 
2 CONT He was endeavoring to get them out of France and into Holland by those secret agents who bravely ventured the hazardous business of aiding the escape of the Huguenots. 
2 CONT The first attempt failed and all four daughters were seized and imprisoned but were later returned to their father. 
2 CONT The three elder daughters made a second attempt, this time leaving Susanne with their father, and this time they succeeded in reaching Holland. 
2 CONT Fearing that letters would be intercepted they wrote their father to send the “little nightcap” they left behind, using this phrase to designate their younger sister Susanne, then 15 years of age. 
2 CONT Susanne was placed in a hogshead marked as merchandise and carried on board a trading vessel bound for Holland, she was removed from the hogshead when the vessel got fairly out to sea and reached her sisters in safety. 
2 CONT Ever afterward she was known as “The Little Night Cap”. 
2 CONT This story has been frequently told and perhaps has lost its savor with those already familiar with it, however, it is repeated for those who may not have heard it.  
2 CONT Abraham Michaux and his wife, Susanne, together with four children left Holland in 1701 and went to England; thence they emigrated to Virginia and settled at Manakintown. 
2 CONT His will was probated August 5, 1717 in Henrico County and that of his wife December 17, 1744 in Goochland County. 
2 CONT '''They had issue of thirteen children, the first of whom were born at Amsterdam, the last in Virginia.''' 
2 CONT * (1) Anne Michaux https://www.geni.com/people/Ann-Michaux/6000000183909510933?through=6000000002228250950
2 CONT * (2) Jane Magdelen Michaux https://www.geni.com/people/Jane-LeGrand/6000000004404260074?through=6000000000654787194, bp. 1697; m. Peter Le Grand.  
2 CONT * (3) Isaac Michaux https://www.geni.com/people/Isaac-Michaux/6000000015539455226?through=6000000000654787194, bp 1699.  
2 CONT * (4) James Michaux https://www.geni.com/people/James-Michaux/6000000183906700841?through=6000000000654787194, bp 1700.  
2 CONT * (5) Jacob Michaux https://www.geni.com/people/Jacob-Michaux/6000000004169842647?through=6000000000654787194, m. Judith Woodson.  
2 CONT * (6) Susanne Michaux https://www.geni.com/people/Susanne-Quin/6000000183905735882?through=6000000000654787194, m. John Quin.  
2 CONT * (7) John Michaux https://www.geni.com/people/John-Michaux/6000000183906708874.  
2 CONT * (8) Abraham Michaux https://www.geni.com/people/Abraham-Michaux/6000000175975496052?through=6000000000654787194, d. unm.  
2 CONT * (9) Olive Judi Michaux https://www.geni.com/people/Olive-Morgan/6000000004056162289?through=6000000000654787194, m. Anthony Morgan.  
2 CONT * (10) John Paul Michaux https://www.geni.com/people/John-Michaux/6000000015539452745?through=6000000000654787194, m. Judith Wilmore.  
2 CONT * (11) Elizabeth Michaux https://www.geni.com/people/Elizabeth-Woodson/6000000183906035850?through=6000000000654787194, m. Sanburn Woodson.  
2 CONT * (12) Ester Mary Michaux https://www.geni.com/people/Ester-Cunningham/334903694300005536?through=6000000000654787194, m. Alexander Cunningham
2 CONT * (13) Annmadlin Michaux https://www.geni.com/people/Anne-Woodson/6000000002228250950?through=6000000000654787194 b. April 1693; m. Richard Woodson  
2 CONT
2 CONT "Agnes Michaux, m. Richard Woodson" is NOT a child of this couple. The Agnes Woodson who married Joseph Morton was the sister, not daughter, of Richard Woodson of “Poplar Hill”. '''There was no Agnes Michaux.''' 
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ABRAHAM MICHAUX AND WIFE SUSANNE ROCHETTE (P. 83)
2 CONT
2 CONT On page 34 of '''“The Woodsons and Their Connections”''', this story is given. 
2 CONT
2 CONT “During the religious persecution consequent upon the revocation of the Edict of Nantes, which was signed by Louis XIV, of France, on October 18, 1685, and published four days afterward, Susanne Rochette lived in Sedan, France.
2 CONT When about fifteen years of age she escaped to Amsterdam where she married Abraham Michaux, a Huguenot, and continued in Holland until she had six children. 
2 CONT They immigrated to Virginia and settled in Manikin Town in Goochland County early in the eighteenth century. Abraham Michaux was a nephew of the celebrated Mr. James Saurin, minister of the French Church of the Savoy in the Strand
2 CONC , London, in the year 1703. 
2 CONT A prayer book, with this note in French inscribed on a blank page; ‘M. Saurin, a minister, has sent this book of Common Prayer to his nephew Abraham Michaux in Virginia. ‘was presented on 1857, by H. F. Cabell, Esq., through Bisho
2 CONC p Meade, to the Theological Seminary at Alexandria, Virginia. (Cabells and their Kin, p. 272.)  “Abraham Michaux was born in Sedan, France in 1672 and died in Virginia in 1717. 
2 CONT He was an ‘inscribed’ member of the French Huguenot Church in Amsterdam, Holland, January 28, 1691. There he married Susanne Rochette on July 13, 1692. 
2 CONT “Their children were baptized as follows: Anne, May 7, 1693; Jane, January 3, 1697; Jacob, August 15, 1700. (See Archives of Holland Church.) 
2 CONT On May 8, 1701, they left Amsterdam for London, England, and from there came to Virginia, probably the same year or in 1702. 
2 CONT His uncle Rev. James Saurin, sent him the above-mentioned prayer book in 1703. 
2 CONT Abraham Michaux died in 1717 and his wife, Susanne, died in 1744. 
2 CONT
