1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Son of Abraham Huddleston and Mary "Polly" Allen Huddleston. 
2 CONT He married Elizabeth Humphreys on August 25,1839 in Lawrence County, Arkansas. 
2 CONT They had three children together. They were 
2 CONT # Lucy Jane(Hurst,Palmer), 
2 CONT # Jesse, and 
2 CONT # Mary Angeline.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Rodicey Jane Darby on June 2, 1847 in Oregon County, Missouri. 
2 CONT They had at least seven children together. 
2 CONT A newspaper article says he had 16 children. 
2 CONT Documented children were 
2 CONT # James William, 
2 CONT # Abraham Martin, 
2 CONT # Nancy C.(Carter,Dotson), 
2 CONT # John R., 
2 CONT # Rufus McClellan, 
2 CONT # Amanda(Moore), 
2 CONT # Tennessee, and 
2 CONT # H. Lewis .
2 CONT
2 CONT Benjamin is possibly buried at Union Hill Cemetery where Rodicey is  buried.
2 CONT * Reference: [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/191319813/benjamin-a-huddleston Find A Grave Memorial] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Mar 21 2023, 0:31:45 UTC''
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/191319813/benjamin-a-huddleston
2 CONT ----
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Huddleston-1816
2 CONT Husband of Elizabeth (Humphreys) Huddleston — married 25 Aug 1839 in Lawrence, Arkansas, United States
2 CONT Husband of Rodica Jane Darby — married 2 Jun 1847 in Moore Township, Oregon, Missouri, United States
2 CONT Father of 
2 CONT # Lucy Jane Huddleston, 
2 CONT # Jesse Huddleston, 
2 CONT # Mary Angeline Huddleston, 
2 CONT # James William Huddleston, 
2 CONT # Abraham Martin Huddleston, 
2 CONT # Nancy C. Huddleston, 
2 CONT # John R. Huddleston, 
2 CONT # Rufus McClellan Huddleston,
2 CONT # Amanda Huddleston, 
2 CONT # Lewis Huddleston and 
2 CONT # Tennessee Huddleston
2 CONT ----
2 CONT Bio:
2 CONT Benjamin was born in 1817 in Smith County (now Macon), TN, the youngest son of Abraham Huddleston & Mary Allen.
2 CONT His father, Abraham had moved to Smith Co, Tn about 1794, probably with his brother Henry and sister Polly Huddleston. About 1818/19 Abraham's moved to Pope Co, Illinois where they were in the 1820 census.
2 CONT 1820 IL, Pope Co, Alexander twp,Hudelston, Abram - 312001 - 0001. . . . Abraham - over 45. . . . woman - over 45 [Polly]. . . . 2 boys 16-18 [John-18, Israel-17]. . . . 1 boy 10-15 [Nathan-15, William-13, Abraham-11]. . . . 3 boys
2 CONC  0-10 [Marshall-9, Jesse-6, Benjamin-3]
2 CONT Oct. 6, 1821 Abraham and all his children (except John who came later) settled in what is now Thomasville, Oregon Co., Mo. They were some of the first white families to settle in that county.
2 CONT He married 1st to Elizabeth Humphreys, Aug 25, 1839, Lawrence County, Ark. She was the daughter of James & Drucilla Humphreys.
2 CONT In 1840 he was in Ripley Co., Mo., p 336:Benjamin Huddleston 00001 - 000011 male - 20-30 [Benjamin-23]1 female - 20-30 [Elizabeth-20]
2 CONT Ben & Elizabeth had 3 children:
2 CONT  Lucy Jane, b 1840 MO; m1 Peter Hurst, 1856/8; he d 1860; m2 John M. Palmer, 1860, 1 ch; he d Civil War; m3 John's brother, Thomas Jefferson "Tom" Palmer, 6 ch; she d abt 1843. Jesse, b 1841; m Georgia Ann Gilliam, 1872; d 1925 Ma
2 CONC ry Angeline, b 1845; m Thomas Wright, 8 ch; d 1909, Scott Co, MOElizabeth died before 1847
2 CONT Ben married 2nd Rodica Jane Darby, June 2, 1847, Oregon County, MO
2 CONT Ben & Rodicy had 7 children:
2 CONT James William, b abt 1850; m Mary Ellen Stapleton; d 1934, Sand Springs, OK 
2 CONT Abraham Martin, b 1853; m Martha Medora "Dora" Morse, 1874, Calloway Co, KY; d 1947, Gamaliel, Baxter Co, Ark. Nancy C., b abt 1859; m ? Carter abt 1878; 1 ch: John; m 2nd Rochard Oscar Dotson, 1885; 2 ch: Oscar, Arthur; John Ar.,
2 CONC  b abt 1861; m Elizabeth Roberts Rufus McClellan, b 1865; m Elizabeth Dora Catherine "Katie" Dotson, 11 ch; d 1948 Amanda, b 1868; m John Robert Moore, 1883, 6 ch; d bef 1902. Tennessee Lewis, b 1870 (maybe the same person); m Cla
2 CONC ra ?1850-1900 censuses they were in Oregon Co., Mo (at same location)1850 Oregon Co, Mo census: HUDDLESTON, Benjamin A. - 33 M TN Farmer. . . . Rodicy - 22 F Alabama. . . . Lucy - 9 F MO. . . . Jessie - [8] M MO. . . . Mary A. - 4
2 CONC  F MO
2 CONT Jan 3, 1856 Ben bought 55 acres.
2 CONT Oregon County, Missouri Deed Abstracts 1845-1868, Benjamin A. and Rodicey Huddleston of Oregon Co. to George Halfacre of thesame place 13 Aug 1859 $400 NE 1/4 and NE 1/4 of SE 1/4 Sect 10 T24N 200acres. signed Benjamin A Huddlesto
2 CONC n, Rodicy Huddleston in presence of:Alpha O. Bellaha, GW Thomas, JW Griffith, clerk, by BF Stewart, DC filed 13Aug 1859 Rec: 9 Jan 1860 MG Norman, clerk.
2 CONT 1860 Oregon Co census, p 206Huddleston, B. A. - 43 Tn. . . . . Rhodicy - 32 Tn. . . . . Jesse - 18 Mo. . . . . Mary - 14 Mo. . . . . William - 8 Mo. . . . . Abraham - 5 Mo. . . . . Nancy - 10/12 MoHurst, Lucy - 20 Mo [widowed daug
2 CONC hter]
2 CONT 1870 MO census: Oregon Co, Jobe twp, PO Alton, p 39 (orig 76)Huddleston, B.A. - 54, Tenn. . . . . Rhodiny - 43, Ala. . . . . William - 17, Mo. . . . . Abram - 15, Mo. . . . . Nancy - 11, Mo. . . . . John - 9, Mo. . . . . Rufus - 6
2 CONC , Mo. . . . . Manda - 3, Mo. . . . . Tennessee - 4/12, Mo
2 CONT 1880 Oregon County census, p10 Moore Township (around Thomasville)HUDDLESTON, Benjamin - Farmer m M 63 TN TN VA. . . . . Rodica - Wife, KpHse m F 52 AL SC AL. . . . . Rufus R. - Son, wrks on farm s M 15 MO TN AL. . . . . Lewis - S
2 CONC on M 10 MO TN AL. . . . . Manda - Dau F 12 MO TN AL. . . . . Nancy C. - wrks at home F 20 MO TN ALCARTER, John - Grandson M 1 MO MO MO [son of Nancy]
2 CONT In 1900 he was living with son Rufus in Oregon Co, Moore twp:Huddleston, Benjamin, father, born Feb 1817, TN, age 83, widowed, father born Maryland, mother VA
2 CONT The South Missourian-Democrat newspaper, Oct 9, 1902:"Uncle Ben Huddleston, living near Thomasville, was in town Monday attending court. Uncle Ben will be 86 years old in Feb. 1903. He has lived in this county ever since he was 4 
2 CONC years old, making 82 years; that is, he lived in the same house when this county was part of Wayne, part of Ripley, and cut off and made Oregon. He has 16 children, 73 grandchildren and 33 great-granchildren, and looks like he mig
2 CONC ht live to see a generation or two of great-great-grandchildren."
2 CONT The South Missourian-Democrat newspaper (Alton, Oregon County), June 25, 1903, has this article about Ben:"Uncle Ben Huddleston, whilst not the oldest man living in the county, has lived in the county longer than any other person.
2 CONC  He lived in the same house first when this was Wayne County, second Ripley County, and then Oregon County in 1845. Having lived in the same house on the same ground in 3 different counties. He was born 10 Feb. 1817 in Smith count
2 CONC y, Tenn. His father settled here Oct 6, 1821 on Eleven Point River 3 miles below Thomasville. Mr. Huddleston is the father of 16 children, 5 boys and 3 girls are living. He has 74 grandchildren and 34 great-grandchildren. Uncle Be
2 CONC n is a man after the president's own heart. He has lived, multiplied and replenished the earth..."
2 CONT Benjamin died 1904, age 86 at his son Rufus' home in Oregon County, MO (He would have been 87 on his birthday, Feb 10) Unknown burial place.Find A Grave: Memorial #191319813
2 CONT The journal-Gazette (West Plains, Howell Co, MO), Thurs, Feb 4, 1904, pg 1The Oldest Resident Dead -- "Uncle" Ben Huddleston, who has resided in Southern Missouri for a longer period than any person known, died at the home of his 
2 CONC son, Rufus Huddleston near Thomasville last Thursday morning, at the advanced age of 87 years. He has lived on the same place for 83 years, having come to Southern Missouri in 1821.Although "Uncle" Ben Huddleson was not the oldest
2 CONC  man residing in this section, still he has lived in this section longer than any other person. He lived in the same house first when the county was known as Wayne county, then when that portion in which he resided was apportioned
2 CONC  off into Ripley county, and lastly when Oregon county was formed from a portion of Ripley county. He often remarked that he had lived in 3 different counties and had never moved a single time.Mr. Huddleston was born in Smith Coun
2 CONC ty, Tennessee. His father settled on Eleven Points River, 3 miles below Thomasville, October 6, 1821. At that time the country was a dense wilderness, over which elk, bear and thousands of deer roamed. Indians camped near his fath
2 CONC er's home and as a boy he went among them."Uncle" Ben Huddleston was the father of 16 children of whom 5 sons and 3 daughters are living He had 74 grandchildren and 34 great-grandchildren, and it can truthfully be said that he was
2 CONC  a man after President Roosevelt's own heart.The funeral was held at Thomasville Sunday, the entire community turning out to pay their last respect to the venerable pioneer who did much in bringing this country out of the wilderne
2 CONC ss.
2 CONT Though the above article sounds like he died the week before Feb. 4, this next article is clearer.
2 CONT West Plains newspaperLive on one Arm 83 yearsThe Death of an Oregon County man who had 16 childrenWest Plains, MO, Feb. 4 - Benjamin Huddleston died at his home on Eleven Points, in Oregon county today. He was 87 years old, and fo
2 CONC r 83 years lived on the farm where he died. He has resided in 3 counties without moving, first when the county was Wayne, then when Ripley was created, and lastly when Oregon was made. He was the father of 16 children and had 74 g
2 CONC randchildren and 34 great-grandchildren.
