1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Evidence of birth date & location, marriage date & location and spouse's information =
2 CONT [https://familysearch.org/ark:/61903/2:1:9M3S-FYC]  and [https://familysearch.org/ark:/61903/2:1:M9QZ-WCH]
2 CONT
2 CONT evidence of marriage [http://files.usgwarchives.net/va/fluvanna/vitals/marriages/marr7758.txt]
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth probably wasn't born ins 1764, it was a later date, but before her mother died in 1781. She didn't marry until 1797, so 1764 is unlikely.
2 CONT
2 CONT Possible 1840 census https://familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:XHBM-H2F If correct she was no more than 70 years old in 1840, so she would have been born after 1770
2 CONT * Reference: [https://www.myheritage.com/research/record-1-354476581-1-500020/elizabeth-marks-born-bragg-in-myheritage-family-trees MyHeritage Family Trees] - [http://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Dec 9 2016,
2 CONC  3:39:35 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT Comment from [https://www.geni.com/discussions/176664?msg=1193304]
2 CONT
2 CONT This may already be known, but Vincent Marks lived in St. John's parish in either Albemarle or Fluvanna County,( been a while since I read this). He was listed as a member of the Virginia Militia in 1812, briefly, as I'm fairly ce
2 CONC rtain was due to the British invasion of Washington, D.C. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He had a son named Austin Minor. A listing of the residents of St. John's parish included surnames including Austins and Min.ors. I do not know if they were related or just well thought of by Vincent and his wife Betsy Bragg. Vinc
2 CONC ent and Betsy were married in Fluvanna County. Thanks to all for your continuing research. I would love to know Vincent's father's name.
