2 CONT Name: Thomas B Venable
2 CONT Age: 68
2 CONT Birth Date: 9 Dec 1824
2 CONT Burial Date: 24 Jun 1893
2 CONT Cemetery: Elmwood
2 CONT Burial Place: Oxford, Granville, N C
2 CONT
2 CONT From the James Thomas Brown 1880 Testament.. – ‘John George Brown and his wife Rebecca Monteith Garden or Brown, payable by the testator as heir and representative of his deceased father under the couple’s antenuptial marriage con
2 CONC tract of 24 July 1843. Substantial legacies go to his sisters Isabella Alston Brown or Venable (of North Carolina) and Elizabeth Morton Brown, and to three nephews — Alexander Garden Brown, John George Garden Brown and James Rober
2 CONC t Brown, sons of John George Brown. The 1879 codicil bequeaths the ancient family sword by Andrew Ferrara, “with which my Ancestor of Auchlochan fought … for the cause of the Scottish Covenanters”, and the old Auchlochan family pa
2 CONC pers to his nephew Thomas Brown Venable’.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ' In the death of Col. Thomas Brown Venable, Granville county loses a son who possessed an iron will, a strong intellect, and a commanding place among his fellows. The history of his life would be of singular interest. For his ear
2 CONC thly existence covered the most exciting period of American history, and his opportunity for seeing the inner workings at Washington and Richmond were very great. Abram W. Venerable was the intimate friend and companion of Mr. Cal
2 CONC houn. He was present at his death bed. mourned at his funeral. To him the State of South Carolina, by special act, voted its debt of gratiude for love and kindness to her greatest son. He When war came on, Mr. Venable was chosen t
2 CONC o represent our District in the Confederate Congress. He became the confidential friend of President Davis. During all his young manhood, the boy Brown was the constant companion of his father. In Washington and Richmond the son w
2 CONC as on terms of intimacy with the constitutional lawyer Calhoun, with the truculent Wigfall, with Yancey and Toombs, and Hill and Benjamin. When North Carolina passed her ordinance of secession young Venable was designated as the m
2 CONC essenger to convey the same to authorized headquarters; which office those of us who knew him may well imagine he performed with great dignity and some outward show of ceremony. Just previous to the war young Venable visited his u
2 CONC ncle at his ancestral mansion in Scotland. There, during two months of delightful sojourn, he saw something of the life of Scottish noblemen. Right meirily did he dwell on the famous dinners at which Sir Colin Campbell, Mr. Peele,
2 CONC  Thomas Brown and other " auld acquaintances " were gathered.. This uncle, Thomas Brown, by will, devised that mansion and its broad acres to Col. Venable. But a few years before the death of Mr. Brown, the Bank of Glasgow, of whi
2 CONC ch he was a director and large stockholder broke, greatly shattering the fortune of Mr. Brown. In consequence he purchased an annuity of the Scotch government and gave to it all his property. Venable was a lawyer of great power. I
2 CONC n his palmy days the very ground trembled under his feet. His practice came not by favoriteism, but because his clientage thought him the strongest man at the bar. Col. Venable was born December 9, 1825. Col. ** Of him who has jus
2 CONC t departed, we may say that he had a kind heart, he was a devoted father and husband, he was firm in the faith of the fathers. What more can be said ** Col. Venable was a factor in politics. He made and he unmade. Yet with all his
2 CONC  firmness his eye often filled with the sympathetic tear. He was, during many years, a ruling elder in the Presbyterian church, of whose traditions no man was more proud. A beautiful sight it was, as his kind pastor, Rev. Ernest T
2 CONC hacker, together with Rev. T. U. Faucette and all the Elders and Deacons of the Presbyterian church and many other sorrowing hearts, followed him, at set of Sunday's sun, to his final resting place, where cool and sweet, we laid h
2 CONC im to rest to await the trump of the resurrection morn.'
2 CONT
