1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Birth:  Apr. 16, 1830 Charlotte County Virginia, USA  Death:  Oct. 31, 1916 Charlotte County Virginia, USA 
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard V. Gaines enlisted, September 23, 1861, at Charlotte Co., VA. At enlistment he was a Sgt. When mustered out he was a 2nd Lt. Served with Capt. Paris' Virginia Artillery; Staunton Hill Artillery. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard Gaines played a distinguished part in the War between the State, but an incident at its close will indicate his fiber better than anyone else. During the last year of the war, the Confederacy found it necessary to send age
2 CONC nts to Mexico with gold to buy mules. After the surrender one such agent in Gaines' territory reported to him as his superior and turned over $5,000 of the precious metal. Gaines' problem was to dispose of this in a manner satisfy
2 CONC ing to his conscience. It could not be returned to the Confederate Government because there was none. He could not keep it himself. In the end there seemed to him no other proper course except to return it to the United States Gov
2 CONC ernment.
2 CONT
2 CONT At that time the south was entirely without currency of any kind and gold was an unheard of miracle. Many uses for the money were suggested to him but he clung to his principle that since peace was made it was his duty to relinqui
2 CONC sh it to the Government of which the South was again a part.
2 CONT
2 CONT His plantation "Do-Well" was not spared a visit from the Union Troops. Though the silver, carefully buried, was saved, a spool bed had one of its posts sliced off and a wardrobe one of its doors slashed through. Most poignant of a
2 CONC ll, is the tale of Don Juan. A prized stallion, Don Juan was secreted in the basement. When two Union officers came to the front door, Don Juan emitted from below stairs what could only be described as a "whinney." The officers, t
2 CONC hough smiling, courteously departed. The aftermath is reported in the words of Miss Bessie Gaines, Richard Gaines daughter.
2 CONT
2 CONT "The next morning they (the officers) called again. I, a child of five, stood on the portico fascinated by their gaily caparisoned steeds. Pointing to the handsomer of the two horses, I said, 'If I had a pretty horse like that, I 
2 CONC would put him in the cellar with Don Juan.' It probably seemed a tempting of Providence to the soldiers to ignore two such clear revelations, and when the corps moved on, Don Juan followed in their train."
2 CONT
2 CONT The family however, had no regrets later when the newspapers reported the death of Don Juan as, rearing in a military parade in Chicago, burst a blood vessel in the course of unseating his distinguished rider -- no other than Gene
2 CONC ral Philip Sheridan.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.sovanow.com/index.php?/news/article/before_the_land_stand_custers_most_brazen_theft_finally_exposed/
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.smithsonianmag.com/history/when-custer-stole-thoroughbred-180956961/?no-ist
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard Gaines was a wealthy planter by occupation. 
2 CONT
