1 NOTE {geni:about_me} "John Edwards was a captain in the colonial militia and was with the First Virginia Regiment in the French and Indian War. His wife, Jane Arrington was a daughter of Thomas Arrington of Westmoreland county."
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT From [https://sites.google.com/site/kinggenealogy/%22thekingfamily%22bygoodekingfeldhausergeorgem.g.stafford The King Family" by Goode King Feldhauser/George M.G. Stafford]
2 CONT
2 CONT >John, Richard, William, and Thomas Edwards, grandsons of Richard of Wales, came to Virginia, and John, the eldest settled in Northumberland county.  His son, John Edwards, Jr., was a vestryman in Lancaster county, and then remove
2 CONC d to Surry county where he was a burgess.  He married Frances Cole and their son, William Edwards, member of the House of Burgesses in 1653, lived in Surry county and married Dorothy Withers.  Among their children was William Edwa
2 CONC rds, Jr., also a burgess from Surry, who married Ann [SIC: Elizabeth] Harrison, daughter of Col. Benjamin Harrison, the "Councillor" (progenitor of two Presidents of the United States), and they were the parents of John Edwards wh
2 CONC o married Jane Arrington. '''[SIC:  they were not.  See  [https://books.google.com/books?id=ACcjAQAAIAAJ&pg=PA82&lpg=PA82&dq=william+edwards+sarah+hilliard&source=bl&ots=fRil_lYEBz&sig=X_KMFajKCUiBA_HGqFxJIO59EkE&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0a
2 CONC hUKEwiTu-nzg7bNAhVK72MKHT3UCmcQ6AEINDAJ#v=onepage&q=william%20edwards%20sarah%20hilliard&f=false page 82] of The William and Mary Quarterly, Volume 15 By Earl Gregg Swem'''] This latter couple lived in Prince William county and la
2 CONC ter moved to Spotsylvania county. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: The above Feldhauser genealogy is baseless and probably wrong. Please see a critique here: https://archive.org/details/the-descendants-of-meredith-edwards-of-westmoreland-county-virginia/page/n1/mode/2up
2 CONT
2 CONT Also: John Edwards of Spotsylvania is the same John Edwards of King George. He died in 1743, not 1757. The John Edwards who died March 6, 1757 was from Haverhill, MA. Please see: https://edwardsvirginiagenealogy.blogspot.com/2021/
2 CONC 02/john-edwards-of-denbighshire-feb-8-1683.html
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT * [https://books.google.com/books?id=sc34AgAAQBAJ&pg=PA175&lpg=PA175&dq=John+Edwards+1683+Jane+Arrington&source=bl&ots=TQQFPd7b33&sig=pAwQtx6Tb7nRDvntaUfKTaJpWT4&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwi3xc_WubbNAhVH12MKHQh7DnwQ6AEINzAI#v=onepage&q
2 CONC =John%20Edwards%201683%20Jane%20Arrington&f=false page 175] of Coombs Family History: A surprising and exciting journey back in time
2 CONT * http://worldconnect.genealogy.rootsweb.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=tsmith&id=I59985
2 CONT * http://roots-and-boughs.com/Kathy's%20Lines/edwards.html
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT The Edwards Name Generally believed to be a Welsh family name, its use can be found in England 200 years before it appeared in Wales. The name is derived from the old English word Eadweard which means 'prosperity guard'. Prior to 
2 CONC the Norman conquest, Edward was a personal name with widespread use probably due to the fact that Edward the Confessor was the Patron Saint of England before Saint George. Many references to the name can be found in the Doomsday B
2 CONC ook including one 'Eaduuardus 1066'.
2 CONT
2 CONT Other early records show William Edward listed in the Curia Regis Rolls in Suffolk, England in 1219 and a Christina Edwardis recorded in Huntingdonshire, England in 1279.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Welsh use of hereditary family names started as late as the 16th century when the normal practice was to take the fathers name as the family name. Thus there became a large number of family names taken from the names of Evan, 
2 CONC Hugh, William, Davy and Edward.
