2 CONT
2 CONT Plantation owner Tidewater NC.  . His service record is given in the Roster of NC troops in Colonial Wars, NC Historical Commission file g.o.146 Dobbs Co. Commissioned Ensign in the Colonial Militia March 11,1761.  A lieutenant in
2 CONC  the French/Indian war. He and his family belonged to the Primitive Baptist Church.********
2 CONT
2 CONT born 1732 in Perquimana, North Carolina (Source: Winslow's History of Perquimans County, North Carolina. Land office Grant Book 25 page 136 and Book 32 page 147, Dublin County, North Carolina), and died 30 Aug 1780 in Sampson or D
2 CONC ublin, North Carolina. He married ARTHESHA (her last name is unknown.) She was born Abt. 1736, and died 1807. Notes for JETHRO OATES: Jethro was commissioned Lieutenant 11 Mar 1761, in Captain Lewis Piplin's Company and served unt
2 CONC il 1765. Jethro was Commissioned Ensign, 11 March 1761, Capt. Lew Perkin's Co., serving until 1765. He died 30 Aug. 1780. (Roster of N.C. Troops in Colonial Wars., N.C. Historical Commission, File G.O. 146 Dobbs County, N.C., Col.
2 CONC  Richard Caswell's Regiment). Was he a Lieutenant or an Ensign? - Dale
2 CONT
2 CONT History of Perquimans County, N.C., by Mrs. Winslow, p. 290:Petition: No date given, but from the context the date is between 1749 and 1753.Jethro Oates showeth that in the year 1738, John Wyatt in his will, left property to be di
2 CONC vided between the children of Elizabeth Oates, his daughter, and said Jethro Oates being one of them is now come of age and prays his part of the estate, now in the hands of William Wyatt and Thomas Pierce. Jethro Oates died Aug. 
2 CONC 30, 1780. His will was probated in the January Court 1781, Duplin County, N.C. Will Book A. p. 374-375.The Last Will and Testament of Jethro Oates, of Duplin County, North Carolina.Item, I give and bequeath to my eldest daughter L
2 CONC ydda Stevens, (slaves) Ben and Jena, five cows and yearlings, one feather bed and furniture to her and her heirs forever, having them in their possession.Item, I give and bequeath to my next eldest daughter Amy Oates King, (slaves
2 CONC ) Simon and Grace, seven cows and two 2 year old stears, one feather bead and furniture in her possession, to her and her heirs forever. I lend to my beloved wife Artesha Oates all the rest of my Estate Lands, tenaments, negroes, 
2 CONC goods, bed cloathes to her during her natural life or widowhood, not to be removed out of the county and afterwards to be equally divided amongst my six children hereunder named. Jesse Oates; Jethro Oates; Elizabeth Oates; Michael
2 CONC s Oates; Susannah Oates and John Oates, and to be equally divided by my surviving Executors, Artesha Oates; Stephen King; Roger Snell; Micha King.Signed, sealed and delivered in the presence of us the 30th day of August 1780. Witn
2 CONC ess James Oates                           Jethro Oates (seal)Duplin County, Jan. Term 1781
2 CONT
