1 NOTE !Revolutionary War Records Duplin and Sampson Counties, NC
2 CONT By Virginia L. Bizzell and Oscar M. Bizzell
2 CONT OATES, Jethro, Private, N.C. Militia
2 CONT soldier's name appears in Caswell's List as a participant in the Battle
2 CONT of Moore's Creek Bridge. Also, Oates received three day's pay for
2 CONT the hire of his Negroes and three horses that hauled provisions to Col.
2 CONT Caswell at Tatum's Bridge.
2 CONT Jethro Oates patented 600 acres of land on Young's Swamp and Goshen
2 CONT Swamp, along the Duplin/Sampson line, and later purchased other lands
2 CONT there. Jethro's wife was Artesha .
2 CONT They had the following children:
2 CONT 1- Lydia who married Barnabas Stevens.
2 CONT 2- Amy who married Stephen King.
2 CONT 3- Jesse who married and settled in Ky.
2 CONT 4- Jethro E. (1768-1821) who married 1st to Letitia Carraway and
2 CONT     2nd to Elizabeth Ivey.
2 CONT 5- John (1775-1826) who married Susan Cogdell.
2 CONT 6- Michael who settled in Mecklenburg County, N.C. And
2 CONT 7- Susannah (no info).
2 CONT Soldier died 30 Aug 1780.
2 CONT __________
2 CONT Two cousins listed in Revolutionary War Records below
2 CONT __________
2 CONT OATES, Samuel, Private, N.C. Malitia
2 CONT Soldier was born 4 Feb 1760 in Wayne county, N.C., to James and
2 CONT Sarah Fellow Oates. He lived in Sampson County, N.C., until 1798
2 CONT when he moved to Winyaw District, S.C.
2 CONT Soldier served under Capt. Michael King and Col. James Kenan of
2 CONT Duplin County and according to Caswell's Journal. "A", they
2 CONT participated in the Battle of Moore's Creek Bridge on 27 Feb 1776.
2 CONT He received two pounds for his 20 days of service.
2 CONT
2 CONT OATES, Stephenson, Private, N.C. Militia
2 CONT soldier was born 1764 in Wayne County, N .C ., "the year the woods
2 CONT burned" was written in the family bible. Later, Stephenson Oates wrote
2 CONT in this bible," I was bonded unto my Uncle Jethro Oates". The Jethro
2 CONT Oates family lived in Piney Grove Township, Sampson County.
2 CONT Stephenson enlisted and served in the Revolutionary War, according to a
2 CONT published family history.
2 CONT Stephenson married Ferebe Carraway and they moved to Moore County,
2 CONT N.C. after the War. In 1798, the family moved to Darlington County, S.C.
2 CONT where they reared a family of eleven children.
