1 NOTE {geni:about_me} A Patriot of the American Revolution for VIRGINIA.  [http://services.dar.org/Public/DAR_Research/search_adb/?action=full&p_id=A000542  DAR Ancestor # A000542]
2 CONT
2 CONT Possibly due to failing health, Robert Addams wrote his Will in Feb 1738. His estate was left to his wife Mourning, sons James and Robert and daughters Judith Clark, Mary Moreman [Moorman], Eliza Moreman [Moorman], Agnes Farguson 
2 CONC [Ferguson], Susanna, Lucy, Anna the younger and Sally.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT The last of eleven children born to Captain Robert Sr and Mourning (Lewis) Adams of Henrico Co (now Goochland) VA; James married Cecily Ford in 1751, a daughter of William Ford Sr.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children with Cecily:
2 CONT *  1) James Adams Jr (1753-1835), m: Jane Cunningham.
2 CONT *  2) William Adams (b: 1755)
2 CONT *  3) Robert Adams (b: 1757), m: Lucy Williams.
2 CONT *  4) Thomas Ford Adams (1758-1836), m: Sarah Ford.
2 CONT *  5) John Adams
2 CONT *  6) Nancy "Nance" Adams (b: 1763)
2 CONT *  7) Richard Adams, m: Susannah Dawson.
2 CONT *  8) Sarah "Sallie" Adams (b: 1768)
2 CONT *  9) Charles Adams, m: Mary Dawson.
2 CONT * 10) Milly Adams
2 CONT * 10) Mourning Adams (b: 1772), m: Jesse Pace.
2 CONT * 11) Susannah "Sulky" Adams
2 CONT * 
2 CONT
2 CONT During the Indian Wars, James served in the Virginia Militia Regulars, under the command of John Smith, taking part in the Cherokee Expedition in 1760. From 1771-1773, he served as Justice of the Peace in Albermarle Co VA. 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Justice of the Peace, Albemarle Co., VA 1771-1773 Was a militia soldier in John Smith Co., VA of Regulars. Part of the Cherokee Expedition of 1760 until end of that campaign.
2 CONT
