1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Charles Moorman was the grandson of Zachariah and son of Thomas. He became the most influential of the Quakers in Louisa County, Virginia where a considerable number of Quakers had purchased land.
2 CONT
2 CONT Married
2 CONT #  Elizabeth Reynolds. There is some dispute as to whether she was the daughter of Christopher Reynolds and Elizabeth, or the daughter of Thomas “Renalls”, the recorded baptism dated 15 of March 1690-1691. The Louisa County court 
2 CONC records simply identify Charles’ wife as Elizabeth. She died in 1763.
2 CONT
2 CONT   CHILDREN OF CHARLES MOORMAN & ELIZABETH (REYNOLDS)  
2 CONT # Thomas Moorman - born 04 Sep 1705; married Rachel (Clark), daughter of Christopher; died 10 Nov 1767.
2 CONT # Anne Moorman - born in 1708 in Hanover Co., Virginia.
2 CONT # Achilles Moorman - born 26 Oct 1713; married Elizabeth (Adams), daughter of Robert; died in Nov 1785.
2 CONT # Judith Moorman - born in 1714; married John Douglas.
2 CONT # Charles Moorman - born in 1715; married Mary (Adams), daughter of Robert; died in 1778.
2 CONT # Mary Moorman - born 1716 in Green Springs, Virginia. (Anthony Roots & Branches list Mary as having married William Ballard whose 2nd wife was Rachel (Clark) Moorman, widow of Thomas. She lists their children as Thomas, Frances, 
2 CONC Byrum, Delphia, Moorman, and David. Other researchers insist that William Ballard's first was Mary Byrum or Byrom, therefore I have not included this information.
2 CONT
2 CONT Links
2 CONT * http://freepages.genealogy.rootsweb.com/~sunnyann/moorman.html
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT In the early eighteenth century, a considerable number of Quakers purchased land in what is now Louisa and Caroline County, and established meetings at Cedar Creek (not far from the border of Louisa) in Hanover County, and at Gola
2 CONC nsville in Caroline County. Charles Moorman was one of the more influential of these Quaker settlers, and with his wife Elizabeth Reynolds, purchased considerable property in Albemarle County. At some point they removed to Hanover
2 CONC  County (near present day Ashland), before finally settling at Green Springs in Louisa County. They had five children, who are shown per tradition as being born in Green Springs, but most of their children were likely born in eith
2 CONC er New Kent or Hanover County.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Moorman's eldest son, Charles, Jr., married Elizabeth Reynolds and located in Louisa county, where he patented three tracts of land: also patented 40O acres in Albemarle county at the junction of Medium and Moorman rivers, 
2 CONC from which circumstance the latter stream received its name. Later he secured a second tract in the same locality; he died in 1757. __________________________ ...Among the most influential of these Quakers was Charles Moorman(3) o
2 CONC f Louisa County, who married Elizabeth reynolds. She bore him five children, Thomas(4) (1708-1766), Judith(4) (Douglas), Ann(4) (Martin), Achilles(4) and Charles(4). The family purchased considerable land in Albemarle County on an
2 CONC d near Moormans River, a stream named for Thomas (" William and Mary College Quarterly Historical Magazine: "The Moorman Family of Virginia"; Paullin, Charles O.; 2nd Series, Vol. 12, No. 3 (July 1931), pp. 177-180. Hereinafter ci
2 CONC ted as "Wm & Mary Quarterly.")  Biography: Charles, the other son, lived in Louisa County near "Green Spring" where he had settled in 1704. He married Elizabeth Reynolds. They were both devout Quakers, although they owned slaves. 
2 CONC Charles was a Justice of the Peace and with his son, Thomas, was an overseer of the Society of Friends. Charles died in 1756.  
2 CONT
2 CONT The first was Thomas (1705-1765) who married Rachel Clark. They lvied in Louisa County ner Green Spring, but a few years before he died, he and his family moved to Bedford County where he held large tracts of land. Records at Bedf
2 CONC ord show that he first bought land in that County, July 17, 1762. His will is dated July 22, 1765; it was probated in November of the following year. The second son of Rachel and Thomas Moorman, Micajah (1735) married Susannah Chi
2 CONC les and lived on land upon which part of Lynchburg now stands. He was one of the trustees of the City. Micajah (1735-1806) raised his own eleven children, as well as his sister Elizabeth's sons, Moorman and Christopher Johnson, af
2 CONC ter her death. These two nephews afterwards married Micajah's two daughters. The eldest son, Zachariah, in 1763, settled five miles south of Lynch's Ferry on the James. He married Elizabeth, daughter of Henry Terrell of Caroline C
2 CONC ounty in 1755. His second wife was Elizabeth Johnson. Many of the Moormans were disowned by the Society for holding slaves and "marrying out of unity". Thomas Clark Moorman, with almost his whole family started migration to the We
2 CONC st when he set out in 1755 for the Ohio Wilderness. Many Quakers followed him later (Brown, Lynchburg's Pioneer Quakers, pg. 60-61.) 
2 CONT
2 CONT The second child of Charles and Elizabeth was Judith, who married John Douglas. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The third child, Charles, married Mary Adams; 
2 CONT
2 CONT the fourth child, Achilles, married Elizabeth Adams and lived in Bedford County.  Charles MOORMAN and Elizabeth REYNOLDS  by Linda Sparks Starr March 1999 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT In 1669, Zachariah Moorman, along with his children, Charles, Thomas and Sally Ann, sailed from Southampton on the Glasgow and landed in Barbados in West Indies.  Zachariah and Mary Moorman's first surviving  child  was  Thomas, b
2 CONC orn in 1658.  
