3 CTRY United States
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Mildred Cobb was born in 1717 in St Peters Parish, New Kent county Va. She died in 1811. She married Jacob Moon about 1730 in Virginia
2 CONT
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT #  		M	i	 William Moon
2 CONT #  		F	ii	 Susannah Moon was born in 1738 in Virginia.
2 CONT #  		M	iii	 William Moon was born on 26 Nov 1740 in Virginia.
2 CONT #  		F	iv	 Patricia"Patsy" Moon was born in 1745 in Virginia.
2 CONT #  		F	v	 Pleasant Moon was born in 1747 in Virginia.
2 CONT #  		F	vi	 Sally Moon was born in 1750 in Virginia.
2 CONT #  		M	vii	 Jacob jr Moon was born in 1754 in Virginia.
2 CONT #  		M	viii	 Jesse Moon was born in 1755 in Virginia.
2 CONT #  		U	ix	 Archelaus Moon was born in 1756 in Virginia.
2 CONT #  		U	x	 Pleasant Moon was born in 1770 in Virginia.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT The will of Mildred (Cobb) Moon, dated May 27, 1811, left her granddaughter, Mildred Moon, her negro Leak, “to her and her heirs forever.” 
2 CONT Her son, William, was appointed executor. 
2 CONT The will was recorded in Albemarle County, December 2, 1811, and proved in 1812. (Will Book 5, p. 184.) 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Cobb or Cobbs family, as it was originally, is one of the oldest family names known to English history.
2 CONT On November 13, 1750, as shown by Deed Book I, p. 229, Records of Albemarle County, the original Jacob Moon of Albemarle County, son of William I, purchased from Thomas Kirkpatrick four hundred acres in South Garden among the moun
2 CONC tains along the branches of the Hardware River, and in the gorge of its south fork. He also entered a small tract of land in the same vicinity. In 1779 he sold two hundred and eighty acres of land in Albemarle County on Little Tot
2 CONC ier Creek. On October 25, 1784. he sold the land in South Garden to George Divers (Deed Book 9, p. 53). This book also shows that Jacob Moon, Sr., was a citizen of Bedford County on that date.
2 CONT
2 CONT On June 3, 1796, Jacob and Mildred (Cobb) Moon deeded “For and in consideration of the natural love and affection which they bear for their said son, William Moon, as well as for the further consideration of five shillings current
2 CONC  money in hand paid the receipt whereof they do hereby acknowledge have given and granted these presents do give and grant unto the said William Moon his heirs, executors, administrators or assigns forever one tract of land lying 
2 CONC in the county ot Albemarle on the waters of Totier Creek containing one hundred and fifty acres more or less.” (Deed Book 12, p. 108).
2 CONT
2 CONT The Virginia Magazine of History and Biography (Vol. 18, p. 258) shows that a Jacob Moon’s name appears in a petition under Virginia legislative papers of Albemarle, Amherst, and Buckingham Counties on November 9, 1776, as one of 
2 CONC the Dissenters from the Church of England “for the sake of good order.”
2 CONT
