1 NOTE {geni:about_me} A Patriot of the American Revolution for VIRGINIA with the rank of ENSIGN.  [http://services.dar.org/Public/DAR_Research/search_adb/?action=full&p_id=A079195  DAR Ancestor #  A079195]
2 CONT
2 CONT ONLY CHILD OF PATRIOT WAS CHRISTOPHER. 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT JACOB MOON
2 CONT
2 CONT It has been thought that Jacob, the Paymaster, who was born in 1737, was the son of Jacob Moon, who, with his brother William, settled in Albemarle County sometime before 1750. It is more probable, however, that he was an uncle of
2 CONC  the above Jacob, or of another line. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The records of the War Department at Washington show that Jacob Moon, the Paymaster, served in the 14th Virginia Regiment, commanded successively by Col. Charles Lewis, the Lieutenant-Colonel Abraham Buford, and Col. William Davie
2 CONC s. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was appointed April 26, 1777, and resigned August 28, 1778. as Paymaster of the 14th Regiment.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the fall of 1778 he married Ann or Nancy, who was born about the year 1760, daughter of Christopher Ammon, it is thought of New Kent County, Virginia, sometime before 1780.
2 CONT
2 CONT On June 29, 1779, he enlisted in a Virginia Regiment from Bedford County. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Two thousand pounds was advanced to him to recruit men. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He served until March 15, 1731, when at the Battle of Guilford Court House he was either killed, or so badly wounded, that he died four days later. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He served as Lieutenant with the Virginia troops and when he was wounded at the Battle of Guilford he was in Col. Lynch’s Virginia Regiment. 
2 CONT
2 CONT His heirs received 2666 acres of land for his Revolutionary service, and were entitled to additional bounty land.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacob Moon’s will dated March 2. 1731. was probated May 28, 1/81. 
2 CONT (Records of Bedford County, Will Book A, p. 393.) 
2 CONT He left to his youngest son two hundred acres of land in Albemarle County adjoining the old Furnace. 
2 CONT He appointed his brothers, William and Archelaus, and his wife executors.
2 CONT
2 CONT A few years after Jacob Moon’s death, his widow married in Bedford County, Samuel Hancock, and on account of Hancocks’ services in the Revolution she received a pension May 22, 1841, in Bedford County, from the date of his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT >'''Jacob and Ann or Nancy (Ammon) Moon had two sons, Christopher and Jacob, Jr., who was born after his father’s death, August 11, 1781, and died in infancy.''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT Christopher was born November 15, 1779. 
2 CONT
2 CONT On his application, executed October 24, 1842, he, was allowed the pension that was due his mother on account of the Revolutionary services of his father.
2 CONT
2 CONT On June 22, 1821, Christopher Moon conveyed to James Callaway 87 acres of land in Bedford County on the branches of the Elk Creek. (Records of Bedford County, Deed Book K, p. 367).
2 CONT
