2 CONT killed at the Battle of Guilford Courthouse, North Carolina on March 15, 1781. Rev. War Pension W4691.* Reference: [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/242636419/jacob-moon Find A Grave Memorial] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/S
2 CONC martCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Aug 22 2022, 0:56:23 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONC ----------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT At the Battle of Guilford Courthouse, the Virginia riflemen under the command of Colonel Charles Lynch were deployed on the right wing of the American army, along with Lt. Col. William Washington’s dragoons. During the battle, Bri
2 CONC tish General Charles Lord Cornwallis became incensed when he saw the damage Lynch’s sharpshooting riflemen were doing to the British 33rd Regiment of Foot, his old regiment. So, Cornwallis sent forward his Hessian Jaegers with ord
2 CONC ers to target the Riflemen’s officers and silence the Virginians. The Jaegers rushed forward and opened fire.
2 CONT The Jaegers, armed with short-barreled hunting rifles, were, like the Virginians, crack shots. They took aim at Lynch’s officers, with deadly results.
2 CONT Four of the Riflemen’s captains went down in the barrage. Captains Thomas Helm and William Jones, both shot through the head, were killed instantly. Captains David Beard was mortally wounded, as was Captain Jacob Moon, who was sho
2 CONC t through the side.
2 CONT Moon lived near New London and had served in the Virginia Continental line for 3 years before being commissioned a captain in the Bedford County militia. Veterans who witnessed Moon’s wounding reported that ball passed all the way
2 CONC  through him, and that he caught it as it came out the other side, calling out for his brother-in-law, Lt. Michael Gilbert, when he was hit. Captain Moon was carried off the battlefield, in great pain. 
2 CONT Moon was taken to a house about 3 miles from the battlefield, along with Ensign Henry Brown, who had also been shot by the Jaegers. That evening, as Greene’s army retreated to its camp at the Troublesome Ironworks, Brown evacuated
2 CONC  with them, despite a very painful wound in his thigh, enduring a miserable all-night march in a cold driving rain that veterans recalled as being more horrible than the battle itself. Brown survived his wound (and the arduous ret
2 CONC reat) and was afterwards carried back to Virginia in a horse litter, by his father and brother.
2 CONT Being too badly wounded to move, Captain Jacob Moon was one of the many American casualties who were left behind when the army withdrew. He lingered on in great pain for several days, before dying on March 19. It is likely that Mo
2 CONC on had a premonition of his death. In camp on the evening of March 2, while on the march to join Greene’s army, he wrote his will and had it witnessed by Lieutenant Benjamin Rice.
2 CONT Captain Jacob Moon died in Guilford County, North Carolina on March 19, 1781, two hundred forty-five years ago today—one of four captains in Colonel Charles Lynch’s battalion of Virginia riflemen who fell at the Battle of Guilford
2 CONC  Courthouse. 
