1 NAME Phoebe /Nicks/
2 GIVN Phoebe
2 SURN Nicks
2 _MARNM Brashear
1 SEX F
1 BIRT
2 DATE 8 JUN 1738
2 ADDR
3 STAE Maryland
3 CTRY United States
1 BURI
2 DATE 1811
2 PLAC Brashears-Kries Cemetery
2 ADDR
3 CITY Harriman
3 STAE Tennessee
3 CTRY United States
1 DEAT
2 DATE 15 JAN 1811
2 ADDR
3 CITY Greensboro
3 STAE North Carolina
3 CTRY United States
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Named for her grandmother, Phoebe (Price) Nicks.
2 CONT
2 CONT Phoebe is buried in an unmarked grave. Find A Grave Memorial # 66287036.
2 CONT
2 CONT PHOEBE'S NICKS ANCESTRY - The Nicks family stems from a William Nicks in Northamptonshire, England. Some of the family lived in New Kent Co, VA, and Talbot Co, MD, before moving to present day Guilford Co, NC.
2 CONT
2 CONT Copied from: A Brashears Family History by Charles Brashear page 17.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Samuel Brashears (RSB) married, about 1754, surely in Orange Co, NC, though we have found no records, Phoebe Nicks, b. 8 June 1738, probably in Talbot County, Maryland. She died in Guilford Co, NC in Dec 1811 and was buried
2 CONC  there, but her body was exhumed and moved to Roane County TN in 1812, where she was read. In the Brashears-Kries Cemetery, behind the RSB homestead. She was the d/o John Nicks and Margaret Edwards.
2 CONT
2 CONT Copied from: A Brashears Family History by Charles Brashear page 15
2 CONT
2 CONT PHOEBE'S POST–HUMOUS TRAVEL Phoebe (Nicks) Brashears died Dec 1811 and was buried in Guilford Co, NC, probably while on a visit to her brothers and sisters. Robert Samuel Brashears hired a grandson, Robert Samuel Gilliland, to exh
2 CONC ume her remains, transport them to Tennessee, and re-bury her. Old Robert Samuel Brashears was either too sick or too tight to pay up, even though the grandson gave him a bill: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert S. Brashears, Debtor To Robert S. Gilliland
2 CONT
2 CONT December 1811 To: 36 days service from the time said Brashears wife died until the above number of days, @3/per day........................................ $18.00
2 CONT
2 CONT January 1812 To: raising and moving said Brashear's wife and entering her again.............................................. 25.00
2 CONT
2 CONT Aprile May June 1812 To: 64 days services and horse trip to North Carolina York County and back to said Brashear's house in Roane County Tennessee with and for said Brashear's at 6/ per ........................64.00 --------------
2 CONC -------------------------------------______ --------------------------------------------------$107.00 ---------------------------------------------------$46.00 ---------------------------------------------------$32.00
2 CONT
2 CONT Copied from: A Brashears Family History by Charles Brashear page 29 
2 CONT
2 CONT  Parents:
2 CONT   John Nicks (1716 - 1781)
2 CONT
2 CONT  Spouse:
2 CONT   Robert Samuel Brashears (1731 - 1816)*
2 CONT
2 CONT  Children:
2 CONT   Phillip Brashears (1755 - 1815)*
2 CONT   Isaac Brashears (1760 - 1833)*
2 CONT   Samuel Brashears (1763 - 1829)*
2 CONT   Rebecca Brashears Roberts (1771 - 1859)*
2 CONT   Nancy Brashears Roberts (1773 - 1850)*
2 CONT   Mary Brashear Mason (1776 - 1816)*
2 CONT   Bassil Brashears (1781 - 1826)*
2 CONT
2 CONT  Sibling:
2 CONT   Phoebe Nicks Brashears (1738 - 1811)
2 CONT   John Nicks (1753 - 1830)*
2 CONT
2 CONT *Calculated relationship
2 CONT
2 CONT Burial: Brashears-Kries Cemetery  Sugar Grove Roane County Tennessee, USA
2 CONT
2 CONT ***A Brashear(s) Family History, Descendants of Robert and Benois Brasseur; Vol. 2; Robert C. Brashear of North Carolina and Some Descendants in TN, KY, MO, TX, etc., by Charles Brashear; page 78: Phoebe Nicks, d/o John Nicks and 
2 CONC Margaret Edwards, was born June 8, 1738, probably in Talbot Co., Maryland, before her family moved to North Carolina. She died in NC in Dec 1811. John Nicks had migrated from Talbot or Dorchester Co, Maryland, to Orange Co, North 
2 CONC Carolina, in 1753, with a stopover in Culpepper Co, Virginia. He is listed among the "Nottingham Colony," the founders of Buffalo Presbyterian Church in Orange, later Guilford Co, North Carolina.
