1 NOTE
2 CONT
2 CONT http://files.usgwarchives.net/al/montgomery/history/scotts-jamisons.txt   states:
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The Wyatts and Scotts and Colliers were related to each other in several ways. Cornelius Collier 
2 CONT (the grandfather of the distinguished Alabamian, the late Henry Watkins Collier, who was judge 
2 CONT of the Supreme Court and Governor of the State) married Elizabeth Wyatt. He was a wealthy 
2 CONT planter of Lunenburg County, Va., and his family seat was "Porto Bello" in York County, Va. 
2 CONT The Colliers were originally from England, but all of them who settled in America were Whigs 
2 CONT during the Revolution. It was a mournful illustration of the way family ties are ruptured by civil 
2 CONT war, that at the time young Wyatt (son of Cornelius) was pouring out his life blood at the battle 
2 CONT of Eutaw Springs in the cause of American Independence, his English cousin, Sir George Collier, 
2 CONT a rear admiral of the Red, was ravaging with his fleet the coast of Rhode Island. About the same 
2 CONT time Tarleton with his dragoons was trampling the crops at Portobello into the ground; proudly 
2 CONT unconscious of the fate, which awaited him at Yorktown. The father of Governor Collier was 
2 CONT James Collier, who married Elizabeth Bouldin. 
2 CONT
2 CONT There were three brothers of the Scotts of English origin, in the county of Gloucester, Va., Frank, 
2 CONT James and Thomas. 1. Frank Scott married and remained in Gloucester. 2. James Scott married 
2 CONT Francis Collier, sister of Cornelius Collier, of "Portobello." 3. Thomas Scott married Catherine 
2 CONT Tomkites. Their son Frank married Nancy Wyatt, the Mrs. Scott, of Lawrence County, of whom 
2 CONT we have been speaking. (See Scott Family.) 
1 NOTE
2 CONT
