0 @I6000000030148089239@ INDI
1 NAME Nathan /Winters/
2 GIVN Nathan
2 SURN Winters
2 _MARNM Winters
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 PLAC Galt East Cemetery
2 ADDR
3 STAE MO
1 BIRT
2 DATE 12 SEP 1794
2 ADDR
3 CITY Gallatin
3 STAE Tennessee
3 CTRY United States
1 DEAT
2 DATE 1865
2 ADDR
3 STAE Missouri
3 CTRY United States
1 OCCU Methodist Minister
1 TITL Rev.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''I and others have researched this line for many years and there is NO evidence linking Nathan Winters with Caleb Winters as indicated in the above additions to this tree. The additions were made by other people,
2 CONC  not me. These additions need to be removed due to lack of documented verification of any relationship between Nathan and Caleb Winters.''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Findagrave, Nathan Winters was born 12 Sep 1794 in Gallatin, Sumner County, Tennessee, USA and died16 Jun 1865 (aged 70) in Galt, Grundy County, Missouri, USA. He is buried in Galt East Cemetery, Grundy County, Missou
2 CONC ri, USA. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Nathan's birthplace of Sumner County is said to be recorded in a family Bible, however, no Winters entries can be found in the Sumner County Tax Lists for 1790, 1792, 1794, 1795 - 1801. The Reconstructed 1810 TN Census Records lis
2 CONC t a John Winters in Sumner County and a Joshua Winters in Greene County, TN. These are from the 1810 Tax Lists. Since Nathan first shows up in the Tax Lists for Greene County (1815), Joshua may be his father or a near relative. Na
2 CONC than named his first-born son Joshua, so this may be confirmation.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1815 Nathan and his wife, Nancy Ruth (Lindsey/Lindsay), daughter of another intrepid frontiersman, Isaac Lindsey, moved to Illinois Territory locating in St. Clair County. In 1817 Nathan Winters is listed as donating $10 to a b
2 CONC uilding fund for a new church in the village of Shiloh. A Shiloh Church had stood in the same spot since the early 1800s. In 1819 a new brick church was constructed with the donations.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rev. Nathan Winters is recorded in several area histories as a prominent Methodist preacher and pioneer of Illinois. Joshua Winters, possibly his father, is listed as living in the village of Shiloh in Clair County around 1822. ['
2 CONC 'History of St. Clair Co, ILL'') Sometime in the early 1820s, Nathan moved his family from St. Clair County IL to Morgan County IL. He was a farmer until the breaking out of the Indian troubles, in which he was engaged in three ca
2 CONC mpaigns as an officer, and four times as a private, under Andrew Jackson. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Nathan Winters was a Captain in the Third Regiment during the Blackhawk War. (The Black Hawk War was a brief conflict between the United States and Native Americans led by Black Hawk, a Sauk leader. The war erupted soon after Blac
2 CONC k Hawk and a group of Sauks, Meskwakis, and Kickapoos, known as the "British Band", crossed the Mississippi River, into the U.S. state of Illinois, from Iowa Indian Territory in April 1832.)   "The Third Regiment consisted of six 
2 CONC companies, commanded, respectively, by Captains Benjamin Barney and Elisha Petty of Pike County, John Harris of Macoupin, and William B. Smith, William T. Givens and Nathan or Nathaniel Winters of Morgan County."  SEE https://www.
2 CONC familysearch.org/search/catalog/2612994 
2 CONT
2 CONT SEE ALSO: ''The Black Hawk war: including a review of Black Hawk's life; illustrated with upward of three hundred rare and interesting portraits and views by Frank E. Stevens''
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1840, Nathan's son Ezekial obtained government land in Grundy Co., MO, then known as Livingston County where he raised hogs and drove them to market in Hannibal and Brunswick, MO. His father moved there with him (or shortly aft
2 CONC er) and in 1841 officiated at the second marriage ceremony in Grundy Co, MO. Nathan Winters was said to be the first preacher in the township and preached the first sermon there at a private home. More info about the family can be
2 CONC  found in ''The History of Grundy County, MO'' online.
2 CONT
2 CONT There may be a clue to Nathan's lineage in the The Black Hawk book mentioned above which lists a J. D.(Dever/Devers) Winters, mail contractor, who served as a guide for the soldiers in the campaign. The man was John D. Winters (17
2 CONC 94-1869) who won contracts for carrying the mail from Peoria to Galena IL in 2 horse stages from 1830-35. (US Congressional Serial Set Vol 239, p. 76 online). His Findagrave info is at: https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/16021106
2 CONC 8/john-d_-winters 
2 CONT
2 CONT Forced out of the stage and freight business in Illinois, the J.D. Winters family headed west to California and Nevada where they were in exactly the right place at exactly the right time to make their fortune in transportation, l
2 CONC and, and mining. At the end of his life, J. D. was committed to an asylum (Stockton State Hosp) in Santa Clara County, Calif. He died in Stockton, CA, and was buried in East Lawn Cemetery, Sacramento (near the graves of Nathan Win
2 CONC ter's son James and his wife Nancy McEfee Winters and two of their daughters.) In 1904, his remains and those of his wife were relocated to Brighton, CA cemetery which no longer exists. There is a marvelous article on J.D. at: htt
2 CONC ps://historyonthefox.wordpress.com/2014/03/17/were-pa-adam-hoss-and-little-joe-from-illinois/
2 CONT
2 CONT I believe these two families (Nathan Winters and J. D. Winters) are closely related somehow. 
2 CONT
2 CONT J. D.'s father, according to the above article, was William Winters of Allegheny County PA and his mother was Katherine Devers Winters. J. D. Winters can be found in FindAGrave and in many online articles. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Tangentially, in the same cemetery of East Lawn Memorial Park is a plot for "John Anton Winters" 1866-1953 who was born in Hanover, Germany according to Findagrave. He can also be found under the name Johann Anton Christoph Winter
2 CONC s in MyHeritage. He states in the 1900, 1930, and 1940 census records that his parents were both born in Germany. John Anton immigrated to the US in 1875 or1878. His wife Emma Craddock was born in New Zealand and immigrated in 186
2 CONC 9/70. The fact that he is in this cemetery along with the other Winters who seem to be interconnected leads to the possibility that he may be the connection to the Old World source of this Winters line!
2 CONT
2 CONT It is possible that the Nathan Winters line traces back to German immigrants to PA!
2 CONT
2 CONT No clear DNA connections can be found on FTDNA for Nathan's line. The entries are sketchy and provide little real info regarding lineage.
2 CONT
2 CONT [Interestingly, there is a state legislator and Baptist preacher named Rev. Nathan Winters very active in Wyoming today.]
2 CONT
2 CONT [The line of Moses Winters (1732-1798) may be connected to Nathan's line, though no documentation has yet been found. In ''The Early History of Middle Tennessee'', Moses is recorded as one of the founders/builders of Eaton's Stati
2 CONC on in 1779, a fort/settlement in Middle TN. No other Winters are recorded in the area at this time. Moses settled in Tennessee County which became Robertson County, and his immediate family are all to be found there. Many people s
2 CONC peculate that Nathan's parents were Caleb Winters (born 1760), son of Moses Winters and Sarah Harris, but this is nowhere confirmed, and the extensive research by Caleb's descendants who erected a family monument in TN would claim
2 CONC  otherwise.  The monument does not name our Nathan but rather Moses' son Nathan A. Winters born in 1804. As of now, no researchers have found his parentage. The tax lists for early Robertson County which are available online do no
2 CONC t go back to the 1790s.The Tennessee Early Tax Lists Records 1783-1895 available on Ancestry only go back to 1812 for Robertson County, so it is unknown if there was another Winters line there earlier than 1812. Nathan Winters' ch
2 CONC ildren report his birthplace differently in the 1850/60 censuses: TN, KY, PA, or England!] 
2 CONT
2 CONT
