2 CONT
2 CONT 1797-Excerpt taken from the article "Life & Times of William Smeathers" written 1840-50 by Honorable Thomas McCreery, pub 1866 Owensboro Ky Monitor and republished 1979 in "Back Home in Kentucky" magazine, Owensboro,Ky, quote, "Ne
2 CONC ar Blackford William called upon Natty and Ely Beal; at house of the latter William was agreeably surprised finding his brother, James Smeathers laying seige to Beall's sister in law, Elizabeth Beall." James did later marry Elizab
2 CONC eth and she was pregnant at the time with a child later she named Asa from a lst marriage to a Beall cousin either killed in war or by Indians, yet to be identified, but birth dad to Asa Beall.
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wayne A Hodges Feb 3 2003-"History of Ohio Co KY" pg 12 states "Bill Smeathers was a pioneer who first gave shelter and guidance to the neophyte hunter, John "Bluebeard" Miller who later took Ralph Ringwood to his bachelor ho
2 CONC me to live and became his guide, philosopher and friend." Pg 17 states "There is reasonable authentic traditional proof of the identity of several persons who, probably, resided Ft Hartford during or shortly after its founding, "T
2 CONC ick-eyed John" Miller, veteran indian fighter, and like Bill Smeathers, friend of Ralph Ringwood as long as the latter lived in Ohio." Pg 437 states "Ralph's friend, William Smeathers, also fell upon evil days some five or six yea
2 CONC rs after they parted, when he became first Ohio Countian tried for murder 1809. Smeathers lived near the Yellow Banks (the present Owensboro) which territory then in Ohio County. In defense of his home and sister Mollie, he was fo
2 CONC rced to kill a drunken flatboatman whose craft tied up nearby while the marauding crew threatened the property, lives and honor of the neighboring settlers. Promptly giving himself up to the officers of the law, William was tried 
2 CONC at the April 1809 term of the Ohio Circuit Court, before the eccentric but respected Judge Henry P Broadnax. The celebrated Joseph Hamilton Daveiss, for whom Daviess County was named, volunteered to defend his friend, Smeathers, w
2 CONC ho was acquitted." "Col Joseph Daviess was US Attorney for Ky & had 36,000ac around PANTHER CREEK & OHIO RIVER & WM SMEATHERS did the survey for him." "WM SMEATHERS & BENJAMIN DUNCAN were appointed Land Commissioners to settle lan
2 CONC d disputes." [Duncan, father to Mary E Duncan md Asa Beall [aka Smeathers]]
2 CONT
2 CONT 1798- From the Owensboro official web site "1780, the first white settlers came, via the Ohio River, to what is now Daviess Co. Early settlers inc Joseph Blackford, William Smeathers & Valentine "Felty" Husk. Blackford came 1780, 
2 CONC established hunting camp Blackford Crk, & was later killed by Indians. Smeathers came 1797 and built his cabin on a site near the river now St Elizabeth Street, Owensboro, once the ole Bransford Stemmery.[stemmery a building used 
2 CONC in tobacco industry, after dried, some is re-dried to specifics of a customer,a separate unit from the warehouses or drying houses. Brandford Stemmery would be a building used in tobacco growth by a family named Bransford] From th
2 CONC is dwelling constructed by Smeathers, the first settlement began. The city originally called "Yellowbank" in reference to color of the soil along Ohio Riverbanks. Noteworthy, William's son John Sr in TX names his land 'Yellowbanks
2 CONC '.
2 CONT
2 CONT Settlement was elected as county seat when county formed 1815. Daviess Co was 58th KY Co; named in honour of Col. Joseph H Daveiss, distinguished lawyer & soldier, who was William's lawyer later in his 1809 KY murder trial.
2 CONT
2 CONT [no wife is ever listed for William in KY census/indicating she had died prior to census]
2 CONT
2 CONT 1799-Ohio Co KY Tax Books Jacob Smeathers paid taxes on himself William Smeathers John Smeathers 1800 Ohio Co Ky Census-William Smethers Jacob, John & James 1801 Ohio Co KY-William Smothers,John,James,Jacob 1802 Ohio Co KY-John Sm
2 CONC ethers,William,James,Jacob 1803 Ohio Co KY-John Smethers,James,Jacob,William 1804 Ohio Co KY-Wm Smothers paid tax self;3 horses John Smothers paid tax on self;1 horse James paid tax on self;6 horses Jacob paid taxes on self;1 hors
2 CONC e. 1805 Ohio Co-Wm Smethers paid tax on self;3 horses Jacob Smuthers paid tax on self James (exempt from paying tax on self) 4 horses John Smothers paid tax on self; 3 blacks 1806 Ohio Co-Wm Smeathers,Jacob,John,James 1807 Ohio Co
2 CONC -James Smothers,Wm 1808 Ohio Co-William Smeathers,James Smeathers
2 CONT
2 CONT 1809-William Smeathers kills Andrew Norris,a keelboatman, for raping his sister Mary Smeathers; outside the Smeathers cabin near what's now McConnell Plaza; first recorded murder in the new settlement. 1810-William leaves down Ohi
2 CONC o River lst time to Texas 1811-July-Following Col Daviess' death at Battle of Tippecanoe, John Rowan & James Mead went to Hartford Court to try to collect on Smeathers note;continued until 1813. [possibly due to Pappy had gone to 
2 CONC TX. Rowan can be remembered as the owner of the home which the song "My Old Ky Home" was written.] 1811-1812-CORNSTALK MILITIA enlistment ready to supply soldiers for War of 1812-William Smeathers, Captain, KY Mounted Spies, Enlis
2 CONC tment Sept 18 1812 To what time engaged or enlisted October 30 1812-Official Record William enlists in Major Touisant Dubois Company for KY Mounted Spies, William is Capt of his own battallion which inc. Privates' John Berry [husb
2 CONC and of Betsy Smeathers], Caleb Winters [his wife Mary's half brother];John Smeathers Sr [William's son]; William Glenn [husband of sister Margaret Smeathers] inc/other names assoc w/Pappy; John Glenn, William & John Meeks. Oct 30 
2 CONC 1812-William discharged at Vincennes Ind; given rations to return home inc/grain for his horse. War of 1812 Service Records about William Smeathers "Name:William Smeathers Company:DOBOIS'BATTALION MOUNTED SPIES,Rank-Induction:CAPT
2 CONC AIN Rank-Discharge:CAPTAIN Roll Box:191 Roll Exct:602" 1813 Ohio Co KY-Archibald Smothers,William,James 1815-April-Smeathers cabin site selected as the county seat by commissioners appointed by the first County Court 1816-October–
2 CONC William Smeathers Won $10 judgement against David Glover KY [factbook] 1818-William Smeathers rec'd order to be on Grand Jury Daviess Co IND 1820-Nelson Co Ky census Listing for William Seathers No twsp #210. William then leaves f
2 CONC or TX the 2nd time as Scout for Stephen F Austin to help settle 300 folks that become the Austin colony in Rep of TX.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1820-37-William in Texas-From "History of Tx" by H Yoakum, The Steck Co Austin Tx 1935 Vol 1-'From the discovery of Galveston Island 1686 by the LaSalle Colony til 1816 it has been unsettled. A few roving Carankawaes resort to wes
2 CONC t end to fish;no human habitation on Island. Beginning 1816 Island covered w/green grass which deer herds ate.' Noted by Elsie Turk Smothers: The above Carankawaes were cannibals. A Smothers researcher fr La stated there is a docu
2 CONC ment in Mex City states the Spanish gov offered to give Galveston Island to William Smeathers and he refused the offer because there was no fresh water and the pelican eggs tasted fishy. Thomas McCreery used these words in his Sme
2 CONC athers article.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1821-From the Brazoria County Historical Museum-"William Smeathers (1767-1837) pro guide/hunter who first came to TX 1809 return with Austin/Rightor as a scout/hunter Dec 1821; 14 workmen several investor adventurers left New Orle
2 CONC ans Nov 25 1821 arrived at mouth Brazos Dec 3 explored river built a cabin at Ft Bend, most returned to LA; when they did not find the empresario. 1822-William Smeathers lived Caney Creek in present Matagorda Co 1824; rec a full h
2 CONC eadright on the Brazos due to finding land for the settlers and as promised by Stephen F Austin."
2 CONT
2 CONT 4 Children William and Mary mrd 1788 TN John Bate Smeathers Sr b 1789 Robertson Co Tenn Betsy Smeathers b BET 1791 Robertson Co Tenn Archibald Jacob Smothers b 1794 Robertson Co Tenn Mary Ann Smeathers b 22 APR 1795 Robertson Co T
2 CONC enn
2 CONT
2 CONT 1837-WILLIAM SMEATHERS OBITUARY-Houston Telegraph Aug 19 1837."At Columbia on the morning of the 13th inst. in the 71st year (b 1766) of his age, Mr William Smeathers who was one of the earliest pioneers of this country having res
2 CONC ided in Texas nearly seventeen years, subject to all the privations of a new and uncultivated country, and bravely maintaining himself single handedly on the frontier against the assaults of numerous hoards of predatory savages in
2 CONC  many a hard fought fray. But his course is finished and he has gone to his final audit.In his death his country has lost a bold and hearty defender, and his family a kind parent." [unk who wrote the obit]
2 CONT
2 CONT The original will of William Smeathers is on file in the County Courthouse at Angleton TX. He left all he had to his 2nd son Archibald Jacob inc. his Old Nag, also 100. to bury him [obviously where no one could find him too]also l
2 CONC eft land to his son-in-law, John Berry, and 2 of 3 Berry grandsons, John Bate Berry, Andrew Jackson Berry, inc/his rifle, btw-Audie Murphy descends and is written about by AJ, grandson Andrew Jackson Pope aka Jack Pope. Third son 
2 CONC of John Berry, Joseph was already well established in the blacksmith shop with his dad John inc owning some land. 
2 CONT
