2 DATE AFT 1887
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} James Bird Allen was listed in the 1850 and 1860 census records with his parents and siblings in Platte Township, Andrew County, Missouri.  He was also listed with them in the 1870 census for West Point, Rio Virgin
2 CONC , Utah Territory, as follows:  father, Lewis Allen, 57, James, 19, Margaret, 18, Sarah, 16, Andrew, 13, Martha, 11, Nancy, 8, and Mary K., 4.  His mother had passed away in 1869.  Living next door was his older brother, Rial Allen
2 CONC , 25, and his wife, Susan E., 21, and their 1 year old son Rial A. Allen.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the Millennium File, James Bird Allen was born August 15, 1850, in Savannah, Andrew County, Missouri, USA, and he married Hannah Maria West December 30, 1886, the daughter of Samuel Walker West and his plural wife, Ch
2 CONC ristiana Johansen West.  
2 CONT
2 CONT However, since Hanna died in 1887, they were not married for very long, only about 9 months.  According to her Find A Grave.com memorial, the following obit was from the Deseret News, September 21, 1887, page 16:  "ALLEN - At Wash
2 CONC ington City, Washington County, Utah, Hannah Rebecca, wife of J.B. Allen, and the daughter of Christiana West; born September 21, 1867, died August 9, 1887, in full faith of the Gospel.  The Washington City Cemetery where she was 
2 CONC buried lists her death date as August 10, 1887."  
2 CONT
2 CONT James Bird Allen is also listed in the Membership of The Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints, with his father, mother, and 12 siblings, Tabitha Jane, Beulah Ann, William, Rial, Jonathan Alexander, Samuel, Margaret E., Sara
2 CONC h Melissa, Andrew Jackson, Martha Permelia, Nancy Esther and Mary Ellen Allen.
2 CONT
2 CONT There was also a Bird Allen, 28 years old, living as a boarder in Washington, Utah, in the 1880 census, working as a farmer.  He was living with Andrew Sproul, 38, and his wife, Adaline, 44, and their children, Andrew, 11, Mary, 9
2 CONC , Adaline, 7, Angus, 4, and Annie, 2 years old.  A step-son, Randolph Andrus, 18, was also living in their home.
2 CONT
2 CONT * [http://history.lds.org/overlandtravels/pioneerDetail?lang=eng&pioneerId=26116011932687040730 Mormon Pioneer Overland Travel: James Bird Allen]; Company Unknown (1862); Approximate age at departure: 11.
2 CONT * [http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GSln=allen&GSfn=james&GSmn=bird&GSbyrel=all&GSdyrel=all&GSob=n&GRid=112425375&df=all& Find A Grave Memorial# 112425375]
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From Find A Grave.com:  Need more information about James Bird Allen.  There is no date, or place of death.  I started this site, in hopes of gathering more information.  If anyone has a grave location, please contacted me, at lin
2 CONC k below.  Last known place where he was living was in Arizona, and he went there with his brother Rial.
2 CONT
2 CONT Parents:
2 CONT
2 CONT Lewis Allen (1813 - 1888)
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth Alexander Allen (1819 - 1869)
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse:
2 CONT
2 CONT Hannah Rebecca West Allen (1867 - 1887)
2 CONT
2 CONT Burial:  Unknown
2 CONT
2 CONT Created by: Stacy Allen
2 CONT
2 CONT Record added: Jun 16, 2013
2 CONT
2 CONT Find A Grave Memorial# 112425375
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT NOTE: Since James Bird Allen's brother, Rial Allen, born February 27, 1844, in Paris, Henry County, Tennessee, died May 27, 1899, at Moenave, Coconino County, Arizona, and was buried in the Old LDS Tuba City Church Cemetery, it is
2 CONC  possible that his brother, James did accompany him to Arizona after the death of his wife Hannah, who may have died in childbirth.  The following obituary for Rial Allen is from Find A Grave.com:
2 CONT
2 CONT "Rial came west with his family and arrived in Salt Lake City, Utah in 1862.  He was 18 years old.  He married Susan Elizabeth Collins at the SLC Endowment House, 25 April 1868.  They were called by Brigham Young, the LDS Presiden
2 CONC t, to move to western Utah, at that time beyond St. George.  He and his wife moved with his parents to West Point (called The Muddy Settlement), Utah, which is now Moapa, Clark, Nevada.
2 CONT
2 CONT His mother Elizabeth Alexander Allen in 23 May 1869 died there, never recovering from the birth of her last child. The settlement was later abandoned due to Indian problems and confusion of taxes from Arizona, Utah and Nevada who 
2 CONC at one time or another claimed it. (Nevada won.)  They left on the recommendation of President Brigham Young and because the Nevada sheriffs were coming to collect taxes.  (They had already paid in the other states.) They could no
2 CONC t afford it and were uncomfortable being citizens of ‘wild' Nevada.  Large crops of wheat, cotton, and budding vineyards were left for others to harvest.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1871, when their first son Rial was two, his father, widowed Lewis Allen, bought a ranch in Long Valley near Pipe Springs, Arizona. It was called Moccasin Ranch.  Rial, as the oldest son, and family continued with him and helpe
2 CONC d him raise his unmarried siblings as their family increased.  Rial's wife, Susan Collins Allen apparently traveled to Washington, Washington, Utah, to have their next four children, Susan, Ann, Minnie, and Jim, and then would ret
2 CONC urn.
2 CONT
2 CONT Moccasin Ranch was a very successful farm and orchard.  It was excellent for producing cane, peaches, grapes, and manufacturing molasses.  In 1874, Lewis Allen gave the ranch to help form the Orderville United Order, a communal or
2 CONC ganization of several farms where people pooled all their resources and shared in kind.  The only sweetener they had was from the cane to molasses operation there.  Lewis became the shoe repair and cobbler for this Order of Enoch.
2 CONC   He became called Lewis ‘Moccasin' Lewis.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rial and his family stayed until called to settle the Tonto Basin in Arizona.  They moved and help found the city of Pine, Arizona.  Their house was the first one built and used as a fort against Indians roaming in the area for a 
2 CONC time. Rial Allen was called as the first Bishop and his wife Susan as the first Relief Society President.  It has excellent farming, ranching and timber.  They lived there until 1891, having five more children born there, Nancy Ad
2 CONC eline, "Addie", Margaret Jane "Maggie", Phoebe, Charlotte Beulah "Lottie", and Lafayette Collins "Lafe" Allen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rial was a true Renaissance Man, a man of self-sufficiency and talents.  Besides farming and ranching, he was a blacksmith;  he played violin, most often at Church Socials, made his own furniture, and was the town dentist (pulled 
2 CONC teeth).  When they left in 1891, called again to colonize this time Tuba City, Arizona, there were 25 family and 172 members in Pine, Arizona.  Many were called but only a few were willing to move on to this new desolate outpost. 
2 CONC  Some others who went were close friends and relatives by marriage the Brinkerhoff, Nelson, and Heward families.  They lived first in Moencopi near the old LDS Church site.  Rial Allen's family then moved to Moenave, 6 miles west 
2 CONC of Tuba City, Arizona.  Jacob Hamblin had had a home in that area.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rial ranched and farmed was known for his peaches, beans and excellent stock of animals including horses, mules and cows.  His two youngest sons Lafe and Frank played with the Indian children and learned their language at a young 
2 CONC age while his daughters watched them.  His wife Susan died there, never really recovering from their last child Frank, who was born in Moenave.  She was buried at the old LDS Tuba City Church Cemetery.  Rial Allen would remarry Ne
2 CONC ttie Heward and have one more child named Sessal.  He would die a few years later and be buried next to his first wife.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thank you J. B. Lindner for this information
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouses:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.) Susan Elizabeth Collins, marriage: 25 April 1868
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.) Susannah Janet Heward, marriage: 18 November 1896
2 CONT
2 CONT Death: May 27, 1899, Moenave, Coconino, Arizona.
2 CONT
2 CONT CHILDREN with 1st wife:
2 CONT
2 CONT • Rial Albert Allen 1869 – 1873
2 CONT
2 CONT • Susan Temperance "Tempy" Allen 1871 – 1941
2 CONT
2 CONT • Ann Elizabeth Allen Tanner 1873 – 1957
2 CONT
2 CONT • Sara Malinda "Minnie" Allen Nay 1875 – 1944
2 CONT
2 CONT • James Lewis "Jim" Allen 1878 – 1933
2 CONT
2 CONT • Nancy Adeline "Addie" Allen Brinkerhoff 1880 – 1962
2 CONT
2 CONT • Margaret Jane "Maggie" Allen Holbrook 1882 – 1953
2 CONT
2 CONT • Phoebe Allen Brinkerhoff 1885 – 1944
2 CONT
2 CONT • Charlotte Beulah "Lottie" Allen Higbee 1887 – 1980
2 CONT
2 CONT • Lafayette Collins "Lafe" Allen 1889 – 1932
2 CONT
2 CONT • Franklin "Frank" Allen 1892 – 1969
2 CONT
2 CONT CHILD with 2nd wife:
2 CONT
2 CONT • Sessal Dellma Allen
2 CONT
2 CONT From 1877-1880, A. J. Randall and other men including Rial Allen, Wid and Dave Fuller, explored the Tonto Basin area in Arizona.
