1 OCCU housewife
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Martha Pamelia Allen''' (1858 - 1931), son of Lewis Allen and Elizabeth Alexander, was born in Platte Township, Andrew County, Missouri, on 9 December 1858, the eleventh of thirteen children. Her parents had con
2 CONC verted to The Chursh of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints within a few months of their marriage, and two of her father's siblings had joined with the very earliest settlers in Salt Lake City, Utah. In 1862, her father and his brot
2 CONC her, James Allen, joined an independent oxcart company and emigrated to Utah Territory. Martha was just four years old when they reached the home of her uncle, Andrew Jackson Allen, in Draper, Utah on 30 August 1862, after three g
2 CONC rueling months of travel. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The family first settled in Washington, Utah and remained there for several years, but in 1868 her father was called to to the Muddy Mission in Nevada. Lewis Allen and his family helped found the settlement of West Point, in Linco
2 CONC ln County, Nevada, which was quickly revealed to be a nearly impossible place to settle due to heat, flooding, and extremely alkali soil. During their first winter there, on 23 March 1869, Martha's mother, Elizabeth Alexander, die
2 CONC d and was buried at West Point, Lincoln County, Nevada. Martha was just ten years old.
2 CONT
2 CONT The family returned to Washington in 1870 and Martha's father bought a farm in Utah's Long Valley, naming it the Moccasin Springs Ranch. Five years later, Lewis moved the family again, in response to LDS President Brigham Young's 
2 CONC call to enter the United Order of Enoch, a form of communal living. In February 1875 the valley was surveyed and the town of Orderville, Utah was laid out and building commenced. Ever obedient to the Church leadership, Martha's fa
2 CONC ther turned his profitable Moccasin Springs Ranch over to the Order and moved his family to Orderville.
2 CONT
2 CONT Orderville's streets ran in all four directions. A large square 30 x 30 rods was laid out in the center of which was a community-dining hall. This was also used for meetings and dancing. The dwelling houses were built and joined t
2 CONC ogether to form an enclosure of the square on the order of a fort. Each family had to reside in one room. The dining hall was made of rough lumber and put together with wooden pegs. It was lined with adobe and later plastered. The
2 CONC  kitchen and bakery were built on. They all had assigned jobs. William H. Black was in charge of the dining hall and seven women were assigned to help him. Those eight people cooked and served three meals a day for 80 families. Th
2 CONC e men were served first, at 7 a.m., 12 noon and 6 p.m. The women and children were served later.
2 CONT
2 CONT The storehouse was built on the southeast corner of the property. There was also a shoe shop, where Lewis Allen made and mended shoes. At first his shoes were very simple, with no difference between shoes for the right or left fee
2 CONC t. J. S. Allen and Lewis Allen were in charge of a bucket factory where wooden buckets, tubs, kegs, barrels and churns were made from the local red cedar. Lewis Allen and his son-in-law Willis Webb ran the first molasses mill in t
2 CONC he Order. Molasses was the only sweetening available in Orderville for some time.
2 CONT
2 CONT On April 17, 1878, Martha Pamelia Allen married William Marshall III in St. George, Washington County, Utah. Although St. George was also a United Order community, it is not presently known how Martha and William met. Martha's fat
2 CONC her was still living and working in Orderville at the time of her marriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT Martha and William had nine or ten children. She died of pneumonia at the age of 72, on 16 February 1931, in Huntington, Emery County, Utah.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Children of Martha Pamelia Allen and William Marshall==
2 CONT #William Lewis Marshall (1879 - 1958)
2 CONT #John W. Marshall (1882- 1925)
2 CONT #James Ira Marshall (1883 - 1945)
2 CONT # Adelbert Marshall (1885 - 1963)
2 CONT #Elizabeth Marshall (1887 - 1887)
2 CONT #Arley Marshall (1888 - 1959)
2 CONT #Lorin Marshall (1891 - 1903)
2 CONT #Andrew Marion Marshall (1896 - 1975)
2 CONT #Alison ''or'' Aliene Marshall (1898 1959)
2 CONT #Marie Marshall (b. 1903)
2 CONT #Jesse Marshall (1904 - 1981)
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Notes==
2 CONT *Middle name spelled variously, "Permilla", "Parmelia", and "Pamella"
