1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Judge John Ward Hooper, appointed December 8, 1832, was the first judge of the Cherokee circuit, selected because of his legal acumen at the time when there were such complicated questions concerning the federal, s
2 CONC tate, and Indian Laws.  Judge Hooper subjected himself to criticism from Georgia's Gov. Lumpkin and the Indian agents in Cass (Bartow) County for his stern protection of the Cherokee Indian from the oppressive land speculators.  A
2 CONC fter a legislative investigation of his official conduct, ordered by the Gov., he was found to have followed rule of law concerning his duties.  Judge Hooper was highly revered by the Cherokee as a person who possessed virtue and 
2 CONC honesty to stay the hand of unprovoked injustice at such a time in history when most men would take advantage. Judge of the Cherokee Circuit 1832-35; 1849-50
2 CONT
2 CONT Death of Hon. John W. Hooper - Judge John W. Hooper died at his residence in Dade County, on the morning of Tuesday, the 15th instant, and his remains were brought to Rome and buried in the Laurel Hill Cemetery on the 17th.  He wa
2 CONC s over seventy years old, and well known by all the early settlers in the Cherokee country.  In 1833 he was appointed Judge of the Cherokee circuit, which then embraced all the territory North of the Chatahoochee River, except, pe
2 CONC rhaps Cobb County.  In 1836 or 1837 he moved west and was absent from the state some ten years.
2 CONT
2 CONT He always maintained a high and honorable position as a lawyer, and was a most excellent citizen in all the relations of life.  He was remarkable for his warm-hearted kindess and the generosity of his disposition.  Instead of hoar
2 CONC ding up his world's goods, it was his sweetest joy to contribute to the relief of the suffering, and he made his own life happy by making that of others so.
2 CONT
2 CONT A good man has gone.  In life he was loved by the good and respected by all.  A shadow of gloom now rests upon thousands of hearts.  Peace to his ashes. - Rome Courier.
2 CONT
2 CONT SOURCE:  Find A Grave.com
2 CONT
2 CONT
