1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Abijah Ross was born in 1788, possibly in Giles County, Tennesee, and died in 09 Jan 1844 in Lincoln County, Tennessee. [32]
2 CONT
2 CONT As of Sept 2017 https://www.geni.com/people/James-Ross/6000000000784361622 seems to have been his father:
2 CONT
2 CONT From https://www.geni.com/people/Warren-McLaughlin-III/6000000059094567844
2 CONT
2 CONT >"The likely father of Abijah Cornelius Ross Sr. (1788-9 Jan 1844) is probably [https://www.geni.com/people/James-Ross/6000000000784361622 James P. Ross Jr.] (1742-Oct 1809). I have several DNA connections through him and his wife
2 CONC  https://www.geni.com/people/Mary-Ross/6000000000784604130?through=6000000000784361622  (1747-29 Mar 1788). She may have died giving birth to Abijah Cornelius Ross Sr. (1788-9 Jan 1844) or shortly thereafter."
2 CONT
2 CONT Married:
2 CONT # in 1813 in Nashville Tennesee to Elizabeth Jackson (1792-1847) Her parents are not known with certainly, but as of Sept 2017, "DNA suggests that her parents were  Samuel Jackson Jr. (23 Jan 1758-6 Jun 1834) and Hannah Gibson (9 
2 CONC Oct 1764-Dec 1841). It is my belief that Elizabeth Jackson (1792-5 May 1847) was not born in Nashville, Davidson, Tennessee, but likely immigrated there from North Carolina in about 1809 with a brother." (from Warren McLaughlin)
2 CONT
2 CONT children of Abijah Ross and Elizabeth Jackson:
2 CONT # William Ashburn Ross (1814) m. Mahala Mary Cunningham
2 CONT # Abijah C. Ross (1815) m. Nancy A. Elum
2 CONT # Rebecca Louisa Ross (1818) m. David Farrar
2 CONT # Silas Coleman Ross b 14 Nov 1819 d 03 May 1874, Lincoln, TN m. Matilda Ayers
2 CONT # Sarah D. Ross m. __ Jackson
2 CONT # Nancy Emeline Ross m. Peterson P. Johnson
2 CONT ==Events==
2 CONT # 1813 International Marriage Records, Nashville, Davidson County, Tennessee, married Elizabeth Jackson n.b. birthplace is sometimes listed as North Carolina.
2 CONT # 1820 Tennessee Census, Lincoln, Tennessee
2 CONT # 1830 United States Federal Census, Giles, Tennessee, 13 in household
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Comments 
2 CONT
2 CONT From https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/184098901/abijah-cornelius-ross retrieved March 2019
2 CONT
2 CONT >”His burial place in Lincoln County is unknown. The claim that his father (James P. Ross Jr.) and his mother (Mary Mitchell Ross) had just one child is very likely incorrect. They likely had as many as ten children! Abijah Ross h
2 CONC as a DNA connection to James P. Ross as well as Hugh Ross (1681-1740).”
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Abija and the wildcat==
2 CONT
2 CONT Abija Ross and family lived across the creek from the McLaurine family. He had two large dogs that had some experience in bear fighting. One night these two dogs chased a large wild cat up a large dead poplar tree in the horse lot
2 CONC ; Ross built a fire near the tree and waited for the morning light to show the object. It was a large wild cat 60 feet up in the fork of the tree. His old flint lock was out of fix but he loaded up, primed up the pan and told his 
2 CONC son when he drew sight on the cat to touch fire to the powder; so he drew bead and said, "Touch", which was done; the ball struck the cat and down he came only wounded and whipped both dogs. Ross and sons finally succeeded in kill
2 CONC ing the cat. This was a large wild cat and a big fight. [1]
2 CONT
2 CONT ==a mystery==
2 CONT
2 CONT Abijah Ross, Corporal appears on the Muster Roll, "Hastings Flank Company," 2nd July, 1812, Captain John McIntosh. Canadian Army.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Family Notes==
2 CONT
2 CONT The Ross family also claimed a relationship to Betsy Ross see the book "Fix Ridge, Latah County, Idaho" [2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Betsy Griscom; daughter of the Samuel Griscom who helped build Independence Hall; widow of Col. John Ross whose uncle, George Ross, was one of the signers of the Declaration of the Independence; maker of the first American nationa
2 CONC l flag accepted June 14, 1777. The following day she married Joseph Ashburn, a sea captain. [3]
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Saline Creek, Tennessee==
2 CONT
2 CONT Per various histories of Stewart Co, TN, Saline Creek was identified as "a very early settlement on creek which empties into Cumberland River, west of Bumpus Mills. The area was settled due to large tracts of land being granted fo
2 CONC r service rendeded to North Carolina."  We should note that all of Tenn. was originally part of NC ---ie in 1788 the "County of TN, in NC" was formed by an act of the NC General Assembly, and eight years later, in 1796, this Count
2 CONC y of TN became the State of TN. So land grants to Military Veterans by NC were transferred to the new state of TN.
2 CONT
2 CONT Saline Creek was settled primarily by the following families: Williams, Wallace and '''Ross'''
2 CONT ==Giles County, Tennessee==
2 CONT
2 CONT The first permanent settlement in the county wee made in about 1805, on Elk River, near the month of Richland Creek, and in the neighborhood, of the present towns of Elkton and Prospect, one of which lies above and the other below
2 CONC  the mouth of said creek, by William Crowson ... When these pioneers came they found the county a vast cane-brake and forest, the cane being from twenty to twenty-five feet high. The settlers melted Body force, and cleared away th
2 CONC e cane and built but bout. for each other, and the same kindness and courtesy Was extended to each new-comer for years thereafter 
2 CONT
2 CONT John Dickey, James Ross, Hamilton Campbell, Joseph Bozler, James Ashmore and Daniel Allen settled in the Campbellsville neighborhood between 1808 and 1809. [5]
2 CONT
2 CONT John Williams also operated a powder-mill near the State line, one mile southwest from Elk Mount Springs, and James Ross owned one in the western part of the county. The saltpeter used by these manufacturers was obtained from diff
2 CONC erent sources, principally from a cave near Campbell's Station in Maury County 
2 CONT
2 CONT Giles County was created in 1810 In pursuance of an act of the General Assembly passed November 14, 1809, and at the suggestion of Gen. Jackson was Denied in honor of Gen. William B. Giles, one of the governors of Virginia. Giles 
2 CONC County was formed out of Maury County
2 CONT
2 CONT AN ACT TO ESTABLISH A COUNTY SOUTH OF MAURY COUNTY, AND NORTH OF THE SOUTHERN BOUNDARY OF THE STATE 
2 CONT
2 CONT Section 2 provides that James Ross, Nathaniel Moody, Tyree Rhodes, Gabriel Bumpass and Thomas Whitson be appointed commissioners to select a place on Richland Creek, near the center of the county, for a county seat, at which site 
2 CONC the commissioners shall procure at least 100 acres of land, upon which they shall cause a town to be laid off, with necessary streets at least eighty feet wide, reserving at least two acres for a public square, on which shall be e
2 CONC rected a court house and stocks, also reserving a public lot sufficient to contain a jail, in a convenient part of town, which town shall be known by the name of Pulaski
2 CONT
2 CONT Seventeenth District-J. M. Gordon and R. F. Jackson have horse-power cotton-gins
2 CONT
2 CONT Sheriffs since 1810- John A. Jackson ...
2 CONT
2 CONT Quite a number of the Giles County pioneers served in the Revolutionary war, and for their services as soldiers of the line received grants from the State of North Carolina for the lands in this county, upon which they afterward s
2 CONC ettled. But of them there is no record accessible, and their names have long since passed from the memory of the citizens of the present, if memory of them they ever had 
2 CONT
2 CONT SEC. SIX--CAMPBELLSVILLE AND BIG CREEK
2 CONT
2 CONT John Dickey, Esquire, father of James R. Dickey, Esquire, moved from Logan Co., KY., and first stopped in Maury and thence to this County in 1808. He cut the cane near the Big Springs at Campbellsville and sowed turnips that Fall,
2 CONC  and made a crop of corn in 1809. James ROSS, the grandfather of Jas. R. Dickey, came the same Fall and settled in the place old https://www.geni.com/people/Andrew-D-Yokley/6000000002785341484 lived on until his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ross was one of the Commissioners appointed by the Legislature to locate and lay off the town of Pulaski. They traveled the Bumpass trail. The only road at that time coming south from Columbia was the Bumpass trail. They came up L
2 CONC ittle Bigby, crossed Elk ridge at what is now called the Yokley gap, and came down the eastern or Yokley branch of Big Creek. [6]
2 CONT
