1 CONT
1 CONT An interesting question is why Moses was buried at Wessyngton Plantation. According to John Baker "The plantation was founded in the late 1790s by Joseph Washington (1770-1848), of Southampton County, VA, who was also a cousin to 
1 CONC President Washington. Washington brought slaves with him to TN from VA. After Joseph's death the estate passed to his son George Augustine Washington (1815-1898). In 1860 the plantation contained 15,000 acres of land, held 274 sla
1 CONC ves and was the largest tobacco plantation in America."
1 CONT
1 CONT From http://www.washington.co.uk/history.htm
1 CONT
1 CONT "In 1183, almost 800 years ago in the village of Wessyngton, England, the Washington family took its name. In 1789 General George Washington a descendant of that family and leader of the Revolution, became the first President of a
1 CONC  new nation - the United States of America. 
1 CONT
1 CONT The capital of the new nation was named Washington in his honour. The Wessyngton, which had various spellings until it evolved into Washington, comes from the Anglo Saxon - 'Hwaes' a Saxon Chief, 'Inga' meaning family of, and 'Tun
1 CONC ' an estate - the estate of Hwae's (Wassa's) family."
1 CONT
1 CONT Also see http://www.washington.co.uk/history.htm for the The Washington Family history.
1 CONT
1 CONT From http://www.sandhollow.net/davidmorgan/genealogy/notes.html#NI4013
1 CONT WINTERS Moses (There is a huge stone for the Moses Winters family. 
1 CONT Following is a transcription of that momument:) 
1 CONT _______________________________________________________________________
1 CONT MOSES WINTERS 
1 CONT and his wife 
1 CONT ELIZABETH 
1 CONT
1 CONT Early Inn Keepers 
1 CONT Who came with the first settlers of 1779 
1 CONT Granted Tavern License 1797 
1 CONT In 1784 Moses was granted 640 A. Land 
1 CONT Where Wessyington was built in 1819 
1 CONT He and his wife are buried there. 
1 CONT Joined Red River Baptist Church August 27, 1791 
1 CONT Rev. Soldier - PVT in Col. Wm. Christian's CO. 1776-7 
1 CONT
1 CONT Their Children: 
1 CONT
1 CONT Caleb 1760 - 1843 - Married: 1) Sarah Harris 2) Mary 
1 CONT Duncan 
1 CONT Elizabeth - b 1763 (married) Nicks 
1 CONT Sarah - b 1765 (married) Nicks 
1 CONT Phoebe - b 1767 (married) McNeilly 
1 CONT Mary - b 1769 (married) Smothers 
1 CONT Nancy - b 1772 (married) Cocks 
1 CONT Catherine - b 1774 (married) Cocks 
1 CONT Amy - b 1776 
1 CONT Elizabeth - b 1780 (married) Benj. Wood 
1 CONT Aaron - b 1782? 
1 CONT Moses - b 1784? 
1 CONT
1 CONT Erected by their descendants 1970
1 CONT _______________________________________________________________________
1 CONT It is believed that Moses Winters came from SC to the Holston Settlement in eastern Tennessee, and then joined with James Robertson to travel to on to the Robertson TN area. 
1 CONT
1 CONT One researcher mentions that Moses "came from SC to the Holston Settlement in East TN where he joined with James Robertson to travel overland to Middle TN, and arrived there Dec 15, 1779. His wife, Elizabeth (Head) Winters and the
1 CONC ir seven daughters followed in the famous John Donelson's Flotilla down the Tennessee River, and arrived in April of 1780. This Moses Winters died in Robertson County in 1798." (http://www.roanetn.com/winters.htm)
1 CONT
1 CONT John Donelson’s Flatboat Trip 
1 CONT
1 CONT John Donelson was chosen to lead a flotilla of flatboats from the Watauga area to Middle Tennessee carrying the women, children, and old people.   He directed the building of over thirty boats for around 250 people.  The boats wou
1 CONC ld carry the old people as well as their clothing, tools, furniture, and garden seed.  The distance was at least 1,000 miles.  The flotilla left on December 22, 1779.  The first night they encountered the same freezing weather tha
1 CONC t the overland men were experiencing.  The river froze and they had to wait for two months for the river to thaw before they could continue their journey.  It was a dangerous journey.  Thirty people lost their lives along the way.
1 CONC    The men who came overland were becoming very alarmed because their familles were so late in arriving.  On the morning of April 24, 1780 the first flatboat was sighted by the lookout.  For a week they stopped their work and cele
1 CONC brated being together at last.  (http://volweb.utk.edu/Schools/sumnercs/nberry/flatb.htm)
1 CONT
1 CONT "James Robertson’s Overland Trip to the French Lick 
1 CONT LDW: (James Robertson is considered the founder of Nashville. TN)
1 CONT
1 CONT "At Watauga, it was decided that James Robertson would lead the middle aged men and young boys overland to the French Lick in the Cumberland Country.  The trip was 300 miles through the woods to the Bluffs.   In November, 1779 bet
1 CONC ween 200 and 300 men and boys began the journey.  They first followed the Wilderness Road through  Cumberland Gap into Kentucky and then followed trails to stations along the rivers.  They took a flock of sheep, a herd of cattle, 
1 CONC  and pack horses along.  They arrived at the Cumberland River in the midst of snow and freezing weather.  They arrived on Christmas Eve, 1779 on the northern bank of the Cumberland River.  The settlement was to be on the South sid
1 CONC e of the river.  It was easy to get the sheep, cattle, and horses across the river.  It had been so tremendously cold that the river frozen solid and they could all walk across.  They built some cabins and blockhouses connected by
1 CONC  stockades to protect themselves from the Indians."
1 CONT
1 CONT From The Settlement of Nashville, published by The Historical Commission of Metropolitan Nashville, and Davidson County, 1974
1 CONT
1 CONT Margaret Nichol writes on September 13, 2002:
1 CONT
1 CONT "Major Moses Winters, who settled Oliver Springs, Tennessee was probably the son of Moses Winters, Sr. who is said to have migrated to upper East Tennessee prior to the Revolutionary War. Moses Winters, Sr. married my ancestor, El
1 CONC izabeth Head Crain after 1762 when her younger son, Lewis Crain, was born. I believe Moses Winters, Jr. and Caleb Winters were sons by a previous marriage. Elizabeth Crain's children, by her previous marriage, were: John Crain, bo
1 CONC rn in 1759 (proven by RW pension application); and Lewis Crain, 1762. These dates correspond with birth dates given for the older Winters children giving rise to the theory that they were probably by Moses Winter's first wife.
1 CONT The lead in writeup on Major Moses Winters concerning his settling Oliver Springs, Tennessee gives the information that he was not to be confused with the Moses Winters who migrated from South Carolina and then goes on to give the
1 CONC  information that Major Moses Winters settled first at Carter's Station. This is exactly where Moses Winters,Sr., Elizabeth Head Crain and her sons were when Moses Winters, Sr. and John Crain volunteered for Christian's Campaign (
1 CONC also proven by John Crain's RW petition). There is a possibility that Moses Winters, Sr. could have had another son named Lewis."
1 CONT
1 CONT Corrie Moen wrote on September 20, 2005:
1 CONT
1 CONT "Elizabeth Head Craine Winters is the mother of Caleb Winters
1 CONT there is an article that she brought her daughters by water while Moses brought his Step son, and his son Caleb with Robertson. Moses Jr from Elizabeth was born in 1784, when Elizabeth was nearly 50 years old, he married Elizabeth
1 CONC  Pool.  Major Moses Winters is from a cousin line, he was born in 1760 the same time Caleb Winters was born
1 CONT
1 CONT "I descend From Caleb Winters, and his Daughter Sarah Sally Winters head"
