0 @N6000000000310311317@ NOTE
1 CONT Bedford Co VA Court Record, 1797. 
1 CONT Real estate conveyances, Bedford Co VA, 1783, 1800, 1803, 1804, 1808, 1820. 
1 CONT He is recorded as the father at several of his children’s marriages, but not Elizabeth’s. If she is his daughter, she was probably the oldest child. There were other Dosses in Bedford Co, but by the time of Elizabeth’s marriage, h
1 CONC e and a Joel (who may been a brother and was the husband of Hannah Ayres) Doss appear to be the only ones left.
1 CONT Azariah appears in various court records, Bedford Co VA 1784-1786, Bk 8, p 11, 64, 77, 107, 145, 189; 1795-1799, Bk 11, p 144; 1808-1811, Bk 15, p 35. Madonna (Doss) McCarthy, who sent me most of my Doss data, calls him “a fightin
1 CONC g fool.” Charged with feloniously burning John Hurt’s tobacco house, remanded for trial, 9 Mar 1809, Bk 15, p 48.
1 CONT In addition to Joel who married Azariah's sister-in-law, probable brothers were Zaccheus (deeds 1778, 1779-wife Mary in the second), Stephen (deeds, 1782, 1783), James (survey, 1780), and Edward (deed, 1794-wife Mary). One of them
1 CONC  may have been the father of the others.
1 CONT
1 CONT Revolutionary War Pension, heritagequest.com: "My father Izariah Doss a native of Virginia, served as a soldier enlisted in the Army of Gen. Washington, was promoted to a captaincy of a Whig company, to oppose the Tories in North 
1 CONC Carolina. Was subsequently engaged in South Carolina, against the British, at the Cowpens, Camden, Eutaw Springs and elsewhere, and at the capture of Cornwallis at Yorktown in Virginia. On his arrival at Elizabethtown, Kentucky, h
1 CONC e made proposals to Cad. Churchwell and Mr. Percival, to draw his pension, but never received anything to the knowledge of my father in family. My father died in Monroe County near Tomkinsville, Kentucky, in January 1825, and my m
1 CONC other in Russellville, Ky, in 1831. Neither of them ever applied for or received a pension.
1 CONT "So far as my knowledge extends, my brother George Washington Doss and myself are the only surviving representatives of my father’s family.
1 CONT Lydia Ann Hynes.
1 CONT "P.S. This letter is intended to be sent to Mount Vernon, another of the same date and purport to be placed in the Post Office, Blairsville, Posey County, Indiana to either of which places ... will be very acceptable."
1 CONT Sent to Washington, D.C. from Posey Co IN, dated December 16, 1849. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Few marriage records have survived from Bedford Co VA dating back to the 1780s and 1770s. The record of Azariah Doss' marriage to Lydia Ayres is not one of them. The marriage year "ca 1778" is based upon the assumption that Elizab
1 CONC eth Doss was the eldest daughter of Azariah and Lydia (Ayres) Doss, and further that Azariah and Lydia were married approx. a year previous to her birth (born in 1779).
0 @N6000000001694275544@ NOTE
1 CONT Some researchers believe that the father of William was Zachariah Doss, born Bef. 1720, in Pittsylvania Co, VA, and died Bet. 1810 - 1812 in NC.  His mother is believed to be Elizabeth Atkinson.  It is estimated that they were mar
1 CONC ried about 1740
1 CONT
1 CONT ***     ***     ***     ***     ***     ***     ***
1 CONT
1 CONT Census images were viewed on the Heritage Quest web site.
1 CONT
1 CONT ***     ***     ***     ***     ***     ***     ***
1 CONT
1 CONT 1810 Census, Roll 39, Page 149, Buncombe Co, NC
1 CONT
1 CONT William Doss is listed in the age group of 22 thru 44 and a female between the ages of 16 thru 25 are living in Buncombe Co, NC.
1 CONT
1 CONT ***     ***     ***     ***     ***     ***     ***
1 CONT
1 CONT 1820 Census, Roll 28, Page 110, Wayne County, Monticello, KY
1 CONT
1 CONT William Doss and his wife are evidently living in separate households.
1 CONT
1 CONT In William's home there are listed:
1 CONT
1 CONT One free white males of 16 and under 26, including heads of households,
1 CONT
1 CONT Three free white males of 16 and under 45, including heads of families,
1 CONT
1 CONT One free white females of 26 and under 45, including heads of families
1 CONT
1 CONT There was one person listed under the "Numbers of persons engaged in Manufactures".
1 CONT
1 CONT ***     ***     ***     ***     ***     ***     ***
1 CONT
1 CONT According to William Doss' Revolutionary War Pension record, he was born in May of 1734.  If that is true and the above statement is true, his parents were not married at the time he was born.
1 CONT
1 CONT ***     ***     ***     ***     ***     ***     ***
1 CONT
1 CONT 1830 Census, Series M19, Roll 176, Fentress County.
1 CONT
1 CONT The beginning page number for 1830 is page 1A and ending on page 16B.  The names of the residents are on the "even" page numbers. 
1 CONT
1 CONT William Doss is not in the Wayne County, KY census index.  He could possibly still living in Fentress Co, TN.  However, all the pages have been checked and his name was not listed or it was overlooked by this researcher, BLH.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Wayne County, KY roll number is 42, and begins on page number 216 A&B through page 262 A&B.  The summary is on page 264.
1 CONT
1 CONT ***     ***     ***     ***     ***     ***     ***
1 CONT
1 CONT William Doss was living in Fentress County Tennessee in 1833.  He is included in the tax list there.
1 CONT
1 CONT Need to confirm the War Departments list of Revolutionary War soldiers that William Doss, who died in KY, applied for a pension under the Act of 1832.
1 CONT
1 CONT Revolutionary War - Pension Record for William Doss:
1 CONT
1 CONT William Doss of Fentress County, Tennessee
1 CONT
1 CONT Certificate No.  22323
1 CONT Widow's Pension No.  W9404
1 CONT Bounty Land Warrant No. 80029-160-55
1 CONT
1 CONT William Doss, aged 69 in May 1832, in Fentress Co., Tennessee, on 23 April 1833 stated he had no record of his age, and entered service in Buckingham Co., Va., where he was born and raised, and lived until the end of the war.  He 
1 CONC was drafted as a private in the militia under Capt. Mosely.  Later he was under Capt. John Chambers and Col. Holcum, was at the Battle of Guilford, and was discharged in the spring of 1781.  He was called out again under Capt. Hil
1 CONC ler, and was at the Siege of York.  Part of the time he was under Col. Skipper [or Skipwith].  He was discharged after the surrender of Cornwallis.  After the war he lived in the following places: a few years in Franklin Co., Geor
1 CONC gia; a short time Buckingham Co., Va., a few years in Wilkes Co., North Carolina; a few years in Buncombe Co. [N. C.]; a few years in Wayne Co., Kentucky; then to Overton, Tennessee, to the part that is now Fentress County, where 
1 CONC he has lived for about 12 years.  Thomas Butler, aged 70, of Anderson Co., Tenn, declared on 3 Sept. 1833 in Morgan Co., Tenn, that he was with William Doss after the Siege of York.
1 CONT
1 CONT William Doss, on 14 May 1838 in Wayne Co., Ky., declared he has lost his pension certificate.  He resided in Fentress Co., Tenn., until eighteen months ago when he moved to Wayne Co., Ky.  He asks for a transfer to the Lexington, 
1 CONC Ky. Agency.
1 CONT
1 CONT Nancy Doss, deposed on 3 October 1853 in Fentress Co., Tenn., that she was the widow of William Doss who died in said county on 15 September 1853, and that she and her husband were married in Haywood Co., N. C. by Squire McHenry a
1 CONC bout 40 years ago.  She and her husband both lived in Buncombe Co., then moved from N. C. to near Jacksborough, Tenn., and from there to Fentress Co.  Their oldest child is now about 38 years old.  Most of her children live in Fen
1 CONC tress County.
1 CONT
1 CONT On 10 April 1855 Nancy Doss gave her age as 65 years old, and said she was married to William Doss about 25 or 26 years.  [An unexplained contradiction; we found their marriage record; they married in Haywood Co. 30 Oct. 1809.  Th
1 CONC is couple stopped living together; perhaps that explains this discrepancy.]
1 CONT
1 CONT William Doss, aged 24 declared on 10 August 1857 in Clinton Co., Ky. that his father William Doss died on 15 September 1853.  His oldest sister, Fanny Doss, if living would be about 15 or 16 years older than he.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ennuiss [sic] Doss, aged 33, and Sally (X) Doss, aged 27, deposed on 14 October 1857 in Fentress Co., Tenn. that they were both children of William and Nancy Doss, and that their father died on 15 September 1853.
1 CONT
1 CONT P. H. Smith of Albany, Clinton Co., Ky., deposed on 21 October 1857 that a year or two ago W. J. Dabney deposited $1.00 in letter to the Clerk of Haywood Co., N. C., asking for recorded evidence of the marriage of William Doss and
1 CONC  Nancy Brown.  Smith is deputy postmaster.  William Doss of Fentress Co., Tenn., private in the company of Capt. Mosely in the Virginia Line for nine months, was placed on the East Tennessee Pension Roll at $30 per annum under the
1 CONC  Act of 1832.  Certificate no. 22323 was issued on 25 October 1833, and later transferred to the Kentucky Pension Roll.
1 CONT
1 CONT ***     ***     ***     ***     ***     ***     ***
1 CONT
1 CONT 1840 Census, Wayne County, KY, Series M704, Roll 126, Page 148 A & B
1 CONT
1 CONT Wm. Doss is the only person living in his home.  He is listed under the column of being age 70 but under 80.  In another column on page 148 B, his name is listed as being a Pensioner of the Revolutionary War.  His age is listed th
1 CONC ere as age 76.  Other information on this page states that he is employed in Agriculture.
1 CONT
1 CONT The name listed above his on the census page is John Bell, ? F.  The name listed below his is Washington Young.
1 CONT
1 CONT Where are Nancy and the children?    Are they in Kentucky or Tennessee.
1 CONT
1 CONT This needs further investigation for 1840.  All the census pages for 1840 were checked in Fentress County, TN on the Heritage Quest web site.  The roll number for Fentress Co, is 523, beginning on pages 1 A&B through pages 24 A&B.
1 CONC   (Franklin Co, TN begins on pages 25 A&B.)
1 CONT
1 CONT ***     ***     ***     ***     ***     ***     ***
1 CONT
1 CONT 1850 September 27, Census, Roll 877, Page 416C, District # 8Fentress Co, TN
1 CONT
1 CONT William Doss (Wm. Daws), born in VA, age 83 is living with his daughter, James and Catherine/Kitty Crockett's family.  James, a farmer is age 31. Kitty is age 28.  They have two sons, Joseph age 10 and Francis age 6.
1 CONT
1 CONT He and Nancy have been living in separate homes since at least 1820 according to the census records.
1 CONT
1 CONT ***     ***     ***     ***     ***     ***     ***
0 @N6000000004851298284@ NOTE James4 PATY (Edward3, Edward2, Theophilus1) was born circa 1705 at Prince George County, Virginia. He married Sarah Clanton circa 1741. He died on 10 December 1758 at Halifax County, Virginia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Reference: 320. He The name Paty occurs in early English records in various forms such as, Pete, Pettey, Petty, Pattie, Patye, and Paty. This later form (Paty) was changed by the addition of the letter 't' for some of the families
1 CONC . The most common form other than Patty is Petty.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Patys came to England from France as soldiers in the army of William the Conqueror, when he invaded England in 1066 and defeated King Harold , at the Battle of Senlac. *A few generations after the Norman invasion the name was 
1 CONC found scattered over England, Scotland and the north of Ireland, as well as Scotland. *A coat of arms was granted to the family by King Charles I of England . (Burkes History of Heraldry).
1 CONT
1 CONT Sir William Patty was Physician General of the English Army in Ireland; Sir Charnall Patty was High Sherif f of Oxfordshire, England, and both were Knighted in 1661. Henry Patty was the Earl of Shelburn.
1 CONT
1 CONT *Amoung the earliest records of the family after they came to England are those of William Pete and Robert Pete in 1397. One John Paty Tetsworth in Oxfordshire in the 16th century married Alice Sparhawke. He died in 1530 leaving t
1 CONC wo sons John and James. Of these, John married Mary, daughter of William Charnall. James married Cicely Crispe and was the father of William,Peter, and Elizabeth.
1 CONT
1 CONT *John Paty, eldest son of John and Mary (Charnall) married Elizabeth Snape, by whom he was the father of John, Leonard, Maxmillian, Christopher, George and several daughters. Of these John first married Anne Breise or Brice by who
1 CONC m he was the father of John, Thomas, Elizabeth, Anne, Alice, Dorothy, Mary, Maude, and Frances. By his second wife, the widow Anne Johnson Webley, he had further issue of Edmond, Maxmillian, Leonard and Rebecca. *Leonard, second s
1 CONC on of John and Elizabeth Snape Paty, married a daughter of Henry Crispe and was the father by her of John, Snape, Leonard, Christopher, who made his home in the County,  of Kent. *Christopher, second son of John and Mary Charnall 
1 CONC Paty, married a Miss Lambourn and was the father of Charnall Patty who became the High Sheriff of Oxfordshire. The son, Charnall, married Ellen, daughter of Edmond Wilson, and had issue by her of Christopher, Susan, Annis, Elizabe
1 CONC th, Mary, George, Francis, of whom the first was the father by his wife Judith (daughter of Nicholas Boat) of Christopher and Ellen. *Robert Paty son of John and Mary Charnall Paty married Penelope, daughter of Richard Tavener, an
1 CONC d had issue by her of Francis Harcourt, and Mary. *Richard Paty of Warwickshire, about the middle of the sixteenth century, was the father by his wife, Matilda Colman, of Elizabeth, Anne, Thomas, Nicholas, William, Richard, John, 
1 CONC and Robert. Of these, the last married Clememcia Manners of Oxfordshire and had issue by her of Nicholas, Elizabeth, William, and John of whom the first married Jana or Jane, the daughter of Rich ard Blount in the early seventeeth
1 CONC  century. *A Thomas Paty was living in London in the early seventeeth century an d married Joan Hanson in 1602. The names of his children are not in evidence. *Anthony Paty was the father of Sir. William and Anthony. In 1776, Sir 
1 CONC William married Elizabeth, daughter of Sir Hardress Waller of Limerick County,  . Their Children were John, Charles, Henry, and Anne.
1 CONT
1 CONT *Families of Pattys and Patys are scattered over the British Isles and while no history that deals with a direct line of descent has been kept, it is established to a certainty that all branches of the family in England , and Scot
1 CONC land have common ancestors from those Patys who came from France with William the Conqueror in 1066 A.D. *Source References: *Bardsley, English and Welsh Surnames, 1901 Burke. Encyclopedia of Heraldry, 1844 Burke. General Armory, 
1 CONC 1884 The Oxford Historical Society, Vol. 19., 1881 Collins Peerage of Ireland, Vol. 2., 1812 Harleian Society, Oxfordshire, Visitations, 1871 Warwickshire Visitations, 1877
1 CONT
1 CONT *The name for this history will be spelled Paty, Patty, and Pattey as that is the way the majority of the records used for source material were spelled.
1 CONT
1 CONT The History will start with James Paty, who settled in what was to become Halifax County, Virginia, in the early 1700's. *As more research is done and this History is up-dated, I hope to find the proof needed to tie up the remaini
1 CONC ng loose ends. I would appreciate any information from anyone searching for our elusive emigrant. *James and his family were not Quakers, even if they are listed in Quaker records. One of their sons, James married a women of this 
1 CONC faith and later took up the Quaker faith. Three sons and two unnamed daughters are listed in James's will. His wife, Sarah's last name is thought to have been Clanton. I have found no proof of this other than it is listed in some 
1 CONC Quaker Research.
1 CONT
1 CONT *There is proof of the Clanton clan marrying into the Petty spelling of the name. *A few source references that may trun out to belong to our clan: *Goochland County,  Virginia. Deed Book 3 pg 3, To James Peaty, 90 acres on Mudd y
1 CONC  Creek provided he pays six months work. 20 Feb 1737. *Eastland, Thomas, plat for 50 acres in Craven County,  1772/04/18 0009 003 0014 00526 01 *Patey, James, account audited (file No. 5724) of claims growing out o f the American 
1 CONC Revolution 1776 C. or later 0015 003 0115 00139 00
1 CONT
1 CONT ** Divison of Virginia Counties * CHARLES CITY (1634) : : PRINCE GEORGE (1703) : : BRUNSWICK (1732) : : LUNENBURG (1746) : : ---------------------------------------------------------------- : : : : : : : : : : : : HALIFAX BEDFORD-
1 CONC ------ CHARLOTTE MECKLENBURG (1752) (1754) : (1765) (1765) : : : : : PITTSYLVANIA : : (1767) : : : : : : CAMPBELL : HENRY (1782) : (1777) : : FRANKLIN : (1786) PATRICK (1791) *Lunenburg County, Virginia was formed from Charles Cit
1 CONC y Virginia, Jam es was in the Pittsylvania County, Virginia Old Survey Book 1, 1746-1 782, page 1. Survey dated Feb 12, 1746 for James Paty, 340 acres on both sides o f Nixes Creek. *Lunenberg County, Virginia Deed Book 2, pg 437.
1 CONC  Jan 8, 1752, Rec Ap r 7, 1752. John Denny of Lunenberg & Sarah, his wife, to James Pattey of Lunenbe rg, 10 B, about 50 acres bounded by a ridge, a branch, Stanton Rive r just above the upper end of Mush Island, a place known by 
1 CONC the nam e of Bears house. John Denney, Sarah Denney. Wit. Thomas Dillard Jr. , George Wilcock, James Doss. *The above proves James Paty, was in Lunenberg County, Virginia as ear ly as 1746. Also James Doss, the father of Delilah D
1 CONC oss, who married Jesse Paty, son of James and Sarah Paty. James died eight years after this, in 1759. Since Jesse Paty his first born son was born California 1744, I would think he was born around 1721.
1 CONT
1 CONT *Will of James Pattey, 10 Dec 1758, proved 08 May 1759, Halifax County, Virginia Circuit Court, Book O, P 58. *Inventory, Estate of James Patty, 21 May 1759, Halifax County, Virgin Iowa Circuit Court, Book O pp 67-68. *In 1768 a J
1 CONC ames Pattey of Berkley County, South Carolina, granted a Power of Attorney to Jesse Pattey of Pittsylvania County, Virginia to sell land left to him by his father, James Patey, deceased, Pittsylvania County, Vir ginia, Deed Book 1
1 CONC , page 286. *Edward Paty, who I feel is the father of James Patey, died in Berkeley District South Carolina. Is this why James Patey Jr. moved there from Virginia? *James settled in Newberry County,  South Carolina, (taken from Be
1 CONC rkele y District) and raised his family there. James lived here till his death. *I need to find out more about Edward, and his other children, as James Jr, may have gone there because he still had family there, Uncles ect. *.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sarah CLANTON. Reference: 321. She Sarah Clanton is listed as wife of James Paty, in the Quakers records , but I have found no other information on her. She is listed only as Sarah in the will of James Paty. *. She was born circa 
1 CONC 1721 at Virginia.
0 @N6000000004851298180@ NOTE 27. ii. James PATY was born between 1747 and 1750 at Lunenburg County, Virginia. He married Margaret Mote, daughter of David Mote and Dorcas Nicholds, on 11 March 1769 at Newberry County, SC. He died on 28 July 1796 at Newberry Co
1 CONC unty, SC.
1 CONT He Power of Attorney, 07 Dec 1768, James Pattey of Berkeley County, Sout h Carolina to Jesse Pattey of Pittsylvania County, Virginia power of attorney to sell land left to him by will of James Patty, Deceased. Pittsylvani a County
1 CONC , Virginia, Deed Book 1, p 286. Virginia, Deed Book 1, pg 286. This sh ows that James moved to South Carolina as early as 1768, and before his brothe r Jesse Paty did. *James settled in Newberry County, South Carolina, where he li
1 CONC ved an d raised his family. He is buried there. James was a Quaker, as were most of his c hildren. He was thought to be a birthright Quaker, This was not the case, he m arried a Quaker and choose to become a Quaker after the marri
1 CONC age. *It was not the practice of Quakers to own slaves or fight in the wars , but James did own slaves. There are records in the Newberry Court house , where he bought three slaves. *Note: Page 759 Hinshaw Vol. V. West branch MM p
1 CONC age 49 Ouarkers Recs t o Ohio 1806 from SC MV Pioneers of Ohio page 122; page 138 from Bush River Sout h Carolina 21 Mar 1807 to Mill Creek 1827; section 17; June 1934 Issue: Sunday J ournal Ireton records. *Patey, James, land Gra
1 CONC nt for 200 acres in Berkley County,  1773/06/23 0002 005 0031 00112 00 *Patey, James, Memorial for 200 acres on Bush Creek, Berkely County, 1773/11/30 0030 002 0013 00132 03 *Patey, James, to Isaac Henry, Bill of sale for a slave 
1 CONC named Harry, w arranted sound. 1840/05/02 0002 001 005w 00168 00 *South Carolina 1790 Census: Newberry District *James Paty *2 males over 16 3 males under 16 4 females *Land grants/deeds Newberry S.C. James Paty in 1767, 100 acres
1 CONC , and in 1773 200 Acres. *.
0 @N6000000004851298224@ NOTE 8. iii. Sarah 'Sally' PATY was born circa 1748 at Campbell County, Virginia.
1 CONT She I believe this daughter of James, to be the mother of Nancy Patty wh o married Benjamin Cannifax, and Elijah Paty who married Ava Parrott. From th e dates, and location she has to be the granddaughter of James Paty and Sarah C
1 CONC lan ton. Information from Bedford County, Virginia, Court Order book # 8, pag e 28, June Court 1784, 'Church wardens bind out Nancy and Elijah Patey bastard c hildren of Sally Paty.' *On the original marriage record from Campbell 
1 CONC County, Virginia, she i s listed as dr of Sally, not Larry, although I can see where someone could misread this, I am sure it is Sally or Sara. I have a copy of the original r ecord. *.
0 @N6000000004851298164@ NOTE 29. iv. Charles PATY was born circa 1749 at Campbell County, Virginia. He married Mary Jane Jay, daughter of William Jay and Mary Elizabeth Vestal, on 11 March 1773 at Bush River MM, Newberry County, SC.
1 CONT He Patey, Charles account audited (file No. 5760) of claims growing ou t of American Revolution. 1776 C. or later 0051 003 0115 00522 00. *Patty, Charles, Plat for 100 acres in Berkley County,  1770/02/14 0009 003 0019 00091 03 *P
1 CONC atey, Charles, land Grant for 100 acres in Berkley County,  1773/05/18 0002 005 0029 00512 00 *1790 South Carolina Census Newberry District *Charles Paty 2 males over 16 1 male under 16 2 females *.
