0 @N6000000001985881644@ NOTE Charles and Elizabeth settled near Green Springs about 1704.  They were Quakers but owned slaves (Woods History of Albemarle County, VA).  In 1725 entered 254 acres in Louisa County; in 1724, 111 acres adjoining.  In 1735 patented
1 CONC  400 acres in Albemarle County (then Goochland County) at the junction of Mechurn and Moorman Rivers (from which entry the river received its name).  In 1736 entered 453 acres in Louisa County.  In 1738, along with Charles Mills, 
1 CONC entered 4000 acres lying between Moorman and Mechurn Rivers (Va.Mag. IV, p. 228).  In 1744 Charles was living in Louisa County not far from Green Springs.  It is said by his descendants that Charles was 15 years old and Elixabeth 
1 CONC 13 when they married.  Elizabeth was 15 when their eledest son, Thomas, was born in 1705.
